/* GENERATED by scripts/compose-full-mocks.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
window.ACE_FULL_MOCKS = {"version":"2026-10-01","rules":"five choices; key never the longest; even key letters per section; no item from The Climb or the Prometric mocks; every item blind re-solved","mocks":[{"id":"FULL_1","name":"Full-Length Test 1","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-7-d4b5kr","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A cell enters mitosis carrying a mutation that keeps cohesin from being cleaved at the centromere. Everything else works: chromosomes condense, the envelope breaks down, and the spindle attaches normally from both poles. What is the most likely result?","opts":["The cell skips metaphase entirely and moves straight into cytokinesis","Anaphase runs on schedule, but each daughter cell receives only half the usual DNA","The chromosomes never condense, so mitosis cannot get started at all","Replication restarts, because the cell reads its chromosomes as unduplicated","The chromatids stay joined and fail to segregate, so anaphase cannot complete"],"correct":4,"why":"Trace the one job the missing step was doing. Cohesin is the glue holding sister chromatids together, and cleaving it is the trigger that lets the spindle pull them apart. Attachment is fine here, so the cell reaches metaphase and tries to go on, but the chromatids are still glued and cannot be separated. Any question that removes cohesin cleavage is asking about segregation failure, not about entry into mitosis.","traps":["Metaphase depends on spindle attachment, which the question tells you is working, so the cell has no reason to skip it.","If the chromatids never come apart there is nothing to distribute, so anaphase cannot run normally in the first place.","Condensation is driven by a separate set of proteins and happens well before cohesin cleavage would ever matter.","Replication is licensed once per cycle during S phase, and cohesin has no role in telling the cell whether its DNA has been copied.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1ex8fre","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A herbicide blocks electron transfer from photosystem II to plastoquinone. What happens inside a treated chloroplast?","opts":["Every light reaction stops at once, including all ATP synthesis, since the chain has one entry point","NADPH production continues, but oxygen release stops","Oxygen release continues at a normal rate, while NADPH production stops entirely","The Calvin cycle keeps running normally on carbon dioxide stored in the stroma","Oxygen release and NADPH production both stop, while cyclic flow can still make some ATP"],"correct":4,"why":"Treat the chain as a line of people passing buckets. Block one handoff and the person upstream cannot let go, so photosystem II stops pulling replacement electrons out of water and the oxygen stops with it. Downstream of the block, photosystem I can still loop electrons in a circle, which is why a trickle of ATP survives.","traps":["Photosystem I can still cycle electrons back through the cytochrome complex and pump protons, so some ATP survives the block.","NADPH needs electrons arriving from photosystem II during linear flow, so it stops as well.","Electrons have nowhere to go, so photosystem II cannot pull replacements out of water and oxygen release stops too.","A normal leaf stores no pool of carbon dioxide, and without ATP and NADPH the Calvin cycle stalls within seconds.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-15zgkb4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"While the electron transport chain is running hard, how do the two sides of the inner mitochondrial membrane compare?","opts":["The intermembrane space has a higher proton concentration and a lower pH than the matrix","The matrix has a higher proton concentration and therefore a lower pH than the intermembrane space","The matrix becomes positively charged relative to the intermembrane space","Both compartments hold the same proton concentration, and only the charge differs","The intermembrane space has a higher pH, because protons are consumed there"],"correct":0,"why":"Follow the pumps and the rest falls out. Complexes I, III, and IV move protons from the matrix out into the intermembrane space, so that space ends up crowded with protons, acidic, and positive relative to the matrix. ATP synthase then lets those protons fall back into the matrix, and that downhill flow is what powers ATP synthesis.","traps":[null,"This has the gradient backwards, since protons are pumped out of the matrix, leaving the matrix proton-poor.","Pumping positive charge out of the matrix leaves the matrix relatively negative, not positive.","If the concentrations matched there would be no gradient at all and nothing to drive ATP synthase.","More protons means a lower pH, not higher, and protons are being added to that space rather than used up."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Echinoderms and chordates are placed together in which major group of bilaterian animals?","opts":["Ecdysozoa","Lophotrochozoa","Radiata, the radially symmetric animals","Protostomes","Deuterostomes"],"correct":4,"why":"The question is testing the top-level split of bilaterian animals, and the split is made on how the embryo builds its gut. In deuterostomes the first opening of the gastrula becomes the anus and the mouth forms second, and cleavage is radial and indeterminate. Echinoderms and chordates both develop this way, which is why a sea star is a closer relative of yours than a snail is. The name is the memory hook, since deuteros means second, and it is the mouth that comes second.","traps":["Ecdysozoans are the molting protostomes, the arthropods and roundworms.","Lophotrochozoans are a protostome subgroup, the animals with a lophophore or a trochophore larva, such as molluscs and annelids.","Adult sea stars are radially symmetric, but their larvae are bilateral and chordates are never radial, so radial symmetry is not what unites these two.","Protostomes are the other branch, the molluscs, annelids and arthropods, where the blastopore becomes the mouth.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p094","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the best example of a hinge joint?","opts":["Vertebrae","Elbow, knee","Hip","The joint between the atlas and the axis at the top of the neck","Thumb base"],"correct":1,"why":"Joint questions ask you to match the shape of the joint surfaces to the motion they allow. A hinge joint is a cylinder sitting in a trough, so it opens and closes in a single plane like a door, and the elbow and the knee are the classic pair. When a joint name shows up on the exam, picture the motion first: bending in one plane points to a hinge, motion in every direction points to ball and socket, and spinning around a long axis points to a pivot.","traps":["The small facet joints between vertebrae glide on each other and the discs between the bodies are cartilaginous, so neither one works like a hinge.",null,"The hip is a ball and socket joint, so it swings in every direction rather than being limited to one plane of motion.","The atlas and axis form a pivot joint, which rotates the head side to side rather than bending it open and closed.","The base of the thumb is a saddle joint, which is what lets the thumb swing across the palm and oppose the fingers."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-104j1lu","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"In an illuminated chloroplast, which way do protons move through ATP synthase, and where does the ATP appear?","opts":["From the lumen into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the lumen","From the stroma into the lumen, with the ATP appearing in the lumen","From the stroma into the lumen, with the ATP appearing in the stroma","From the cytosol into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the stroma","From the lumen into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the stroma"],"correct":4,"why":"Pumps push uphill and turbines run downhill. The electron carriers load protons into the lumen, so the lumen becomes the crowded acidic side, and ATP synthase lets that crowd stream back out into the stroma. The ATP shows up exactly where the Calvin cycle needs it, which makes a handy sanity check.","traps":["The direction is right, but the catalytic head of the enzyme faces the stroma, so the ATP is released there.","That is the direction the electron carriers pump protons, which is the uphill direction rather than the direction through the enzyme.","Protons move down their gradient through the enzyme, which means out of the lumen, not into it.","The cytosol sits outside the chloroplast entirely and takes no part in this gradient.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-yh7561","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell takes DNA damage during G1. The p53 protein accumulates and the cell halts before it can enter S phase. What does p53 switch on to produce that halt?","opts":["Separase, which cleaves the cohesin holding the sister chromatids together","Cyclin D, which pushes the cell through the restriction point sooner","The anaphase-promoting complex, which strips cyclin B out of the cell","Telomerase, which lengthens the chromosome ends before replication starts","p21, an inhibitor of cyclin-CDK complexes, which holds the cell in G1"],"correct":4,"why":"This tests whether you can follow a stop signal down to the machine it actually stops. Cyclin-CDK complexes are the accelerator, so any checkpoint arrest has to work by blocking one of them. Damaged DNA stabilizes p53, p53 turns on p21, and p21 binds and inhibits the cyclin-CDK that would otherwise carry the cell into S phase. Repair then happens, or the cell is steered into apoptosis, which is why p53 is lost in so many cancers.","traps":["Cohesin cleavage happens at the start of anaphase, far downstream of a G1 decision about whether to replicate at all.","Cyclin D drives the cell forward past the restriction point, which is the opposite of what a damage response needs.","The anaphase-promoting complex acts during mitosis to bring it to an end, so it cannot be what blocks entry into S phase.","Lengthening telomeres does nothing about damage in the middle of a chromosome, and telomerase is not part of the damage checkpoint.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1wnmho1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"DNA polymerase cannot begin a strand from bare template. Something else must lay down a short starter that polymerase can then extend. What is that starter made of, and what happens to it later?","opts":["A short protein tag made by helicase, later stripped off by the polymerase","A short DNA piece made by ligase, which is then left permanently in place","A short RNA piece made by primase, later removed and replaced with DNA","A short RNA piece made by RNA polymerase II, later spliced out as an intron","A short DNA piece made by telomerase, later extended into the whole strand"],"correct":2,"why":"The point here is that the starter is RNA and not DNA, and that is not an accident. Primase can begin a chain from nothing, polymerase cannot, so the RNA primer hands polymerase the free 3' hydroxyl it needs. The RNA is later excised, the gap filled with DNA, and the nick sealed. If a question mentions something that is put down during replication and then removed and replaced, it is the primer.","traps":["Helicase unwinds DNA and makes no starter of any kind, and polymerase extends nucleic acid rather than protein.","Ligase joins pieces of DNA that already exist and cannot build a new one, and a DNA starter would not need removing in the first place.",null,"RNA polymerase II transcribes messenger RNA, and splicing removes introns from a transcript, which is a separate process from replication.","Telomerase does extend DNA, but only at chromosome ends and only from its own RNA template, so it is not what starts an ordinary fragment."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1yx1bd4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Two membranes are identical except that one is built largely from phospholipids with unsaturated fatty acid tails. At a cool temperature, that membrane will be:","opts":["Less fluid, because the double bonds lock neighboring tails tightly together","More fluid, because the kinks keep the tails from packing tightly","Completely impermeable to dissolved gases","Identical in fluidity, since only cholesterol matters","Solid, because unsaturated tails crystallize first"],"correct":1,"why":"Picture the tails. A double bond puts a permanent kink in the chain, and kinked tails cannot stack neatly, so they keep sliding past one another as the temperature drops. Saturated tails are straight, pack tight, and solidify first. Kink means loose, straight means tight.","traps":["A double bond kinks the chain and prevents tight packing rather than locking chains together.",null,"Small nonpolar gases cross any bilayer readily, whatever the tails look like.","Tail saturation is a major determinant of fluidity right alongside cholesterol.","Saturated tails solidify first, unsaturated tails stay mobile longer as temperature falls."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-11y9km5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Mature human red blood cells have no mitochondria at all. What does that mean for how they make ATP?","opts":["They import ATP from neighboring cells through gap junctions","They make no ATP and live on stored reserves until they are cleared","They run the Krebs cycle in the cytosol instead","They rely on glycolysis and convert pyruvate to lactate to keep NAD+ coming","They use the oxygen they carry to run an electron transport chain in the cytosol"],"correct":3,"why":"No mitochondria means no pyruvate oxidation, no Krebs cycle, and no electron transport chain, so glycolysis is the whole energy budget. To keep glycolysis turning, the cell has to regenerate NAD+, and lactate fermentation is how it does that, which is why red blood cells put out lactate even in a well-oxygenated person. It is a useful case to keep on hand, because it cleanly separates carrying oxygen from using it.","traps":["Red blood cells do not maintain gap junctions with other cells, and ATP is not traded around that way.","These cells work for roughly 120 days, which no fixed ATP store would cover.","Krebs enzymes live in the mitochondrial matrix, so a cell without mitochondria has nowhere to run them.",null,"The oxygen a red blood cell carries is cargo bound to hemoglobin, and the chain needs membrane complexes this cell does not have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-10j9q5x","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Why is oxygen able to interfere with carbon fixation at all?","opts":["Oxygen binds a separate regulatory site that switches RuBisCO off","Both gases compete for the same active site on RuBisCO","Oxygen blocks carbon dioxide from crossing the chloroplast envelope","Oxygen reacts with NADPH before the Calvin cycle can put it to use","Oxygen oxidizes the magnesium ion in the enzyme and inactivates it"],"correct":1,"why":"One site, two gases, and whichever is more plentiful wins the spot. The enzyme's full name, ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase oxygenase, spells out both jobs in a single word. That competition is the whole reason C4 and CAM plants evolved ways to keep carbon dioxide concentrated around the enzyme.","traps":["There is no off switch involved, because oxygen is taken in as a substrate at the working site.",null,"Gases diffuse across the envelope freely, and neither one blocks the other's entry.","NADPH is not what oxygen intercepts, since the competition happens at the enzyme that grabs the gas.","The magnesium in the active site stays put, and photorespiration is not an enzyme poisoning event."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g020","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many Barr bodies would you expect in a somatic cell from a person with Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)?","opts":["0","3","2","1","The number varies from cell to cell, because X inactivation is random"],"correct":3,"why":"A Barr body is an inactivated X chromosome, and a cell keeps exactly one X active no matter how many it carries. So the count is always the number of X chromosomes minus one, which for XXY is one. Run that subtraction on any karyotype you are given and this whole family of questions becomes a single step.","traps":["Zero Barr bodies fits a typical 46,XY male or a 45,X female, where there is only one X to begin with and nothing to inactivate.","Three Barr bodies would require four X chromosomes, which is not what this karyotype describes.","Two Barr bodies would require three X chromosomes, as in a 47,XXX karyotype.",null,"Which X gets silenced is random, and that is why females are mosaics, but how many get silenced is fixed at all but one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g010","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The Hardy-Weinberg model applies only when a specific set of conditions is met. Which of the following is NOT one of those conditions?","opts":["Infinite population","Equal sex ratio","No mutation","Random mating","No migration into or out of the population"],"correct":1,"why":"The model asks what allele frequencies do when nothing disturbs them, so its conditions are simply a list of the five things that do disturb them: mutation, non-random mating, gene flow, drift in a small population, and selection. Test each choice by asking whether it would change allele frequencies from one generation to the next. The ratio of males to females is not on that list, because the model tracks alleles rather than counts of each sex.","traps":["An effectively infinite population is required so that chance sampling between generations, which is genetic drift, does not shift frequencies.",null,"Mutation creates new alleles and shifts frequencies, so ruling it out really is one of the required conditions.","Non-random mating such as inbreeding or assortative mating changes genotype proportions, so random mating is genuinely required.","No gene flow is one of the required conditions, since migrants arriving with different allele frequencies would shift the population's own."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p056","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Filtration of plasma from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule is driven mainly by which of the following?","opts":["Active transport","Aquaporin gradient","Hydrostatic pressure","Colloid osmotic pressure of the plasma proteins","Diffusion only"],"correct":2,"why":"Filtration here is a plumbing question rather than a transport question. Blood enters the glomerulus through a wide afferent arteriole and leaves through a narrower efferent one, which holds capillary hydrostatic pressure high and pushes water and small solutes through the filtration barrier in bulk. Net filtration is that pushing pressure minus the opposing plasma colloid osmotic pressure and capsular pressure, so the choice naming hydrostatic pressure is the driving force.","traps":["Filtration at the glomerulus costs the kidney no ATP directly; active transport shows up later, during reabsorption along the tubule.","Aquaporins move water in the collecting duct under antidiuretic hormone control, well downstream of the filtration step.",null,"Plasma proteins stay behind in the capillary and their osmotic pull works against filtration, holding fluid in rather than pushing it out.","Diffusion moves individual solutes down their own gradients, while filtration pushes water and small solutes together through the filtration slits."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-17676nh","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"Bacteria are grown for many generations in medium whose only nitrogen source is the heavy isotope 15N, so all of their DNA is heavy. They are then moved into medium containing only light 14N and allowed to divide exactly twice. What fraction of the DNA molecules now contain one heavy strand and one light strand?","opts":["One quarter of them","Three quarters of them","All of them","One half of them","None of them, because every molecule is now fully light"],"correct":3,"why":"This is semiconservative replication counted out rather than recited: you start with one molecule holding two heavy strands, and those two strands survive every round intact. After one round there are two molecules and both are hybrid. After two rounds there are four molecules, and the two heavy strands still sit in two of them, so half are hybrid. The shortcut worth keeping is that the number of hybrid molecules stops changing after the first round while the total keeps doubling, so the hybrid fraction halves each generation.","traps":["One quarter is the fraction of individual strands that are heavy after two rounds, which is not the same as the fraction of molecules carrying one.","Three quarters is the fraction of individual strands that are light after two rounds, again a strand count rather than a molecule count.","All hybrid is the picture after one round, and the second round dilutes it, because each hybrid molecule gives one hybrid daughter and one fully light daughter.",null,"This assumes the old strands are discarded, but replication keeps and reuses every parental strand, so the two original heavy strands are still somewhere."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1kxv9cz","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A drug completely blocks triose phosphate isomerase, so dihydroxyacetone phosphate can no longer be turned into glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate. What happens to the net ATP yield of glycolysis per glucose?","opts":["It falls from 2 to 1","It falls from 4 to 2","It rises above 2","It stays at 2","It falls from 2 to 0"],"correct":4,"why":"Work the two halves of the pathway separately. The cell still pays 2 ATP to split the whole six-carbon sugar, but now only one of the two three-carbon pieces continues, so the payoff phase returns 2 ATP and 1 NADH instead of 4 and 2. Two spent against two returned leaves a net of zero, which is exactly why that isomerase step exists.","traps":["Halving feels right, but the investment is not halved: the cell still spends 2 ATP up front on the whole sugar.","The 4 is the gross figure, and the question asks for net, which starts at 2.","Blocking a step in the middle of the pathway does not undo what was already spent at the start.","Only half the carbon now reaches the payoff phase, so the output cannot be unchanged.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-867ilc","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Two electron carriers in the chain are mobile rather than locked inside a complex. Which pair?","opts":["Ubiquinone and ATP synthase","NADH and FADH2","Oxygen and water","Ubiquinone and cytochrome c","Cytochrome c and cytochrome a3"],"correct":3,"why":"Four complexes sit fixed in the membrane, so something has to ferry electrons across the gaps between them. Ubiquinone, also called coenzyme Q, is lipid-soluble and drifts within the membrane from complexes I and II over to complex III, while cytochrome c is a small protein that skims the intermembrane space face from complex III to complex IV. Remember the pattern as one carrier inside the membrane and one riding on its surface.","traps":["ATP synthase spends the gradient the chain builds and carries no electrons at all.","These two deliver electrons to the chain from the matrix, but neither is part of the chain itself.","Oxygen is the endpoint and water is the product, so neither shuttles electrons between complexes.",null,"Cytochrome a3 is built into complex IV rather than being free to move."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_xp008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"In a community with three trophic levels (producers, primary consumers, secondary consumers), the introduction of a top predator (a tertiary consumer) typically results in:","opts":["Trophic cascade; secondary-consumer numbers decline, primary consumers rebound, producer biomass decreases","Trophic cascade; secondary-consumer numbers decline, primary consumers also decline, producer biomass INCREASES","No effect; the new predator only eats producers","Loss of primary consumers across the food web","Linear decline in all populations"],"correct":0,"why":"Adding a tertiary predator triggers a top-down trophic cascade: the new predator suppresses secondary consumers, which releases primary consumers from predation pressure (primary consumers rebound), and the increased primary-consumer grazing then reduces producer biomass. Option A describes this classic four-level cascade correctly.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-a4a4xb","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A brightly lit leaf that is actively fixing carbon is suddenly moved into the dark, with carbon dioxide still available. In the first seconds, what happens to its RuBP and 3-PGA levels?","opts":["Both fall immediately","RuBP falls and 3-PGA rises","RuBP rises and 3-PGA falls","Both rise together","Neither changes until the leaf runs out of carbon dioxide"],"correct":1,"why":"Ask which step stalls first. Darkness cuts off ATP and NADPH, so the reduction step stops, while RuBisCO keeps attaching carbon dioxide to whatever RuBP is left. The molecule just before a blocked step piles up and the one just after it drains, and that rule reads any blocked pathway on any section of the exam.","traps":["Fixation keeps running for a few seconds in the dark, so 3-PGA is produced rather than lost.",null,"That is the pattern for cutting off carbon dioxide instead of light, and it is the reversal students land on most often.","3-PGA does rise, but RuBP cannot rise while it is still being consumed and no longer being rebuilt.","The Calvin cycle depends on ATP and NADPH from the light reactions, so darkness shows up within seconds."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p044","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A person at rest who breathes rapidly and deeply for several minutes will most likely develop which acid base disturbance?","opts":["No pH change","Metabolic alkalosis from a loss of hydrogen ions","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"correct":3,"why":"Two questions sort every acid base problem: which way did pH move, and what moved it. Blowing off carbon dioxide pulls the carbonic acid equilibrium backward, consumes hydrogen ions, and raises blood pH, and because ventilation caused it the label is respiratory. The choice reading respiratory alkalosis is the fit, and holding your breath gives you the mirror image, respiratory acidosis.","traps":["Carbon dioxide is the body's fastest pH lever, so changing how much of it you exhale shifts blood pH within a minute or two.","The pH does rise here, but ventilation is what moved it, which makes it respiratory; metabolic alkalosis comes from losing stomach acid or gaining bicarbonate.","That is what hypoventilation does: trapping carbon dioxide raises the hydrogen ion concentration and drops pH, the opposite of blowing it off.",null,"Metabolic acidosis comes from adding acid or losing bicarbonate, as with lactic acid or ketones, and it drives fast breathing rather than being caused by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p032","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The P wave on an electrocardiogram represents which electrical event?","opts":["AV delay","Ventricular depolarization","Ventricular repolarization","Atrial depolarization","Atrial repolarization"],"correct":3,"why":"An electrocardiogram records the wave of electrical change as it sweeps through the heart, in the order the signal travels. The sinoatrial node fires first and the atria depolarize, and that small bump is the P wave, then the large QRS complex is the ventricles depolarizing, and the T wave is the ventricles repolarizing. Read the tracing in the same order the signal moves and each wave names itself.","traps":["The pause at the atrioventricular node shows up as the flat PR segment rather than as the P wave itself.","That is the QRS complex, larger because the ventricles carry much more muscle mass.","Ventricular repolarization produces the T wave at the end of the cycle.",null,"Atrial repolarization does happen, but it is buried under the much larger QRS complex and never appears as its own wave."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-5547ba43c3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A person drinks alcohol, which inhibits the release of antidiuretic hormone. What is the expected result?","opts":["A higher filtration rate at the glomerulus with no change in urine concentration","Less glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule","Fewer aquaporins in the collecting duct, so more dilute urine is produced","More aquaporins in the collecting duct, so less urine is produced","More Na+ reabsorption in the distal tubule, so blood pressure rises"],"correct":2,"why":"ADH is the water saver. It puts aquaporin water channels into the collecting duct so water can leave the filtrate and return to the blood. Take ADH away and the water stays in the tubule, so urine is plentiful and dilute.","traps":["The main ADH effect is on water permeability of the collecting duct, which changes urine concentration.","ADH does not control glucose handling.",null,"That is what ADH does when it IS present. Blocking ADH gives the opposite.","That is the aldosterone effect, not the ADH effect."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1qjf2kq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Which enzyme sets the pace of the Krebs cycle, and what turns it down?","opts":["Aconitase, slowed by acetyl-CoA","Succinate dehydrogenase, slowed by a shortage of FAD","Isocitrate dehydrogenase, slowed by NADH and ATP","Citrate synthase, slowed by oxygen","Malate dehydrogenase, slowed by oxaloacetate"],"correct":2,"why":"The cycle is regulated the same way glycolysis is, by the products that signal a full tank. Isocitrate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme, and rising NADH and ATP slow it while ADP speeds it back up. If a question tells you a cell is energy-rich and asks what the Krebs cycle does, expect the pathway to throttle at this step.","traps":["Aconitase only rearranges citrate into isocitrate, and acetyl-CoA feeds the cycle rather than slowing it.","Succinate dehydrogenase needs FAD as its electron acceptor, so FAD drives that step rather than blocking it.",null,"Citrate synthase is regulated, but oxygen is not its signal, since the cycle never uses oxygen directly.","Oxaloacetate is the product of that step and citrate synthase consumes it immediately, so it does not build up as a brake."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p025","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Midway through the menstrual cycle, luteinizing hormone rises sharply in what is called the LH surge. What does this surge trigger?","opts":["Menstruation","Recruitment and early growth of the ovarian follicles","Implantation","Fertilization","Ovulation"],"correct":4,"why":"This question is testing which hormone spike does which job in the cycle. Estrogen from the growing follicle climbs until it flips the pituitary into positive feedback, which fires a sharp surge of luteinizing hormone, and that surge ruptures the mature follicle and releases the egg. The same surge then converts the leftover follicle into the corpus luteum. Hold the surge as the trigger that pops the follicle and the rest of the cycle lines up around it.","traps":["Bleeding follows the fall of progesterone at the very end of the cycle, which is the opposite hormonal situation from a mid-cycle spike.","Early follicle growth is driven by follicle-stimulating hormone at the start of the cycle, before this mid-cycle spike happens.","Implantation happens about a week after the egg is released and depends on progesterone from the corpus luteum, not on the spike itself.","Fertilization depends on sperm meeting the egg in the oviduct; the surge only makes an egg available to be met.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-11djtx4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A hormone activates phospholipase C in a smooth muscle cell. Which pair of second messengers is produced, and what does the water-soluble one do?","opts":["IP3 and DAG, and IP3 stays in the membrane to activate protein kinase C","IP3 and DAG, and IP3 releases calcium from the smooth endoplasmic reticulum","DAG and calcium, and DAG travels to the nucleus to bind DNA","cAMP and calcium, and cAMP opens calcium channels in the nucleus","cAMP and cGMP, and cAMP activates protein kinase A in the cytoplasm of the cell"],"correct":1,"why":"Phospholipase C splits the membrane lipid PIP2 into two pieces with different fates. IP3 is water soluble, so it leaves the membrane, travels to the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, and opens calcium channels there, while DAG is lipid soluble and stays in the membrane where it helps switch on protein kinase C. Sort the two by solubility and their destinations follow.","traps":["The roles are swapped, DAG is the piece that remains in the membrane for protein kinase C.",null,"DAG is not a transcription factor, and it does not leave the membrane.","Phospholipase C does not make cAMP, and the calcium store being tapped is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum.","Those are the products of the two cyclases, not of phospholipase C."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-8da5ad8639","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels. What is the direct effect on a neuron exposed to it?","opts":["Neurotransmitter release increases at the terminal","The neuron can no longer fire action potentials","The neuron fires action potentials continuously","The resting potential immediately rises to 0 mV","Repolarization fails, so the membrane stays positive"],"correct":1,"why":"No Na+ entry means no upstroke. The cell still has a normal resting potential, but it can never reach the rising phase, so no signal travels. That is why this toxin causes numbness and paralysis.","traps":["With no action potential reaching the terminal, release drops.",null,"Continuous firing would need Na+ channels that stay open. The toxin BLOCKS them.","Resting potential depends mainly on the K+ leak and the pump, which the toxin leaves alone.","Repolarization is the K+ channel job, and it never gets the chance because the spike never starts."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p035","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The coronary arteries deliver blood to which tissue?","opts":["Heart muscle itself","Liver","Only the inner lining of the chambers, since the outer wall is fed by blood passing through","Brain","Lungs"],"correct":0,"why":"The heart is full of blood, yet the muscle cannot feed itself from it, because the wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chamber. So the first two branches off the aorta, just past the aortic valve, are the coronary arteries, and they loop back over the heart to supply the myocardium itself. Block one and that patch of muscle starves, which is what a heart attack is.","traps":[null,"The liver is fed by the hepatic artery and the hepatic portal vein, both well downstream in the abdomen.","Diffusion from the chambers reaches only a thin inner layer, which is why the coronary arteries supply the full thickness of the heart wall.","The brain is supplied by the carotid and vertebral arteries, which branch further along the aortic arch.","The lungs receive blood from the pulmonary arteries for gas exchange, and their own tissue is fed by the bronchial arteries."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-t3v9co","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell with DNA damage it cannot repair commits to the intrinsic pathway of apoptosis. Which molecule leaves the mitochondrion to set the cascade going, and where does it go once it is out?","opts":["Fas ligand, into the extracellular space, where it binds the neighboring cell","Calcium, into the nucleus, where it condenses the chromatin all by itself","ATP synthase, into the cytosol, where it goes on to activate death receptors","Cytochrome c, into the cytosol, where it helps assemble the apoptosome","Bcl-2, into the cytosol, where it directly cleaves the executioner caspases"],"correct":3,"why":"The elegant part of the intrinsic pathway is that it borrows a respiratory protein. Cytochrome c normally shuttles electrons in the intermembrane space, and when the outer membrane is permeabilized it spills into the cytosol, binds Apaf-1, and builds the apoptosome, which activates caspase-9. Finding cytochrome c in the cytosol is the classic sign that a cell has committed. Remember it by its address: inside the mitochondrion it means respiration, outside it means death.","traps":["Fas ligand is made by another cell entirely and belongs to the extrinsic pathway, not to anything released from a mitochondrion.","Calcium is stored mainly in the endoplasmic reticulum rather than released as the apoptotic trigger, and chromatin condensation happens downstream of the caspases.","ATP synthase is anchored in the inner mitochondrial membrane and stays there, and death receptors are activated by ligands arriving from outside the cell.",null,"Bcl-2 sits on the mitochondrial membrane holding this release shut, so it is the brake rather than the signal, and it is not a protease."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-2c5o89","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Insulin binds the outer portion of its receptor. What does the receptor do next?","opts":["Carries insulin with it into the nucleus","Adds phosphate groups to tyrosines on its own cytoplasmic tails","Cleaves PIP2 into IP3 and DAG","Opens as an ion channel and lets sodium in","Exchanges GDP for GTP on an attached G protein at the inner surface"],"correct":1,"why":"The insulin receptor is the classic receptor tyrosine kinase, and the word kinase is the whole mechanism. Once the hormone binds, the two halves of the receptor activate one another and hang phosphate groups on tyrosine side chains of their own cytoplasmic tails. Those phosphorylated tyrosines then act as docking sites for relay proteins that carry the message inward. Hormone binds, receptor phosphorylates itself, relay proteins dock.","traps":["Insulin is a peptide and never enters the cell, that route belongs to steroids.",null,"Cleaving PIP2 is the job of phospholipase C, further along a different pathway.","Ion channel receptors respond to neurotransmitters, not to insulin.","Loading GTP onto a G protein is what a G-protein-coupled receptor does."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1bz3wg9","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"In a resting neuron the inside of the membrane is negative relative to the outside, and sodium is far more concentrated outside than in. For sodium, the concentration gradient and the electrical gradient:","opts":["Both favor movement of sodium into the cell","Cancel exactly, so sodium already sits at equilibrium","Both favor movement of sodium out of the cell","Oppose each other, with the concentration gradient winning","Oppose each other, with the electrical gradient winning"],"correct":0,"why":"Two forces act on every ion, and they have to be read separately before they are combined. Sodium is more concentrated outside, so the concentration force pushes it in, and sodium is positive while the inside is negative, so the electrical force pulls it in as well. Both point the same way, which is why sodium floods in the instant a channel opens.","traps":[null,"Sodium sits far from equilibrium at rest, which is exactly what makes an action potential possible.","Sodium is high outside rather than inside, so neither force sends it outward.","This assumes the two forces conflict, and for sodium at rest they both point inward.","This treats the negative interior as repelling sodium, but a negative interior attracts a positive ion."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g015","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A single base pair in the coding region of a gene is changed, yet the resulting polypeptide has exactly the same amino acid sequence as before. This type of mutation is called:","opts":["Silent","Missense","Frameshift","Nonsense","Transversion"],"correct":0,"why":"This tests whether you can carry a DNA change through the genetic code and out to the protein. The code is redundant, so several different codons spell the same amino acid, and a base swap that lands on one of those synonyms leaves the protein untouched. Sort the names by what the protein ends up doing: same protein is silent, one amino acid swapped is missense, an early stop is nonsense, and a shifted reading frame is a frameshift.","traps":[null,"A missense mutation swaps one amino acid for another, so the protein sequence does change.","A frameshift comes from inserting or deleting bases in a number not divisible by three, which rewrites every codon downstream.","A nonsense mutation turns a codon into a stop signal and cuts the protein short, so the product is definitely altered.","Transversion describes the chemistry of the swap, a purine traded for a pyrimidine, and says nothing about whether the amino acid stayed the same."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The lens of the eye develops from which embryonic tissue?","opts":["Mesoderm","Neural crest","Endoderm","Ectoderm","Mesoderm and neural crest together"],"correct":3,"why":"This is the payoff of the classic induction experiment. The optic vesicle grows out from the forebrain and presses against the surface ectoderm of the head, and that contact instructs the surface ectoderm to thicken into a lens placode, fold inward, and pinch off as the lens. Hold the geometry in mind: the retina comes from the brain side, and the lens comes from the outer covering that the brain reached out and touched.","traps":["Mesoderm in the head forms the muscles that move the eye and the vessels that supply it, but not the clear lens itself.","Neural crest does build parts of the eye, including much of the cornea and the stroma of the iris, which makes this tempting, but the lens comes from the surface layer instead.","Endoderm never reaches the surface of the head, since it is committed to the lining of the gut tube and the organs that bud from it.",null,"Both of those tissues contribute to the eye, supplying muscles and corneal layers among other parts, but neither one gives rise to the lens."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The producers in a grassland food chain capture 10,000 kcal of energy. Using the ten percent rule for energy transfer between trophic levels, approximately how much of that energy reaches the tertiary consumers?","opts":["10 kcal","100 kcal","9000 kcal","1000 kcal","1 kcal"],"correct":0,"why":"This is the ten percent rule, and the only real trap is counting the steps. Producers pass about a tenth up to primary consumers, leaving 1,000 kcal, those pass a tenth to secondary consumers, leaving 100 kcal, and those pass a tenth to tertiary consumers. Count the arrows rather than the animals: three transfers means dividing by ten three times. The energy that does not move up leaves as heat from respiration and in parts that are never eaten.","traps":[null,"This is the secondary consumer level, which stops one transfer short of the tertiary consumers.","This flips the rule: about ten percent moves up and about ninety percent is lost, so each step divides by ten rather than shaving off a tenth.","This is the primary consumer level, one transfer down from the producers.","This divides by ten four times, counting one more transfer than this chain contains."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A group that contains an ancestral species together with every one of its descendants is described as:","opts":["Syngenetic","A sister group","Monophyletic","Paraphyletic","Polyphyletic"],"correct":2,"why":"Phylogeny questions come down to three shapes, and it helps to draw them. Cut one branch cleanly off the tree and everything on that piece, the ancestor plus every descendant, is a clade, which is the monophyletic choice. Cut the branch but leave a twig behind on the tree and you have a paraphyletic group. Pick two twigs from separate branches because they look alike and you have a polyphyletic one.","traps":["This is not a standard phylogenetic term for a complete ancestor plus descendants group.","A sister group is the lineage branching off next to the one you are looking at, a relative position on the tree rather than a rule about including all descendants.",null,"A paraphyletic group keeps the ancestor but drops some descendants, the classic case being reptiles with the birds left out.","A polyphyletic group gathers species that resemble one another but leaves out their common ancestor, as grouping bats with birds because both fly would do."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1qws3oz","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"What does a round of photorespiration cost the plant?","opts":["It converts glucose back into carbon dioxide to generate ATP","It makes one extra G3P at the cost of one NADPH","It consumes ATP and gives off carbon dioxide while making no sugar","It destroys RuBisCO, which the leaf then has to rebuild","It shuts down the light reactions until the oxygen level inside the leaf falls"],"correct":2,"why":"Name what actually comes out. RuBP plus oxygen gives one usable three carbon acid and one two carbon piece the cell has to salvage, and that salvage operation spends ATP and loses carbon as carbon dioxide. Photorespiration is the hole in the bucket: same machinery, wrong gas, nothing gained.","traps":["That description belongs to cellular respiration, a controlled energy yielding pathway, not to this.","Photorespiration pulls carbon out of the cycle rather than adding a usable three carbon sugar to it.",null,"The enzyme is not consumed and goes right back to work on the next molecule it meets.","The light reactions keep running, which is part of the problem, because they keep making more oxygen."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Crossing a true-breeding red-flowered snapdragon with a true-breeding white-flowered snapdragon produces offspring with pink flowers. This result demonstrates:","opts":["Codominance","Linkage","Epistasis between two different genes","Sex-linkage","Incomplete dominance"],"correct":4,"why":"Look at the heterozygote and ask whether you are seeing both colors or an average of them. One working pigment allele makes only about half the usual amount of red pigment, so the petal lands between the two parents as a uniform pink rather than showing red and white separately. The proof that the alleles themselves stay intact is the next generation, which comes back as one red to two pink to one white.","traps":["Codominance would show both colors at full strength at the same time, such as red and white streaks on one petal, rather than an even in-between shade.","Linkage describes two genes riding together on one chromosome, and only a single flower color gene is involved here.","Epistasis requires a second gene masking the first, but one pigment gene accounts for all three phenotypes on its own.","Sex-linkage would make the result depend on which parent contributed which allele, and it does not here.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-d8063fa866","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"An organism has a diploid number of 8. How many chromatids are present in one cell at metaphase I and in one cell at metaphase II?","opts":["16 at metaphase I and 4 at metaphase II","32 at metaphase I and 16 at metaphase II","16 at metaphase I and 8 at metaphase II","8 at metaphase I and 4 at metaphase II","16 at metaphase I and 16 at metaphase II"],"correct":2,"why":"Start with 8 chromosomes. After replication each has two chromatids, so metaphase I has 8 chromosomes and 16 chromatids. Meiosis I halves the chromosome number, so a metaphase II cell has 4 chromosomes, each still with two chromatids, for 8 chromatids.","traps":["At metaphase II the sister chromatids are still joined, so 4 chromosomes means 8 chromatids.","DNA is replicated once, so 8 chromosomes become 16 chromatids, not 32.",null,"You counted chromosomes. Each chromosome is replicated here, so double it to count chromatids.","Meiosis I already split the cell, so a metaphase II cell holds half as much."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1d483j","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Glucose taken into an intestinal epithelial cell across the apical membrane then leaves across the basolateral membrane into the blood, moving down its concentration gradient through a GLUT carrier. That exit step is:","opts":["Facilitated diffusion","Secondary active transport","Simple diffusion","Primary active transport","Exocytosis"],"correct":0,"why":"The same sugar uses two different mechanisms on two faces of one cell, and that contrast is the point. Entry at the apical side is uphill and borrows the sodium gradient, while exit at the basolateral side is downhill through a carrier with no energy spent, which is facilitated diffusion. At every step ask two things: which way does the gradient run, and is a protein involved.","traps":[null,"That describes the apical entry step, the exit has no gradient to borrow from.","Glucose is polar and bulky, so it needs a carrier rather than the bare bilayer.","No ATP is hydrolyzed at this step, the pump spending ATP in this cell is working on sodium.","Glucose leaves through a carrier protein, not packaged inside a vesicle."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"An autosomal recessive disorder affects 1 in 10,000 individuals in a population that is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. What is the frequency of the recessive allele (q)?","opts":["0.0001","0.001","0.01","0.1","0.99"],"correct":2,"why":"This is a Hardy-Weinberg question wearing a sentence as a disguise. For a recessive condition, the word affected describes homozygous recessive individuals, so the number you are handed is q squared, not q. One in 10,000 is 0.0001, and the square root of that is 0.01. Any time a stem gives you the frequency of affected recessive individuals, take the square root first, then build p and the carrier frequency off that value.","traps":["This is q squared, the frequency of affected individuals, and the square root step still has to happen.","Moving the decimal three places is not the same operation as taking a square root, so this lands between the two real values.",null,"This is the square root of 1/100, so it would fit a disorder affecting 1 in 100 people rather than 1 in 10,000.","This is p, the frequency of the dominant allele, which is what you get after subtracting q from 1."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-0de0fec7bd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A neuron receives a much stronger stimulus than before. How does it signal the greater intensity?","opts":["It fires action potentials more frequently","Each action potential becomes taller","The action potential travels faster down the same axon","Each action potential lasts longer","The resting potential becomes more negative"],"correct":0,"why":"A light switch is either on or off, so the neuron cannot shout louder with a bigger spike. Instead it shouts more often. Stimulus strength is coded as firing frequency.","traps":[null,"Action potentials are all or none. Once threshold is crossed the spike is always the same height.","Speed depends on axon diameter and myelin, not on how hard you stimulate.","Duration is fixed by channel kinetics. It does not stretch with stimulus strength.","Resting potential does not encode intensity. A more negative rest would make the cell HARDER to fire."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A physiology buffer contains 0.10 M HA and 0.20 M A- in 1.0 L of solution (pKa = 4.74). The buffer capacity for added strong acid is limited by the moles of A- available before all conjugate base is converted back to HA. Approximately how many mol/L of strong acid (H+) can be added before the buffer is exhausted?","opts":["~0.30 mol/L","~0.05 mol/L","~0.10 mol/L","~1.0 mol/L","~0.20 mol/L"],"correct":4,"why":"Each mole of added H+ converts one mole of A- to HA. Buffering ends when [A-] reaches zero, so the maximum H+ that can be neutralized equals the initial moles of A-, ~0.20 mol/L. Beyond that, pH crashes.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_p5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"An isoelectronic series Na⁺, Mg²⁺, F⁻, and O²⁻ all share 10 electrons (the Ne configuration) but differ in nuclear charge Z. Which species has the LARGEST ionic radius?","opts":["Na⁺ (Z=11)","O²⁻ (Z=8)","All four have the same radius because they have the same electron count","Mg²⁺ (Z=12)","F⁻ (Z=9)"],"correct":1,"why":"In an isoelectronic series, the ion with the SMALLEST nuclear charge (Z) holds the 10 electrons most loosely, giving the largest radius. O²⁻ (Z=8) < F⁻ (Z=9) < Na⁺ (Z=11) < Mg²⁺ (Z=12), so O²⁻ is largest.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1lxw3py","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 2 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 127 °C and 1 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["6650 L","98.5 L","32.8 L","20.9 L","65.7 L"],"correct":4,"why":"T = 127 + 273 = 400 K. V = nRT / P = (2)(0.0821)(400) / 1 = 65.7 L.","traps":["You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"Given ΔHf° (kJ/mol): CO2(g) = −394, H2O(g) = −242, CH3OH(l) = −239, and O2(g) = 0, calculate the standard enthalpy of combustion for CH3OH(l) + 1.5 O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2 H2O(g) at 25 °C.","opts":["−400 kJ","+1117 kJ","+639 kJ","−639 kJ","−717 kJ"],"correct":3,"why":"ΔH_rxn = sum(ΔHf° products) − sum(ΔHf° reactants) = [−394 + 2(−242)] − [−239 + 1.5(0)] = −878 − (−239) = −639 kJ. Sign errors on reactants give −1117 or +639.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#5e30zy","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"N₂ + 3 H₂ → 2 NH₃\n\nIf 1.5 mol of N₂ reacts with 12 mol of H₂, how many moles of NH₃ can form?","opts":["3 mol","8 mol","13.5 mol","4 mol","1.5 mol"],"correct":0,"why":"N₂: 1.5 / 1 = 1.5. H₂: 12 / 3 = 4. The smaller one limits. Product = 1.5 × 2 = 3 mol.","traps":[null,"You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield.","You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You compared raw moles. Divide each by its coefficient first, then pick the smaller."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A pharmacist measures out 4.50 g of pure water (H₂O, MW = 18.0 g/mol). Avogadro's number is 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹. How many individual hydrogen atoms are present in this sample?","opts":["1.5 × 10²³","9.0 × 10²³","3.0 × 10²³","1.5 × 10²⁴","6.0 × 10²³"],"correct":2,"why":"Moles H₂O = 4.50/18.0 = 0.250 mol. Each H₂O contains 2 H atoms, so mol H = 0.500. Atoms H = 0.500 × 6.022×10²³ ≈ 3.0 × 10²³. The factor-of-2 stoichiometric step (H per H₂O) is the most common error point.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Silver chromate (Ag₂CrO₄) dissolves slightly in pure water according to Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2 Ag⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq). Given K_sp = 1.1 × 10⁻¹² at 25°C, calculate the molar solubility s (mol/L) of Ag₂CrO₄ in pure water.","opts":["2.2 × 10⁻¹²","1.0 × 10⁻⁶","1.1 × 10⁻¹²","6.5 × 10⁻⁵","3.3 × 10⁻⁴"],"correct":3,"why":"If s = solubility, then [Ag⁺] = 2s and [CrO₄²⁻] = s, so K_sp = (2s)²(s) = 4s³. Solving: s³ = K_sp/4 = 2.75×10⁻¹³, s = (2.75×10⁻¹³)^(1/3) ≈ 6.5×10⁻⁵ M. Forgetting the (2s)² stoichiometric factor gives the much smaller 1.0×10⁻⁶ trap.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ti-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 25.0 mL aliquot of 0.100 M acetic acid (CH₃COOH, K_a = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) is titrated with 0.100 M NaOH. At the half-equivalence point, exactly half the acid has been converted to its conjugate base, so [HA] = [A⁻]. What is the pH of the solution at this point?","opts":["2.87","4.74","7.00","8.72","9.26"],"correct":1,"why":"At half-equivalence, the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation pH = pK_a + log([A⁻]/[HA]) reduces to pH = pK_a since [A⁻] = [HA] makes log(1) = 0. pK_a = -log(1.8×10⁻⁵) = 4.74, so pH = 4.74.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rl-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"A reaction A -> products at 298 K is studied with several different starting concentrations [A]_0, and the measured half-life is found to be CONSTANT (independent of [A]_0) for all trials. Using the integrated rate-law dependence of t_(1/2) on [A]_0, what is the order of the reaction with respect to A?","opts":["Zero order","First order","Half order","Second order","Third order"],"correct":1,"why":"Only first-order reactions have a half-life t_(1/2) = ln(2)/k that is independent of [A]_0. Zero-order t_(1/2) is proportional to [A]_0; second-order t_(1/2) is inversely proportional to [A]_0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Heat released combusting 16 g CH₄? (ΔH = −891 kJ/mol)","opts":["891 kJ","1782 kJ","223 kJ","56 kJ","446 kJ"],"correct":0,"why":"mol CH₄ = 16/16 = 1. q = 1 × 891 = 891 kJ.","traps":[null,"That burns two moles, 32 g; this sample is 16 g.","That burns a quarter mole, which would be 4 g of methane; 16 g is a full mole.","That is the heat per gram, 891 divided by 16, stopping before it is multiplied by the 16 g burned.","That burns half a mole, 8 g; methane's molar mass is 16, so 16 g is one whole mole."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A 0.20 m aqueous KCl solution (i = 2 for full dissociation) has Kb = 0.52°C·kg/mol. Pure water boils at 100.00°C at 1 atm. Using ΔTb = i·Kb·m, what is the boiling point of this solution?","opts":["100.10°C","100.21°C","100.00°C","101.04°C","100.52°C"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔTb = (2)(0.52)(0.20) = 0.208°C ≈ 0.21°C. New Tb = 100.00 + 0.21 = 100.21°C. Ignoring i = 2 gives 100.10; using i = 3 gives 100.31; the 1 m result (100.52) is the wrong-molality distractor.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Chlorine trifluoride (ClF₃) is a powerful fluorinating agent with a T-shaped molecular geometry and F-Cl-F angles near 87°. The central Cl has 7 valence electrons, forms 3 Cl-F σ bonds, and has remaining electrons distributed as lone pairs in equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid. How many lone pairs are on Cl?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Cl contributes 7 e⁻; 3 are used in the three Cl-F σ bonds, leaving 4 e⁻ = 2 lone pairs. Steric number = 3 + 2 = 5 (sp³d, trigonal bipyramidal electron geometry). The two LPs go equatorial, producing the T-shaped molecular geometry.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#1x4qwu9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.347 s⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["5.76 s","2 s","2.88 s","0.501 s","0.24 s"],"correct":1,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.347 = 2 s. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.",null,"That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.","You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"When multiplying or dividing measured values, the result must carry the same number of significant figures as the input with the FEWEST sig figs. Calculate 1.50 × 4.0 (where 1.50 has 3 sf and 4.0 has 2 sf) and express the answer with the correct number of significant figures.","opts":["6","6.0","6.00","6.000","6.0000"],"correct":1,"why":"Numerical product: 1.50 × 4.0 = 6.00 (raw). Sig-fig limit: minimum of (3 sf, 2 sf) = 2 sf. Therefore the answer must be reported as 6.0 (2 sig figs). Writing '6' alone implies 1 sig fig; '6.00' implies 3.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Two sparingly soluble salts at 25°C are compared: AgCl (Ksp = 1.8 x 10^-10, dissolves as Ag+ + Cl-) and PbI2 (Ksp = 9.8 x 10^-9, dissolves as Pb^2+ + 2 I-). Note the different stoichiometries: s for AgCl satisfies Ksp = s^2, while s for PbI2 satisfies Ksp = 4s^3. Which salt is MORE soluble in pure water?","opts":["PbI2","AgCl","Both are completely insoluble","Cannot determine without comparing s values","Equal because Ksp values are similar"],"correct":0,"why":"AgCl: s = sqrt(1.8e-10) = 1.3 x 10^-5 M. PbI2: s = (9.8e-9/4)^(1/3) = 1.34 x 10^-3 M. PbI2 is ~100x more soluble, even though Ksp values look 'close' on a log scale. Ksp alone cannot rank solubility when stoichiometries differ.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#x56iha","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 1.5 m aqueous solution of KBr? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-8.37 °C","-2.79 °C","-5.58 °C","-1.54 °C","5.58 °C"],"correct":2,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (2)(1.86)(1.5) = 5.58 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 5.58 = -5.58 °C.","traps":["You counted one ion too many. Write the dissociation out and count the particles.","You forgot the van't Hoff factor. KBr splits into 2 ions, so i = 2.",null,"You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:nr3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A concentration cell uses Cu electrodes in [Cu²⁺] = 0.01 M (left) and 1.0 M (right). E_cell?","opts":["0.059 V","0.030 V","0.118 V","0","−0.059 V"],"correct":0,"why":"Concentration cell → E° = 0. The 1.0 M side becomes the cathode (Cu²⁺ wants to deposit), the 0.01 M side becomes the anode. E = (0.0592/n)·log([Cu²⁺]_cathode/[Cu²⁺]_anode) = (0.0592/2)·log(1.0/0.01) = 0.0296 × 2 = 0.0592 V ≈ 0.059 V.","traps":[null,"0.030 V uses log(10) instead of log(100); the ratio here is 100, so the log is 2.","0.118 V leaves out the division by n: with 2 electrons the 0.0592 is halved before it multiplies log Q.","Concentration cells DO produce voltage.","−0.059 V makes the dilute side the cathode; copper ions plate out on the concentrated side, so the cell voltage comes out positive."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-gibbs#148vz11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction, ΔH = -80 kJ and ΔS = 200 J/K. What is ΔG at 400 K?","opts":["-160 kJ","0 kJ","-80 kJ","-80.2 kJ","-80100 kJ"],"correct":0,"why":"TΔS = 400 × 200 J/K = 80 kJ. ΔG = -80 − (80) = -160 kJ, so the reaction is spontaneous at this temperature.","traps":[null,"Sign slip. ΔG = ΔH MINUS TΔS.","You dropped ΔH. Both terms matter.","You forgot to multiply ΔS by the temperature.","Unit trap. ΔS is in J/K but ΔH is in kJ. Divide TΔS by 1000 before subtracting."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:sr5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"You mix aqueous solutions and want to confirm sulfate (SO₄²⁻) is present in an unknown. Which cation should you add to make sulfate precipitate cleanly?","opts":["K⁺","NH₄⁺","Na⁺","Ba²⁺","Mg²⁺"],"correct":3,"why":"The classic sulfate confirmation: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s), a white precipitate. Ba²⁺ is one of the sulfate exceptions to the \"sulfates are soluble\" rule. Na⁺, NH₄⁺, and K⁺ all form fully soluble sulfates (Group 1 + ammonium), so they cannot precipitate sulfate.","traps":["K₂SO₄ is soluble - same Group 1 rule. To precipitate sulfate you need Ba²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Sr²⁺.","(NH₄)₂SO₄ is soluble - ammonium sulfates are always soluble. No precipitate.","Na₂SO₄ is soluble - Group 1 sulfates are always soluble. No precipitate.",null,"MgSO₄ is soluble, which is why Epsom salts dissolve in a bath; a 2+ charge by itself does not precipitate sulfate."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lr-e12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"For Mg(s) + 2 HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g), a strip of magnesium (24.0 g, MW = 24 → 1.00 mol Mg) is added to 36.5 g HCl (MW = 36.5 → 1.00 mol HCl). Compute mole/coefficient ratios to identify the limiting reactant.","opts":["Cannot determine","Mg is limiting","HCl is limiting","Both simultaneously","H₂ is limiting"],"correct":2,"why":"Ratios: Mg → 1.0/1 = 1.0; HCl → 1.0/2 = 0.5. Smaller ratio = HCl limits. With HCl limiting, only 0.5 mol Mg reacts (consuming 0.5 mol Mg), leaving 0.5 mol Mg unreacted in excess.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#1u8ztlk","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻³ M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["2","2.3","11","11.7","12"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻³ = 1.0 × 10⁻² M. pOH = 2, pH = 14 − 2 = 12.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gv-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"The Daniell cell Zn|Zn²⁺(1 M)‖Cu²⁺(1 M)|Cu has a standard cell potential E°cell = +1.10 V at 25°C, with n = 2 electrons transferred per mole of reaction. Using ΔG° = -n·F·E°cell with F = 96,485 C/mol, calculate ΔG° in kJ/mol.","opts":["−424 kJ/mol","−54 kJ/mol","+424 kJ/mol","−212 kJ/mol","+212 kJ/mol"],"correct":3,"why":"ΔG° = -n·F·E°cell = -(2)(96,485 C/mol)(1.10 V) = -212,267 J/mol ≈ -212 kJ/mol. The negative sign confirms spontaneity, consistent with E°cell > 0. Sign errors and forgetting the factor of n are the main error modes.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Argon (Z = 18) has the noble-gas configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶ with 18 electrons. Among the following common ions, identify the one that is BOTH isoelectronic with Ar AND carries a +2 charge.","opts":["Cl⁻ (Z = 17)","Sc³⁺ (Z = 21)","K⁺ (Z = 19)","Ca²⁺ (Z = 20)","S²⁻ (Z = 16)"],"correct":3,"why":"Isoelectronic with Ar means 18 electrons. Ca has Z = 20 and protons = 20; Ca²⁺ has lost 2 electrons → 18 e⁻, matching Ar. K⁺ and S²⁻ are also 18-electron species but carry +1 and -2 charges respectively, failing the +2 requirement.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf005","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A 5.00 L flask contains 0.250 mol N2 and 0.500 mol O2 at 27°C. Assuming ideal gas behavior, what is the partial pressure of O2 in atm?","opts":["3.69 atm","1.23 atm","2.46 atm","12.3 atm","4.92 atm"],"correct":2,"why":"P(O2) = n(O2)·R·T / V = (0.500)(0.08206)(300) / 5.00 = 2.46 atm. Or use mole fraction: x(O2)·P_total = (2/3)(3.69) ≈ 2.46.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":3,"q":"Xenon tetrafluoride (XeF₄) is a square planar molecule with F-Xe-F angles of 90°. The central xenon has four Xe-F σ bonds and two lone pairs that occupy axial positions to minimize lone-pair/lone-pair repulsion. What hybridization is assigned to the xenon?","opts":["sp³d³","dsp²","sp³d","sp³","sp³d²"],"correct":4,"why":"Steric number at Xe = 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs = 6, requiring sp³d² hybridization (octahedral electron geometry). The two lone pairs sit trans to each other (axial), leaving the four fluorines in a square plane with 90° bond angles.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = +40.0 kJ/mol and ΔS = +100. J/(mol·K), with both values approximately temperature-independent. Setting ΔG = ΔH − T·ΔS = 0 to find the crossover temperature above which the reaction becomes spontaneous, what is T_cross?","opts":["1000 K","200 K","400 K","500 K","300 K"],"correct":2,"why":"Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = 40.0 kJ / 0.100 kJ/K = 400 K. Above 400 K the reaction is spontaneous. Forgetting J->kJ conversion (using 100 directly) gives 0.4 K.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A 5.0% (w/w) NaCl solution has a measured density of 1.04 g/mL at 20 °C, and the molar mass of NaCl is 58.4 g/mol. Assuming the solution is at the same temperature and well mixed, what is the molarity of NaCl in this solution?","opts":["1.78 M","5.0 M","1.04 M","0.89 M","0.45 M"],"correct":3,"why":"Per 1.00 L: mass = 1040 g, mass NaCl = 0.050 x 1040 = 52.0 g, mol = 52.0/58.4 = 0.890 mol. Therefore M = 0.89 mol/L. Skipping the density factor gives ~0.86, while doubling gives 1.78.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#yj646x","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 34 g of NH₃? (molar mass 17 g/mol)","opts":["6","1.2 × 10²⁴","4.01 × 10²³","2.05 × 10²⁵","3.61 × 10²⁴"],"correct":4,"why":"34 g / 17 g/mol = 2 mol NH₃. Times 3 hydrogen per molecule = 6 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.61 × 10²⁴.","traps":["That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each NH₃ holds 3 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 3.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Radioactive decay is what order kinetics?","opts":["2","3","0","1/2","1"],"correct":4,"why":"Half-life is constant regardless of N₀ - that's the 1st-order signature.","traps":["Second order would tie the half-life to how much you start with, doubling it each time the sample halves.","Third order is not what nuclear decay shows, since each nucleus decays on its own without needing to meet others.","Zero order would mean the same number of nuclei decay each second no matter how much sample is left, and the half-life would shrink as the sample shrinks.","Half order comes from the phrase half-life rather than from any rate law, and the constant half-life is exactly what marks the process as first order.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"originals:ab-9b3bacee93","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A buffer made from a weak acid with pKa 4.76 has a pH of 3.76. What is the ratio of conjugate base to weak acid?","opts":["1 to 10","1 to 1","1 to 2","10 to 1","1 to 100"],"correct":0,"why":"pH minus pKa is -1, so log(base/acid) = -1 and base/acid = 0.1. A pH below the pKa means the acid form dominates, and each pH unit is a factor of ten.","traps":[null,"Equal amounts would give a pH equal to the pKa.","The relationship is logarithmic. One pH unit below the pKa means ten times more acid.","That ratio would put the pH one unit ABOVE the pKa. A pH below the pKa means there is more acid than base.","One pH unit is a factor of ten, not one hundred."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-umba16","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"trans-2-butene reacts with Br2 in CCl4 (anti addition through a bromonium ion). Considering both new stereocenters, what is the stereochemical relationship of the 2,3-dibromobutane product?","opts":["A 1:1 mixture of the meso compound and the racemic (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) pair, because the bromonium ion opens at both carbons","A racemic mixture of the (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) enantiomers, formed in equal amounts by attack at either carbon","A single meso compound (the two enantiomers superimpose due to an internal mirror plane)","A cis-dibromide formed by syn addition","A single optically active (2R,3R) product only"],"correct":2,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 to trans-2-butene gives MESO 2,3-dibromobutane (R,S) with an internal mirror plane; cis-2-butene would give the racemic R,R/S,S pair. This is the canonical anti-addition diagnostic. Index 0 correct.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-13b7fabf1b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"Which base is strong enough to deprotonate a terminal alkyne (pKa about 25) essentially completely?","opts":["Sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO3 (the pKa of carbonic acid is about 6)","Sodium amide, NaNH2 (the pKa of ammonia is about 38)","Sodium ethoxide, NaOEt (the pKa of ethanol is about 16)","Sodium hydroxide, NaOH (the pKa of water is about 16)","Sodium acetate (the pKa of acetic acid is about 5)"],"correct":1,"why":"Equilibrium favors the side with the WEAKER acid, the one with the higher pKa. Cross out the metal to find the real base. Amide ion gives ammonia at pKa 38, which is weaker than the alkyne at 25, so the reaction goes forward. Hydroxide would give water at 16, which is stronger than the alkyne, so it does not.","traps":["Bicarbonate is a very weak base. It is nowhere near strong enough.",null,"Ethanol is a stronger acid than the alkyne, so ethoxide cannot pull that proton off to any useful extent.","Water is a STRONGER acid than the alkyne, so the equilibrium would favor the alkyne and hydroxide.","Acetate is a very weak base."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-1o3v4s0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexyne (shown) is treated with NaNH2, then with 1-bromopropane, and finally with Na in liquid NH3. Which statement correctly describes the final product?","opts":["1-nonyne, a terminal alkyne","4-nonyne, an internal alkyne (no reduction occurs)","trans-4-nonene, an internal E alkene","trans-2-nonene, an internal E alkene","cis-4-nonene, an internal Z alkene"],"correct":2,"why":"Acetylide alkylation of 1-hexyne with 1-bromopropane gives 4-nonyne; Na/NH3 then reduces that internal alkyne by anti addition to trans-4-nonene. Numbering: propyl(C3)-C#C-butyl(C4) places the bond at C4.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fqqcym","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexene (drawn) is treated with NBS under radical allylic conditions. The allylic radical formed is delocalized, and the product that results from bromination at the terminal carbon (with double-bond transposition) predominates as the conjugated, more substituted alkene. Which structure is that product?","opts":["BrC/C=C\\CCC","C=CC(Br)CCC","BrCCCCCC=C","BrC/C=C/CCC","C=CCC(Br)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS abstracts the C3 allylic H, giving an allyl radical delocalized between C1 and C3. Trapping at C1 transposes the double bond to C2=C3, giving the more substituted internal alkene (E)-1-bromo-2-hexene (option A); the E isomer is favored over Z. The non-transposed 3-bromo-1-hexene keeps a terminal alkene; the Z isomer and homoallylic placements are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1raxpuc","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,3-dibromopropane (BrCH2CH2CH2Br), then heated in aqueous acid. The dibromide drawn is the alkylating agent. Which structure is the major final product?","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Double intramolecular alkylation of the acetoacetate carbon by the 1,3-dibromide closes a ring: the central carbon plus the three-carbon tether forms a four-membered (cyclobutane) ring still bearing the acetyl and ester. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation leave the methyl ketone, giving 1-cyclobutylethanone/acetylcyclobutane (ring size 4 confirmed). The cyclopropyl ketone (option B, 2-C tether) and cyclopentyl ketone (option C, 4-C tether) are the wrong ring size; the open diketone (option D) skips cyclization; cyclobutanecarboxylic acid (option E) is the acid class (malonic route). Verified correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"BrCCCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1mnmhwn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"2-Pentyne (drawn), an UNsymmetrical internal alkyne, is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which statement best describes the outcome?","opts":["Only 2-pentanone forms, because Markovnikov addition always places the carbonyl oxygen on C2, the alkyne carbon carrying the shorter alkyl chain","A carboxylic acid forms by oxidative cleavage of the triple bond","A single aldehyde forms, because Markovnikov addition of water to an internal alkyne always delivers the oxygen to a chain-end carbon of the molecule","A roughly equal mixture of two regioisomeric ketones (2-pentanone and 3-pentanone) forms, because neither alkyne carbon is strongly favored","No reaction occurs, because internal alkynes are unreactive toward Hg(II)/acid"],"correct":3,"why":"For an unsymmetrical INTERNAL alkyne, the two carbons of the triple bond are similar in substitution, so Markovnikov selectivity is poor and acid-catalyzed hydration gives a mixture of both possible ketones. Internal alkynes give ketones, never aldehydes; the reaction does occur; and no C-C cleavage happens under these conditions.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#CCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-mnw0d4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic). The two new oxygens add to the same face of the ring. Which structure is the product?","opts":["OC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","O=C1CCCC1O","C1CC2OC2C1"],"correct":2,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 performs syn-dihydroxylation; same-face addition on the ring gives the cis-1,2-diol (meso cis-cyclopentane-1,2-diol), opt 0. C is the epoxide (peracid product), D is a mono-ol, E is an over-oxidized alpha-hydroxy ketone.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-rcvby2","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"1-Phenyl-2-propanone (phenylacetone, PhCH2COCH3) is treated with LDA, then CH3I. Deprotonation favors the more resonance-stabilized (benzylic) enolate. What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)C(CC)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C(C)=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccc(C)cc1","CC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The benzylic alpha carbon (PhCH2) gives the more resonance-stabilized enolate because charge delocalizes into the ring, and the stem specifies that enolate, so methylation occurs there to give PhCH(CH3)COCH3 (option A). Distractors methylate the methyl-side alpha carbon, alkylate the ring, add the wrong group, or dialkylate the benzylic carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1lvugyo","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH and warmed. The major product is 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one. This transformation is best classified as which kind of reaction?","opts":["Intramolecular aldol condensation","Claisen condensation","Cannizzaro reaction","Robinson annulation","Intermolecular crossed aldol condensation"],"correct":0,"why":"A terminal methyl enolate attacks the distal carbonyl carbon, closing a six-membered ring beta-hydroxy ketone that dehydrates to the conjugated enone (3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, C7H10O, loss of H2O verified). Both reacting centers are in the same molecule, so it is an intramolecular aldol condensation. Claisen needs an ester; Robinson needs a Michael donor/enone; Cannizzaro needs a non-enolizable aldehyde.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-9wwa4n","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Reaction of the cyclic alkene shown with O3 then Zn/AcOH gives a single dicarbonyl product. Classify the two carbonyl groups in that product.","opts":["Two ketones (a diketone)","One ketone and one carboxylic acid","One ketone and one aldehyde","Two carboxylic acids (a diacid)","Two aldehydes (a dialdehyde)"],"correct":0,"why":"1,2-dimethylcyclopentene is tetrasubstituted at the C=C (each sp2 carbon carries a methyl and a ring carbon, no H). Ring-opening cleavage places a ketone at both former alkene carbons, giving the open-chain diketone 2,6-heptanedione (C7H12O2). With no vinylic C-H, no aldehyde or acid forms.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gntirq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"(2S,3S)-2,3-dimethyloxirane (a single enantiomer of trans-2,3-epoxybutane) is treated with dilute aqueous acid (H3O+). What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","opts":["C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","C[C@H](O)[C@H](C)O","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](C)O","CC(O)C(C)O","CCC(O)CO"],"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed anti (backside) opening inverts one stereocenter. From (2S,3S) trans-epoxide, inverting one center gives (2R,3S) = meso-2,3-butanediol. Options 1/2 are the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) diols.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H]1O[C@H]1C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fp66n0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A benzenediazonium salt (C6H5N2+, from aniline + NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C) is added to a cold basic solution of phenol (drawn). Predict the major azo-coupling product.","opts":["Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1O","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(Nc2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccccc1/N=N/c1ccccc1","O=C1C=CC(=Nc2ccccc2)C=C1"],"correct":1,"why":"Diazonium salts are weak electrophiles that couple at the para position of the strongly activated phenol via EAS, giving 4-(phenylazo)phenol (Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1). The ortho isomer, the reduced N-H (diarylamine) bridge, an extra-OH catechol, and a quinone-imine are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"Oc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-zlk3b3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione is treated with NaOH and warmed (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1=CC(=O)CCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC(=O)C1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Intramolecular aldol closing the favored six-membered ring then dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (C7H10O). Keyed option A is the conjugated enone (verified identical to O=C1CCCC(C)=C1). Distractors: a non-conjugated methylcyclohexenone regioisomer, plain cyclohexanone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and a 5-membered ring. All valid, unique.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-18i1svf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"1-Cyclobutylethan-1-ol is treated with acid. A ring-expansion (alkyl) shift relieves ring strain before elimination. Which is the major alkene product?","opts":["CC1(O)CCCC1","CC=C1CCC1","C=CC1CCC1","C1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"The initial secondary cation on the exocyclic carbon triggers a ring-bond migration that expands cyclobutane to a 1-methylcyclopentyl (tertiary) cation, relieving strain. Zaitsev E1 gives 1-methylcyclopentene (option A). option B/option C are unexpanded cyclobutane alkenes, option D is a substitution (cyclopentanol) product, option E (cyclohexene) has the wrong ring size. Single correct answer.","traps":null,"qs":"OC(C)C1CCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1ywpsoa","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"cis-2-Butene (drawn) reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2. Bromine adds anti across the double bond. Which structure correctly represents the product of this single alkene?","opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CCBr","CCC(C)(Br)Br","C/C(Br)=C(\\Br)C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Option B is verified MESO (the 2R,3S diastereomer = trans-alkene/syn-addition outcome, the classic trap). C is geminal, D wrong skeleton/regiochem, E unreacted.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C\\C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-16yhmrn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (drawn) is placed in warm methanol containing no added strong base. Classify the competing reactions and identify which substitution mechanism is operative.","opts":["SN1 and E2 compete, pairing unimolecular substitution with a bimolecular elimination in which methanol removes a beta-hydrogen as bromide leaves in the same step","Only E2 occurs, giving exclusively the Hofmann alkene","Only SN2 occurs, with inversion of configuration at the tertiary carbon","SN1 and E1 compete (unimolecular), because the tertiary substrate ionizes to a carbocation in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile","SN2 and E2 compete (bimolecular), because methanol is a strong enough base and nucleophile to attack the substrate directly in one concerted step"],"correct":3,"why":"Tertiary halide in warm methanol (polar protic, weak nucleophile, no strong base) = solvolysis: SN1 and E1 compete; substitution via SN1. SN2 blocked (tertiary), E2 needs strong base (absent). Correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1bw83st","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv), limited to monobromination. What is the major product?","opts":["CC(C)(Br)c1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1Br","BrCC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. Cumene's single benzylic C-H is on the central carbon; the resulting radical is both tertiary AND benzylic (doubly stabilized), far weaker than the primary methyl C-H. Br installs there: (2-bromopropan-2-yl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates a primary methyl (non-benzylic, minor), opt[2]/opt[3] are ring (EAS) products, opt[4] is a dibrominated over-reaction.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-jwcdpe","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Acetic acid (drawn) is heated with excess ethanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (Fischer conditions). The product is then identified by IR. Which single answer correctly pairs the product with its diagnostic IR change relative to the starting acid?","opts":["Product is ethyl acetate; a new broad O-H stretch near 3300 cm-1 appears while the C=O absorption near 1740 cm-1 disappears entirely","Product is ethanol only; all carbonyl absorption is lost","Product is acetamide; a sharp N-H stretch (~3300) replaces the carbonyl","Product is acetaldehyde; the C=O moves to about 1715 cm-1 and a pair of diagnostic aldehyde C-H stretches near 2720 and 2820 cm-1 appears","Product is ethyl acetate; the broad O-H stretch (~2500-3300 cm-1) of the acid disappears while a strong C=O (~1740 cm-1) remains"],"correct":4,"why":"Fischer esterification of acetic acid with ethanol (H2SO4 cat.) gives ethyl acetate (CCOC(C)=O). Diagnostic IR: the very broad carboxylic acid O-H (2500-3300 cm-1) is lost while a strong ester C=O remains near 1740 cm-1. No new O-H, aldehyde C-H, or N-H forms.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1kvubba","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone is condensed with pyrrolidine (acid catalyst, water removed) to form an enamine. Which regiochemical product predominates?","opts":["CC1CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CN=C1CCCCC1C","OC1(C)CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CC1=C(N2CCCC2)CCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1N1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"With an unsymmetrical ketone, enamine formation favors the LESS-substituted double bond: forming the alkene toward the methylated carbon (option B) places the bulky pyrrolidine and the alpha-methyl in destabilizing A-1,3 strain, so it is disfavored. The C=C therefore goes away from the methyl group (option A). Option C is a saturated amine, D is a nonsense carbinolamine, and E is an N-methyl imine (wrong amine, wrong class).","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CCCCC1=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"own-organic:ace-0002","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"3-methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which product comes out of the flask in the largest amount?","opts":["CC(Br)C(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)Br","BrCCC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O"],"correct":2,"why":"The proton lands on the end carbon and leaves a secondary cation sitting right next to a tertiary carbon. A hydrogen slides over with its pair, the charge moves to the better spot, and the bromide finds it there. The skeleton you started with is not the skeleton you finish with.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)C","os":true,"src":"own-organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-19rdwte","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"2,3-dichlorobutane is drawn below. This molecule has two stereocenters, but one of its stereoisomers is a meso compound. Taking the meso coincidence into account, how many DISTINCT (different) stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane?","opts":["3 (a pair of enantiomers plus one meso compound)","4 (the full 2^n with no overlap)","1 (only the meso form exists)","2 (a single pair of enantiomers, because the meso form is one member of that pair)","6 (2^n for the two stereocenters, then doubled again for cis and trans forms)"],"correct":0,"why":"Two stereocenters give a 2^n upper bound of 4, but because both stereocenters carry identical substituent sets the (R,S) and (S,R) forms are the same achiral meso compound. That overlap removes one isomer, leaving 3 distinct stereoisomers: the (R,R)/(S,S) enantiomeric pair plus the single meso (R,S). Classic 2^n meso exception.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-s4anoj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"cis-4-tert-Butylcyclohexyl acetate (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH, then mild acidic workup (saponification). Which alcohol is produced?","opts":["CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](OC)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(=O)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":3,"why":"3D geometry analysis confirms SM acetate is cis and keyed option A alcohol is cis (option B is the trans diastereomer). Saponification cleaves the acyl-oxygen bond (C=O carbon attacked), never the alkyl C-O bond at the ring stereocenter, so the ring carbon keeps its configuration and the cis relationship to tert-butyl is preserved.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)O[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gnnsax","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The molecule drawn is the cyclopropenyl (cyclopropenium) cation. Apply Huckel's rule: count the pi electrons and classify the ring.","opts":["0 pi electrons; non-aromatic","2 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=0); aromatic","4 pi electrons (4n with n=1); antiaromatic","6 pi electrons; aromatic","2 pi electrons; antiaromatic"],"correct":1,"why":"One C=C provides 2 pi electrons and the cationic carbon is an empty p orbital completing cyclic overlap. 2 pi electrons = 4n+2 with n=0 -> aromatic, the textbook reason this tiny cation is unusually stable. Option 0 is correct; the 4-, 0-, and 6-electron counts are wrong, and 2 pi electrons cannot be antiaromatic.","traps":null,"qs":"[CH+]1C=C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-75v5a0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanoyl chloride (drawn) is treated with methanol. The bond to the stereocenter is never broken. What is the major product?","opts":["OC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Nucleophilic acyl substitution occurs only at the carbonyl carbon; the alpha stereocenter is a spectator, so configuration is retained. Recognition-level stereo, in DAT scope (no mechanism drawn).","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(Cl)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1odc4g1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is heated with excess KMnO4, then treated with acid. What is the major product?","opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"The isopropyl group has one benzylic C-H, so KMnO4 degrades the whole side chain to a single COOH carbon on the ring, giving benzoic acid (the two methyls are lost as CO2/carbonate). Benzylic alcohol, acetophenone, and 2-phenylpropanoic acid wrongly retain side-chain carbons; KMnO4 does not stop at those stages.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-7d9eh6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"(Bromomethyl)cyclopentane (shown) is solvolyzed in water, giving a single rearranged alcohol as the major product. The primary cation undergoes ring expansion. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C=C1CCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Ionization gives a primary exocyclic cation; a ring C-C bond migrates (ring expansion) to give a secondary cyclohexyl cation, trapped by water to give cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1). Cyclopentylmethanol (OCC1CCCC1) is the unrearranged product; methylenecyclopentane, 1-methylcyclopentene, and cyclohexene are elimination products, not the alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"BrCC1CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-19ll1h0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"The chiral epoxide (R)-2-methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is opened by sodium azide (NaN3, basic conditions). Considering BOTH the regiochemistry of basic opening AND the stereochemistry of the SN2 step, which statement is correct about the carbon that the azide attacks?","opts":["Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, which is not a stereocenter, so the original stereocenter is untouched and retains its configuration","Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, and the backside displacement inverts the configuration at the neighboring stereocenter as the ring opens","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon under basic conditions, and backside attack at that carbon inverts the configuration of the original stereocenter","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon with retention of configuration","Azide attacks both carbons, racemizing the product"],"correct":0,"why":"Under basic conditions azide does SN2 at the less-substituted terminal CH2. That carbon is not a stereocenter, so SN2 inversion there has no stereochemical consequence; the methyl-bearing stereocenter is never bonded to the nucleophile and retains its configuration. Layers basic-condition regioselectivity with the recognition that inversion only matters at the carbon actually attacked. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CO1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-cc3354","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Allylbenzene (3-phenyl-1-propene) is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. A student worries the benzene ring will redirect where bromine adds. Which structure is actually the major product?","opts":["C=CCc1ccccc1Br","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The terminal C=C is separated from the ring by a CH2, so it is NOT conjugated and gains no benzylic stabilization. Anti-Markovnikov addition simply puts Br on the terminal CH2: (3-bromopropyl)benzene. The trap options assume conjugation and place Br on an internal or benzylic carbon; others are a dibromide or ring-brominated arene.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-bf43c0807e","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"The CH2 between the two carbonyls of 2,4-pentanedione has a pKa near 9, while the alpha hydrogens of acetone have a pKa near 20. Why is the dione so much more acidic?","opts":["The two carbonyl groups donate electron density to the central carbon, making the resulting carbanion easier to form","It has a higher molar mass, and the extra mass spreads the negative charge over a larger volume","Its conjugate base is stabilized by resonance with two carbonyl groups instead of one","Its alpha carbon is sp hybridized","It forms stronger hydrogen bonds with water"],"correct":2,"why":"Remove a hydrogen from the carbon between two carbonyls and the negative charge can be pushed onto either oxygen. Oxygen is electronegative, so it holds a negative charge well, and two oxygen sinks beat one. That is why the pKa falls by about ten units. Keep in mind that where the charge lands matters more than how many resonance structures you can draw.","traps":["Carbonyl groups WITHDRAW electron density. That withdrawal is what stabilizes the anion.","Molar mass has nothing to do with acidity.",null,"The carbon is sp3 before the proton is removed. Hybridization is not the explanation.","Hydrogen bonding to solvent does not account for a pKa drop of this size."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-10gwkug","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n1-hexyne + excess H2, Pd/C","opts":["CCCCCC","C1CCCCC1","C=CCCCC","CCCCCCO","CC#CCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Unpoisoned Pd/C with excess H2 fully reduces the terminal alkyne (two equiv H2) to hexane (o0). Lindlar would be needed to stop at 1-hexene; the chain stays linear (no ring), no OH.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#183u3rc","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 120 miles at 60 mph and returns the same 120 miles at 90 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["72 mph","73.5 mph","82.5 mph","75 mph","36 mph"],"correct":0,"why":"Time out = 120/60 = 2 h, time back = 120/90 = 1.33 h. Average = 240 / 3.33 = 72 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":[null,"That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp006","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A solution that is 25% acid by volume is mixed with a solution that is 60% acid. To produce 35 liters of a final solution that is 40% acid, how many liters of the 25% solution must be used?","opts":["10 L","15 L","20 L","12 L","25 L"],"correct":2,"why":"Let x = L of 25%, then 35 − x = L of 60%. 0.25x + 0.60(35 − x) = 0.40(35). 0.25x + 21 − 0.60x = 14. −0.35x = −7. x = 20 L.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1377","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A freight truck averages 60 miles per hour for 4.5 hours. How many miles does it cover?","opts":["13.33","210","240","270","330"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 60 × 4.5 = 270 mi.","traps":["Dividing 60 by 4.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 3.5 hours instead of 4.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 4 hours flat.",null,"Using 5.5 hours instead of the 4.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1738","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"If x differs from 6 by exactly 1, what are the possible values of x?","opts":["7 or 5","-5 or 5","7","5 or -7","-5 or -7"],"correct":0,"why":"|x − 6| = 1 means x − 6 = ±1, so x = 7 or 5.","traps":[null,"That pairs 5 with its negative, and both branches here land above zero, at 7 and 5.","You solved x - 6 = 1 and stopped, and the branch x - 6 = -1 gives 5 as well.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","You subtracted the 6 instead of adding it back on both branches."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#c35opm","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (5 × 10⁻²)(3 × 10⁻⁶) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["1.5 × 10⁻⁷","1.5 × 10⁻⁸","1.5 × 10⁻⁹","8 × 10⁻⁷","1.5 × 10¹²"],"correct":0,"why":"5 × 3 = 15 and 10⁻² × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻⁸. 15 × 10⁻⁸ is not proper form, so write 1.5 × 10⁻⁷. Answer: 1.5 × 10⁻⁷.","traps":[null,"You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1495","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Five trays each hold one full set of instruments, but 5 instruments are missing overall, leaving 70 in total. How many instruments are in a full set?","opts":["13","14","15","16","20"],"correct":2,"why":"'Five less than 5 times a number' becomes 5x minus 5 = 70. Add the 5 to both sides for 5x = 75, then divide by 5 for 15. Undo the addition or subtraction first and the multiplication second.","traps":["Moved the 5 the wrong way: 70 minus 5 is 65, and 65 split five ways gives this.","Divided 70 by 5 and stopped there, leaving the subtracted 5 unaccounted for.",null,"Added the 5 back twice: 70 plus 5 plus 5 is 80, and 80 split five ways gives this.","Five 20s make 100, well past the 75 that 5x has to equal."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1735","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A target reading is 10, and an actual reading of x differs from the target by exactly 7. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["17 or -3","-3 or 3","-3 or -17","17","17 or 3"],"correct":4,"why":"|x − 10| = 7 means x − 10 = ±7, so x = 17 or 3.","traps":["That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","That pairs 3 with its negative, and the other branch is 10 plus 7, which is 17.","You moved the 10 to the wrong side and subtracted it instead of adding it back.","You solved x - 10 = 7 and stopped, and the branch x - 10 = -7 gives 3 as well.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1029","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"What is the sum of 8/14 and 8/12?","opts":["16/26","64/168","26/21","37/28","6/21"],"correct":2,"why":"Reduce first: 8/14 is 4/7 and 8/12 is 2/3. Sevenths and thirds meet at 21, so 12/21 plus 14/21 is 26/21. Reducing before you pick a denominator keeps you out of the big numbers.","traps":["You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 16 over 26.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.",null,"You forced both into twenty-eighths and turned 2/3 into 21/28, which is three quarters.","You put the reduced tops, 4 and 2, over 21 without rescaling either one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1474","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"How many centimeters are in 2.4 meters?","opts":["0.02","24","102.4","240","2400"],"correct":3,"why":"1 m = 100 cm, so 2.4 m × 100 = 240 cm.","traps":["You divided by 100 rather than multiplying, which sends the count the wrong way.","You multiplied by 10 instead of 100; meters to centimeters moves two places.","You added 100 to the 2.4 instead of multiplying the 2.4 by it.",null,"You multiplied by 1000, the kilometer-to-meter step, when meters to centimeters needs 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1497","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Half of a number, increased by 5, equals 13.5. What is the number?","opts":["8.5","16","17","18","21"],"correct":2,"why":"Set it up as x/2 plus 5 = 13.5. Subtract the 5 for x/2 = 8.5, then double for 17. A half in the total is a signal that the answer will be odd, so resist rounding before the doubling.","traps":["Stopped at the half: 13.5 minus 5 leaves 8.5, which is half the number.","Rounded the 8.5 down to 8 before doubling, which loses the odd half.",null,"Rounded the 8.5 up to 9 before doubling, which pushes the number one past.","Half of 21 is 10.5, and 10.5 plus 5 is 15.5, above the 13.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1021","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A vial is 6/8 full, and 1/4 of a vial is drawn out of it. What fraction of the vial is left?","opts":["1/2","5/8","7/12","3/4","6/32"],"correct":0,"why":"Quarters fit inside eighths, so 1/4 becomes 2/8. Then 6/8 minus 2/8 is 4/8, which is 1/2. Reducing first works just as well, since 3/4 minus 1/4 is a half.","traps":[null,"You put 1/4 over 8 but left the 1 on top; a quarter is 2/8.","You moved into twelfths, but eighths and quarters both live comfortably in eighths.","You reduced 6/8 to 3/4 and stopped before subtracting the quarter.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of subtracting them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1262","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"To the nearest hundredth, what value of x satisfies 11x + 10 = -3x - 9?","opts":["-2.36","-1.36","-0.36","0.07","1.36"],"correct":1,"why":"Add 3x to both sides for 14x + 10 = -9, then subtract 10 for 14x = -19. Divide by 14 and x is -19/14, which works out to -1.357 and rounds to -1.36. When every choice carries two decimals, that rounding step is part of the answer, so do the division in full before you trim it.","traps":["Substituting -2.36 leaves about -16 on the left and about -1.9 on the right.",null,"Substituting -0.36 leaves about 6 on the left and about -7.9 on the right.","The 10 was added to the -9 instead of subtracted, leaving 14x = 1.","Dividing 19 by 14 gives 1.36, but 14x = -19 makes x negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pf3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Bag has 4 red, 6 blue. Draw 3 with replacement. P(at least one red)?","opts":["12/10","1 − (4/10)³","(6/10)³","(4/10)³","1 − (6/10)³"],"correct":4,"why":"1 − P(all blue) = 1 − (6/10)³.","traps":["12/10 adds 4/10 three times, and a probability above 1 is the signal to switch to the flip-to-none move.","1 minus (4/10) cubed removes the all red run, when the run to remove is all blue.","(6/10) cubed is the chance of three blues in a row, the piece you subtract from 1.","(4/10) cubed is the chance that all three draws come up red, not that at least one does.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:g2dm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A circle's radius triples. Area changes by what factor?","opts":["×3","×6","×27","×81","×9"],"correct":4,"why":"Area scales as r². Tripling r gives 3² = 9× the area.","traps":["Linear scaling, but area is squared.","Multiplied the factor of 3 by the 2 in the exponent instead of squaring it.","That's cubed, for volume.","Squared the scale factor twice over, once more than an area calls for.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1457","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Two weather stations sit at (-12, 2) and (-6, 8) on a survey grid. Measured in a straight line, about how many units apart are they?","opts":["3.46","6","8.49","12","72"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (6)²) = √72 = 8.49.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1720","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"What is the value of 5^1 × 5^6?","opts":["0","15625","15630","78125","6103515625"],"correct":3,"why":"Same base × adds exponents: 5^(1+6) = 5^7 = 78125.","traps":["Two positive powers multiplied together can never give zero.","You multiplied the exponents, so you landed back on 5^6.","You added the two values, 15625 and 5, instead of multiplying them.",null,"You multiplied the bases as well as adding the exponents, which gives 25^7."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#ta7kr","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 100 and standard deviation 5. About what percent of scores fall between 100 and 105?","opts":["16%","34%","47.5%","50%","68%"],"correct":1,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 34%.","traps":["That is the tail beyond one SD, not the slice inside it.",null,"That is the slice out to TWO SD.","That is everything above the mean. The slice stops at one SD.","That is one SD on BOTH sides. Only the upper side is asked for."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#znh491","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 9 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 3rd place be awarded?","opts":["6","27","84","504","729"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 9P3 = 9 × 8 × 7 = 504.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 3 people. You still have to choose them from 9.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1130","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"What number is 68 percent of 225?","opts":["15.3","72","153","293","15300"],"correct":2,"why":"68% of 225 = 68/100 × 225 = 153.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.","You found the 32 percent left over rather than the 68 percent the question asks for.",null,"You added the 68 to the 225 instead of taking 68 percent of it.","You multiplied 68 by 225 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:cou1579","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Ten runners finish a race and the first three places are recorded in order. How many different orderings of the top three are possible?","opts":["13","30","120","720","1000"],"correct":3,"why":"nPr = 10!/(10-3)! = 720.","traps":["Added 10 and 3.","Multiplied 10 by 3.","That counts the groups with order ignored; each trio can be arranged 6 ways.",null,"Treated it as 10 choices for each of three slots, which lets an item repeat."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1479","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A rinse cycle runs for 3.4 minutes. How many seconds is that?","opts":["0.06","20.4","63.4","184","204"],"correct":4,"why":"1 min = 60 sec, so 3.4 min × 60 = 204 sec.","traps":["You divided the 3.4 by 60 instead of multiplying, which sends the count the wrong way.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You added 60 to the 3.4 instead of multiplying the 3.4 by it.","You read 3.4 minutes as 3 minutes and 4 seconds; the 0.4 is four tenths of a minute, which is 24 seconds.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1639","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A delivery fleet averages 50 drop-offs a day with a standard deviation of 10. What is the z score of a day with 35 drop-offs?","opts":["-15","-1.5","-0.67","-0.3","1.5"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (35 − 50)/10 = -1.5.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, one step short of dividing by the standard deviation.",null,"Flipped the ratio and divided 10 by the 15-point gap.","Divided the 15-point gap by the mean of 50 instead of by the standard deviation of 10.","Right size, wrong side: 35 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1310","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A dental office books 5 cleanings and 2 fillings each day. Over 3 days, how many appointments is that?","opts":["10","11","13","21","30"],"correct":3,"why":"Bracket first: 5 + 2 = 7, then 7 times 3 = 21. Without the bracket this line would be 5 + 6 = 11, which is exactly the trap sitting in the choices. The bracket is the whole question here.","traps":["You added everything, so the times sign never did any work.","You ignored the bracket and multiplied 2 by 3 before adding the 5.","You multiplied the two numbers inside the bracket and then added the 3.",null,"You multiplied all three numbers, treating the plus sign as another times sign."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#1nnrf4b","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 6 red and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["5/22","1/4","5/11","1/2","5/24"],"correct":0,"why":"First red: 6/12. Second red: 5/11. Multiply: 5/22.","traps":[null,"That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1136","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"What is 42 percent of 476?","opts":["19.99","199.92","276.08","518","19992"],"correct":1,"why":"42% of 476 = 42/100 × 476 = 199.92.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 58 percent left over rather than the 42 percent the question asks for.","You added the 42 to the 476 instead of taking 42 percent of it.","You multiplied 42 by 476 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1459","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"In the coordinate plane, how far apart are the points (3, 0) and (9, 5), to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["3.32","6","7.81","11","61"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (5)²) = √61 = 7.81.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1054","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is 5/8 multiplied by 2/8?","opts":["5/32","40/16","10/16","5/64","7/64"],"correct":0,"why":"Multiply across: (5·2)/(8·8) = 10/64 = 5/32.","traps":[null,"You flipped the second fraction and divided: 5/8 divided by 2/8 is 40/16.","You multiplied the tops but added the bottoms (8 + 8) instead of multiplying them.","You halved the top of 10/64 while leaving the bottom alone; reducing has to act on both.","You added the tops (5 + 2) over the multiplied bottoms instead of multiplying across."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1263","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What value of x satisfies 11x + 9 = 3x + 3?","opts":["-1.75","-0.75","0.25","0.75","1.5"],"correct":1,"why":"Move x: (11-3)x = 3-9 → 8x = -6, so x = -0.75.","traps":["Substituting -1.75 gives -10.25 on the left and -2.25 on the right.",null,"Substituting 0.25 gives 11.75 on the left and 3.75 on the right.","Dividing -6 by 8 gives -0.75, so this answer drops the minus sign.","The 9 was added to the 3 instead of subtracted, leaving 8x = 12."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-q46gn4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"The terminal side of an angle θ in standard position passes through the point (-3, 4). What is cos θ?","opts":["-3/5","-4/3","-4/5","3/5","4/5"],"correct":0,"why":"Treat the point as a right triangle hanging off the origin: the horizontal leg is -3, the vertical leg is 4, and the distance from the origin is the square root of 9 plus 16, which is 5. Cosine is the x coordinate over that distance, so it is -3/5. The distance is always positive, so the sign of the answer comes entirely from the coordinate on top.","traps":[null,"This is tan θ, the y coordinate over the x coordinate.","This uses the y coordinate and attaches the sign that belongs to the x coordinate.","This drops the negative sign, but the point sits to the left of the y axis.","This is sin θ, using the y coordinate where the x coordinate belongs."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1163","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A $318 tablet is marked down 45 percent. What is the sale price?","opts":["143.1","174.9","273","303.69","363"],"correct":1,"why":"New = 318 × (1 - 45/100) = 318 × 0.55 = 174.9.","traps":["That is 45 percent of 318, the discount itself rather than what is left to pay.",null,"You subtracted 45 dollars instead of 45 percent, and 45 percent of 318 is 143.10.","That takes off 4.5 percent; a 45 percent cut means multiplying by 0.55.","You added 45 dollars, and this price is coming down by 45 percent."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#x7vu8n","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 126 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["2100 m/s","454 m/s","1.26 × 10⁵ m/s","0.035 m/s","35 m/s"],"correct":4,"why":"126 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 35 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":["You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp002","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"If log₂(x) + log₂(x − 6) = 4, what is the value of x?","opts":["x = 12","x = 4","x = −2","x = 16","x = 8"],"correct":4,"why":"log₂(x(x−6)) = 4, so x(x−6) = 16, so x² − 6x − 16 = 0 → (x − 8)(x + 2) = 0. x = 8 or x = −2. Reject negative (log undefined). x = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1640","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A data set has a mean of 75 and a standard deviation of 5. What is the z score of a value of 70?","opts":["-5","-1","-0.07","1","14"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (70 − 75)/5 = -1.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, stopping before the division by 5.",null,"Divided the 5-point gap by the mean of 75 instead of by the standard deviation of 5.","Right size, wrong side: 70 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative.","Divided 70 by 5 without first subtracting the mean."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1308","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A delivery contains 8 loose vials plus 7 trays holding 4 vials each. How many vials is that in total?","opts":["19","28","36","39","60"],"correct":2,"why":"Multiply before adding: 7 × 4 = 28, then 8 + 28 = 36.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:bn4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A square storage room measures 19 feet on each side. What is its floor area in square feet?","opts":["341","361","379","381","441"],"correct":1,"why":"(20−1)² = 400 − 40 + 1 = 361.","traps":["You made the middle term 60 rather than 40, which lands 20 below 361.",null,"You subtracted 20 and then 1 as well, instead of subtracting 40 and adding 1.","You used 20 for the middle term instead of 2 times 20 times 1, which is 40.","You added the middle term instead of subtracting it, which squares 21, not 19."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1c5w1vp","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$1000 is invested at 20% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 3 years?","opts":["$728.00","$1440.00","$1600.00","$1728.00","$2073.60"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 1000 × (1.2)³ = $1728.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:int1438","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A lab equipment fund of $5000 earns 8.75% interest compounded annually for 3 years. To the nearest cent, what is the total amount in dollars at the end?","opts":["1312.5","5000.26","5437.5","6312.5","6430.69"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 5000·(1.0875)^3 = 6430.69.","traps":["That is the simple interest for three years, and the question asks for the ending amount.","Added rt as about twenty-six cents instead of applying the rate to the balance each year.","Grew the balance for one year instead of three.","Used simple interest, so years two and three never earned interest on the earlier interest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1419","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A research assistant mixes 100 mL of a 25% sugar solution with 100 mL of a 10% sugar solution. What is the percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["8.75","12.5","15","17.5","35"],"correct":3,"why":"Solute total = 25 + 10 = 35 mL in 200 mL → 17.5%.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rt5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"You drive 60 mph for 2 hr, then 40 mph for 3 hr. Average speed?","opts":["48 mph","50 mph","100 mph","52 mph","45 mph"],"correct":0,"why":"Average speed is never the average of the speeds; it is total distance over total time. The legs cover 60 times 2, or 120 miles, and 40 times 3, or another 120 miles, so 240 miles in 5 hours gives 48 mph. The tell is that the times differ, so the 3 hours at 40 mph drag the average below the halfway point of 50.","traps":[null,"Averaging 60 and 40 straight across, which ignores the extra hour spent at 40.","Adding the two speeds together.","Pairing each speed with the other leg's time, 60 for 3 hours and 40 for 2.","Estimating between the two speeds instead of dividing 240 miles by 5 hours."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1154","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $245 airline seat rises 67 percent over a holiday week. What is the new price?","opts":["80.85","164.15","178","312","409.15"],"correct":4,"why":"New = 245 × (1 + 67/100) = 245 × 1.67 = 409.15.","traps":["You took the 67% off the price instead of putting it on.","You stopped on the increase itself, 164.15, instead of adding it to the 245.","You subtracted 67 dollars when the price is going up.","You added 67 dollars instead of adding 67 percent of the 245.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261001"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s15"}},{"id":"FULL_2","name":"Full-Length Test 2","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-7-169wbe8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A culture of dividing cells is treated with a drug that prevents tubulin subunits from polymerizing into microtubules. A few hours later the cells are examined. Where would most of them be, and why?","opts":["Arrested in telophase, because the nuclear envelope cannot reassemble without tubulin","Arrested in G1, because tubulin is needed to pass the restriction point","Arrested in mitosis before anaphase, with the spindle checkpoint still signaling","Dividing on schedule, because actin filaments take over the spindle's job","Arrested in S phase, because the replication fork travels along microtubules"],"correct":2,"why":"Ask which step becomes impossible first, then ask which checkpoint notices. With no microtubules the kinetochores are never captured, so the spindle checkpoint keeps sending its wait signal and the cell cannot begin anaphase. Cells already past that point finish, and everything behind them piles up in mitosis. Drugs at either end of tubulin dynamics, one blocking assembly and one freezing filaments so they cannot shorten, stall cells at the very same place.","traps":["Telophase comes after the chromosomes have already been separated, so a cell that never separated them cannot reach it.","The restriction point weighs growth factors and cell size, and nothing about it requires a spindle to exist yet.",null,"Actin drives the cleavage furrow, but it cannot attach kinetochores or sort chromosomes, so it is no substitute for the spindle.","Replication forks are driven by enzymes acting directly on the DNA, not by anything being hauled along microtubules."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-v3v9kk","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"A cell spends most of its life in interphase, which is divided into three phases, and then divides. Which sequence puts the interphase stages in order and correctly names the one in which the DNA is copied?","opts":["G1, then G2, then S, with the DNA copied during S","S, then G1, then G2, with the DNA copied during S","G1, then S, then G2, with the DNA copied during G2","G0, then G1, then G2, with the DNA copied during G2","G1, then S, then G2, with the DNA copied during S"],"correct":4,"why":"The names carry the answer. G1 and G2 are gaps, and they are numbered by which side of S phase they sit on, so the order can only be G1, S, G2. Synthesis here means DNA synthesis, so replication happens in S and nowhere else. G2 is when the cell grows and verifies the copy before it commits to mitosis.","traps":["The two gap phases are named for where they sit around S phase, so G2 cannot come before it.","This puts synthesis before the first gap, and a cell that has just divided needs to grow and check itself before it commits to copying its DNA.","The order is right but the copying is not, since G2 is the gap after replication, used for growth and for checking the copied DNA.","G0 is not a stage every cell passes through, it is a resting state that some cells step into out of G1.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In human populations, birth weight clusters tightly around about 7 pounds and the average has changed very little over time. Which type of natural selection does this pattern illustrate?","opts":["Drift","Stabilizing","Sexual selection acting on a trait that improves mating success","Directional","Disruptive"],"correct":1,"why":"Match the shape of the distribution to the mode of selection. Selection that favors the middle and trims both tails leaves a single narrow peak that stays put, which is exactly what birth weight looks like: very small infants and very large ones have both historically faced more difficulty than average sized ones. Directional selection would slide that peak to one side, and disruptive selection would split it into two.","traps":["Drift is random and would not hold a trait so precisely at one value across many separate populations.",null,"Sexual selection shapes traits that affect success in finding a mate, and birth weight is fixed long before an individual reaches reproductive age.","Directional selection would move average birth weight steadily heavier or lighter over generations, and the average has held its position.","Disruptive selection would favor very small and very large infants over average ones and produce two peaks, the opposite of one tight cluster."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-76s5y6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"For each molecule of ATP the sodium potassium pump hydrolyzes, how many ions move and in which direction?","opts":["Two sodium in, two potassium out","Three sodium out, two potassium in","Three sodium in, two potassium out","Two sodium out, three potassium in","Three sodium out, three potassium in"],"correct":1,"why":"This is a number worth owning outright: three sodium leave, two potassium enter, one ATP spent. Because three positive charges go out and only two come back in, the pump leaves the inside slightly more negative, which is what electrogenic means. Sodium is the ion that belongs outside, so picture the pump shoving it back out.","traps":["Sodium leaves rather than enters, and a two for two exchange would move no net charge.",null,"Both directions are reversed, the pump keeps sodium low inside and potassium high inside.","The counts are swapped, three is the sodium number and two is the potassium number.","A three for three exchange would move no net charge, and this pump is electrogenic."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e027","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A symbiotic relationship in which one organism benefits while the other is neither helped nor harmed is called:","opts":["Competition","Mutualism","Amensalism (one harmed, one unaffected)","Parasitism","Commensalism"],"correct":4,"why":"Score each partner with a plus, a minus, or a zero and these names sort themselves out. Mutualism is plus plus, parasitism and predation are plus minus, competition is minus minus, and commensalism is plus zero. Barnacles riding on a whale are the standard picture: the barnacle gets transport and a feeding current while the whale carries on unaffected. Truly neutral partners are rare in nature, so exam items keep it clean by telling you outright that the second organism is unaffected.","traps":["Competition costs both organisms, since each reduces what the other can obtain.","Mutualism has both partners gaining, the way plant roots and mycorrhizal fungi trade sugar for minerals.","Amensalism is the mirror image of this relationship: one organism is harmed while the other is unaffected.","Parasitism has one partner gaining at the other's expense, so the second organism is harmed rather than untouched.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g035","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a dihybrid cross where a homozygous recessive genotype at one gene masks the expression of a second gene, as in the Bombay phenotype, what phenotypic ratio is expected in the F2 generation?","opts":["9:6:1","12:3:1","13:3","9:3:3:1","9:3:4"],"correct":4,"why":"Start from the ordinary dihybrid 9:3:3:1 and ask which classes end up looking identical. In recessive epistasis, being homozygous recessive at the masking gene hides the second gene completely, so one of the 3 classes merges with the 1 class and becomes 4. That leaves 9:3:4, and the appearance of a 4 in any modified ratio is your signal that a recessive genotype is doing the masking.","traps":["This is duplicate gene action with an additive effect, where the two single-dominant classes look alike and combine into 6.","A 12 appears when a dominant allele at one gene does the masking, merging the 9 and one 3 class together.","This comes from dominant suppression, where the 9, a 3 and the 1 all end up sharing one phenotype.","That is the unmodified dihybrid ratio, seen when two genes act independently with no interaction between them.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-n1b43c","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"During anaphase two motions happen at the same time: the chromatids travel toward the poles, and the poles themselves move farther apart. Which microtubules drive each of those motions?","opts":["Kinetochore microtubules shorten to draw chromatids poleward, and polar microtubules slide to push the poles apart","Kinetochore microtubules lengthen to shove the chromatids poleward, while astral microtubules shorten and draw the poles together","Astral microtubules drag the chromatids inward, and kinetochore microtubules shove the two poles apart","Actin filaments pull the chromatids poleward, and microtubules push the two poles apart from the middle","Polar microtubules pull the chromatids, and kinetochore microtubules anchor each pole to the cell cortex"],"correct":0,"why":"Name each fiber by what it touches. Kinetochore microtubules run from a pole to a chromosome and shorten, reeling the chromatids in. Polar microtubules from opposite poles overlap in the middle and slide past one another, which lengthens the spindle and drives the poles apart, with astral fibers pulling from the cortex to help. Two separate motions, two separate sets of fibers, and the names tell you which is which.","traps":[null,"The chromatids are pulled rather than pushed, so those fibers shorten, and drawing the poles together would undo the separation the cell just achieved.","Astral microtubules reach outward from each pole toward the cortex and never touch a chromosome, since only kinetochore microtubules attach to chromosomes.","Actin drives the cleavage furrow after the chromosomes have been sorted, and it plays no part in moving them.","This swaps the two jobs, because the microtubules that touch chromosomes are the ones named for the kinetochore and the polar ones overlap each other in the middle."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d027","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Which set of features is present in all chordates at some point in the life cycle?","opts":["Bilateral symmetry only","Notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, pharyngeal slits, post-anal tail","Notochord, ventral solid nerve cord, pharyngeal slits and post-anal tail","Vertebral column only","Coelom"],"correct":1,"why":"The four chordate features are worth being able to recite, and the phrase at some point in the life cycle is doing real work in the stem. You had all four as an embryo: a notochord, later replaced by vertebrae, a dorsal hollow nerve cord that became your brain and spinal cord, pharyngeal slits that became structures of the jaw, ear and throat, and a post-anal tail. A sea squirt larva shows all four and then loses most of them as it settles into the adult form. When a chordate question appears, run that list of four in order.","traps":["Bilateral symmetry is shared with flatworms, molluscs and arthropods, so it cannot define chordates.",null,"A ventral solid nerve cord is the protostome pattern seen in earthworms and insects; the chordate nerve cord is dorsal and hollow.","A vertebral column belongs to the vertebrates, a subgroup within the phylum; tunicates and lancelets are chordates with no vertebrae.","A coelom is shared with annelids, molluscs and echinoderms, so it does not pick out chordates either."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The blastopore forms during gastrulation. In which group of animals does the blastopore become the mouth?","opts":["Deuterostomes","Protostomes","Both equally","Neither","Humans and other vertebrates"],"correct":1,"why":"The names carry the answer once you translate them. Protostome means first mouth, so the blastopore, the first opening, becomes the mouth and the anus forms second. Deuterostome means second mouth, so the blastopore becomes the anus instead. Attach the rest of the pattern to each name: protostomes show spiral determinate cleavage and schizocoely, while deuterostomes show radial indeterminate cleavage and enterocoely and include the echinoderms and all chordates.","traps":["In deuterostomes the blastopore becomes the anus and the mouth breaks through second, which is what the prefix deutero, meaning second, is telling you.",null,"The fate of the blastopore is one of the defining splits between these two groups, so it cannot come out the same way in both.","The blastopore always becomes one of the two openings of the gut, either the mouth or the anus depending on the lineage.","Vertebrates, humans included, are deuterostomes, so the blastopore becomes the anus and the mouth forms later as a second opening."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1n24d6b","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Trace one electron through noncyclic (linear) electron flow. Which order is correct?","opts":["Water, photosystem II, plastoquinone, cytochrome complex, plastocyanin, photosystem I, ferredoxin, NADP+","Water, photosystem I, ferredoxin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, plastocyanin, photosystem II, NADP+","Water, photosystem II, ferredoxin, plastocyanin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, photosystem I, and finally NADP+","Water, photosystem I, plastoquinone, cytochrome complex, plastocyanin, photosystem II, ferredoxin, NADP+","NADP+, ferredoxin, photosystem I, plastocyanin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, photosystem II, water"],"correct":0,"why":"The trap in this one is the numbering. Photosystem II fires first even though it carries the higher number, because it was discovered second. Hold the shape in your head as the letter Z: water at the bottom left, an uphill jump at photosystem II, a slide down through the carriers, a second uphill jump at photosystem I, then NADP+ waiting at the top right.","traps":[null,"Photosystem I appears first here and ferredoxin appears far too early, since ferredoxin only receives electrons after the second light hit.","Ferredoxin sits on the far side of photosystem I, not between photosystem II and the cytochrome complex.","The photosystems are numbered by discovery order, not working order, so starting at photosystem I flips the chain.","This runs the whole chain backwards, with NADP+ donating electrons instead of accepting them at the end."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d016","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"An adult marine animal is radially symmetric, but its larva is bilaterally symmetric. The animal most likely belongs to which group?","opts":["Cnidarian","Sponge","Echinoderm","Mollusc","A flatworm such as a planarian"],"correct":2,"why":"Symmetry is worth checking at both ends of the life cycle, because one group changes partway through. Sea stars, urchins and sand dollars hatch as bilateral swimming larvae and only take on five-part radial symmetry as adults, which tells you the radial look is secondary, acquired later. That is also why echinoderms group with chordates as deuterostomes despite how the adult looks. When a stem hands you a bilateral larva with a radial adult, it is pointing at the echinoderm choice.","traps":["Cnidarians are radial animals and are not bilaterians at all, so a bilateral larval stage is not part of their story.","Sponges are mostly irregular in shape and have no true tissues, so neither half of the description fits.",null,"Molluscs stay bilateral as adults; even a snail is a bilateral animal whose shell coils, rather than a radial one.","Flatworms are bilateral as larvae and as adults, with a definite head end, and never become radial."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p038","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Red blood cells from which blood type can be given to a recipient of any ABO and Rh type, making it the universal donor?","opts":["A+","AB+","O negative","B-","O+ (Rh positive)"],"correct":2,"why":"This is an antigen counting question, not an antibody one. Donor red cells are safe in anyone only when they display nothing for the recipient's antibodies to grab, and type O negative displays no A antigen, no B antigen, and no Rh antigen. Flip the logic for the universal recipient, which is the blood type whose plasma carries the fewest antibodies, AB positive.","traps":["Type A positive carries both the A antigen and the Rh antigen, so anti-A antibodies or Rh sensitization in the recipient could attack it.","AB positive is the universal recipient because its plasma holds no anti-A or anti-B antibodies, but as a donor it displays the most antigens of all.",null,"B negative avoids the Rh problem but still displays the B antigen, which the anti-B antibodies in a type A or type O recipient would attack.","O positive red cells carry no A or B antigen, but the Rh antigen is still there and an Rh negative recipient can respond to it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-8bmdz8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"One glucose is oxidized completely through glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, and the Krebs cycle. How many CO2 molecules are released, and where do they come from?","opts":["2, both from pyruvate oxidation","12, six carbons released twice","4, all from the Krebs cycle","6, two from pyruvate oxidation and four from the Krebs cycle","6, two released during glycolysis and four during the Krebs cycle"],"correct":3,"why":"Carbon bookkeeping settles this before you recall a single enzyme. Glucose has six carbons and all six leave as CO2, so the total has to be six. One carbon comes off each pyruvate on the way to acetyl-CoA, which is two, and each turn of the cycle releases two more, which is four across the two turns.","traps":["This counts only the conversion of the two pyruvates into acetyl-CoA and stops there.","Glucose holds six carbons total, so six is the ceiling rather than the halfway mark.","This counts the two turns of the cycle but forgets the carbon lost on the way in.",null,"Glycolysis releases no CO2 at all; its six carbons leave intact as two three-carbon pyruvates."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-f98995ebcf","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which hormone is released from the posterior pituitary but is actually made in the hypothalamus?","opts":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)","Growth hormone (GH)","Prolactin","Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)"],"correct":4,"why":"The posterior pituitary is a storage closet, not a factory. Hypothalamic neurons make ADH and oxytocin, ship them down their axons, and store them in the posterior lobe until release. The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones.","traps":["TSH is an anterior pituitary hormone.","GH is made AND released by the anterior pituitary.","Prolactin is made and released by the anterior pituitary.","ACTH is an anterior pituitary hormone.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-3-q3fw7z","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Compared with a peptide hormone acting on the same tissue, a steroid hormone usually produces a response that:","opts":["Begins faster and fades faster","Begins faster and lasts longer","Begins more slowly and lasts longer","Begins more slowly and fades faster","Is identical in timing, since both work through second messengers"],"correct":2,"why":"Timing follows mechanism. A peptide hormone flips switches that are already built, so cAMP rises and kinases fire within seconds, and the effect dies as soon as the messenger is broken down. A steroid changes which genes are transcribed, so new protein has to be made before anything happens, and that protein keeps working for hours after the hormone is gone. Gene transcription means slow on and slow off.","traps":["That is the peptide profile, since second messenger cascades rise quickly and quit quickly.","Steroid effects are not fast, transcription and translation both take time.",null,"Steroid effects persist, because the newly made protein lingers after the signal ends.","Steroids do not use second messengers, they act as transcription factors."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e045","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the equation for logistic population growth?","opts":["dN/dt = rN(N-K)/K","N = N₀ × e^(rt)","dN/dt = rN(K-N)/K","N = K","dN/dt = rN"],"correct":2,"why":"Logistic growth is exponential growth with a brake bolted on. The rN piece is the same engine that drives exponential growth, and the (K minus N) over K term is a fraction between 0 and 1 measuring how much room is left. When N is small that fraction sits near 1 and growth looks exponential; as N approaches K it shrinks toward 0 and growth stops, producing the S-shaped curve. Read the brake term that way and you never have to memorize the formula blind.","traps":["This reverses the sign, which would make the population shrink whenever it sits below carrying capacity and grow once it passes it.","This is the integrated form of exponential growth, giving population size over time rather than a logistic rate of change.",null,"This states that the population equals carrying capacity, which is the endpoint rather than an equation for growth.","This is exponential growth, with no ceiling at all, so the population would never level off."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-18jhy06","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"A plant growing in dry soil closes its stomata in the middle of the day. What happens inside the guard cells to make that happen?","opts":["The guard cells divide and seal the pore with new cells","Carbon dioxide inside the leaf falls, and the drop pulls the pore shut","The guard cell chloroplasts stop absorbing blue light and the pore contracts","Potassium ions leave, water follows by osmosis, and the cells go limp","Potassium ions enter, water follows them in, and the cells swell"],"correct":3,"why":"Guard cells behave like two long balloons: pump solute in and they swell and bow apart, let solute out and they slump back together. Drought sends a hormone signal, abscisic acid, that opens channels for potassium to leave, water follows it out, and the pore closes. Water always follows the salt, and that single sentence rebuilds either direction on demand.","traps":["Guard cells do not divide to open or close a pore, since the pore is controlled by their shape.","Low carbon dioxide inside the leaf is a signal to open the pore, not to close it.","Blue light is still hitting the leaf at midday, and the drought signal closes the pore in spite of it.",null,"That is the opening mechanism, which runs in exactly the other direction when water is plentiful."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g064","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A woman who is red-green colorblind has children with a man who has normal color vision. Red-green color blindness is X-linked recessive. What is expected among their children?","opts":["No effect","All sons affected, all daughters carriers","All sons normal","25% affected each","All daughters affected and all sons unaffected"],"correct":1,"why":"An affected mother carries the colorblind allele on both of her X chromosomes, so every single child receives one copy from her. A son pairs that X with a Y and has nothing to mask it, so all sons are colorblind. A daughter pairs it with her father's normal X, so she sees color normally while carrying the allele. This criss-cross from mother to son is the classic X-linked recessive signature worth recognising on sight.","traps":["The mother is affected and passes an affected X to every child, so this cross certainly has consequences.",null,"Sons take their only X chromosome from the mother, and both of hers carry the colorblind allele.","Quarter fractions arise when each parent contributes an allele by chance, but here the mother's contribution is certain.","This reverses the pattern: the father gives each daughter a normal X that masks the allele, and he gives his sons only a Y."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p031","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Cardiac output equals stroke volume multiplied by which quantity?","opts":["Heart rate","Blood pressure","Vessel diameter","Hematocrit","Ejection fraction"],"correct":0,"why":"Cardiac output is how much blood the heart delivers each minute, so it has to combine how much leaves per beat with how many beats happen per minute. Stroke volume times heart rate gives volume per minute, and a typical resting value is about 70 milliliters times 70 beats per minute, close to 5 liters per minute. When a cardiac formula gets confusing, check the units, since the pairing that yields volume per minute is the right one.","traps":[null,"Blood pressure is what results when output meets resistance in the vessels, not a factor inside the output formula.","Vessel diameter sets resistance downstream and says nothing about how much blood the heart ejects per minute.","Hematocrit describes what fraction of blood is red cells, which affects viscosity and oxygen carrying rather than volume pumped.","Ejection fraction is the share of the filled ventricle that gets ejected, so it helps determine stroke volume rather than multiplying it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p019","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which cells of the stomach lining secrete hydrochloric acid?","opts":["G cells","Chief cells","Mucous neck cells","Goblet cells","Parietal cells"],"correct":4,"why":"Sort the stomach lining by what each cell hands over. Parietal cells pump out hydrochloric acid and also make intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption, while chief cells release pepsinogen, the inactive form of the protein digesting enzyme. The acid from the parietal cells is what converts pepsinogen into active pepsin, so pair them as parietal for acid and chief for enzyme.","traps":["G cells release the hormone gastrin, which tells the parietal cells to make acid rather than making it themselves.","Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the enzyme precursor that the acid then activates.","Mucous neck cells produce the mucus layer that shields the stomach wall from its own acid.","Goblet cells secrete protective mucus and are found mainly in the intestine and the airways.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1v7ag0l","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Apoptosis runs on a family of enzymes that sit in the cell at all times as inactive precursors and are switched on in a cascade once the decision to die is made. What type of enzyme are they, and how does the cascade work?","opts":["Proteases, switched on by cleavage, initiator caspases activating executioner ones","Nucleases, activated by a calcium surge, which cut the plasma membrane into blebs","Phosphatases, activated by cytochrome c, which strip phosphate off the mitotic spindle","Kinases, activated by cyclin binding, which phosphorylate the nuclear lamins","Ligases, activated by ATP binding, which crosslink the cytoskeleton into a rigid mesh"],"correct":0,"why":"Caspases are proteases, and the name is worth unpacking: they carry a cysteine in the active site and cut their targets after aspartate residues. They wait in the cytosol as inactive procaspases, which lets a cell commit to dying in seconds without having to make anything new. Initiator caspases are activated first and then cleave executioner caspases, which go on to cut hundreds of cell proteins. Cleaving is both how they get switched on and how they do their work.","traps":[null,"Nucleases do act later in apoptosis to chop up the DNA, but they are downstream, and a nuclease cannot cut a membrane.","Cytochrome c does belong to apoptosis, but what it triggers is a protease cascade, and stripping phosphates off the spindle is not part of cell death.","Lamin phosphorylation is how a cell takes its nuclear envelope apart for mitosis, which is a different process run by a different enzyme.","Ligases join molecules together, and apoptosis works by taking cell structures apart rather than stiffening them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1hhdtel","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Two cells from the same organism are examined. One is partway through mitosis and the other is partway through meiosis I. Which statement distinguishes the two correctly?","opts":["In mitosis sister chromatids separate, while in meiosis I homologous chromosomes separate","In mitosis the daughter cells differ genetically, while in meiosis I they are identical","In mitosis homologous chromosomes pair up and cross over, while in meiosis I they stay apart","In mitosis the chromosome number is halved, while in meiosis I it stays the same","In mitosis a spindle forms, while in meiosis I no spindle is required at all"],"correct":0,"why":"One sentence sorts almost every mitosis versus meiosis question: mitosis splits sisters, meiosis I splits homologs. That single difference explains the rest, because pulling homologs apart is what halves the number and what requires them to pair up beforehand. Picture anaphase in each: in mitosis a chromosome comes apart at its centromere, while in meiosis I the centromeres stay intact and whole chromosomes travel opposite ways. Meiosis II then looks like mitosis running on a haploid cell.","traps":[null,"Mitosis produces genetically identical daughters, and meiosis is where crossing over and independent assortment create the variation.","This is backward, because pairing and crossing over are the signature of prophase I of meiosis and homologs behave independently in mitosis.","Halving is the meiotic outcome, and mitosis preserves the chromosome number exactly, which is what lets it repeat indefinitely.","Both divisions use a spindle, because any cell that moves chromosomes needs microtubules to do the moving."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-yn3j9l","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Oxygen never appears as a reactant in any Krebs cycle reaction, yet the cycle stops almost immediately when a cell runs out of oxygen. Why?","opts":["Without oxygen the matrix pH drops far enough to denature the cycle's dehydrogenase enzymes","Without oxygen the cycle runs backward and consumes the ATP it previously made","Without oxygen pyruvate can no longer cross the inner mitochondrial membrane","Without oxygen acetyl-CoA is redirected into fatty acid synthesis by a direct oxygen sensor","Without oxygen the chain stops, so NAD+ is never regenerated for the cycle"],"correct":4,"why":"The link is the supply of oxidized carriers. Three cycle steps need NAD+ and one needs FAD, and the only way a cell restocks them in bulk is by dumping electrons onto oxygen at the end of the chain. No oxygen means no reoxidation, every carrier sits in its loaded form, and each dehydrogenase step has nothing left to hand electrons to.","traps":["The matrix does not acidify enough to denature enzymes, and the block is a missing reactant rather than protein damage.","The cycle's reactions are not simply reversed when oxygen disappears; the pathway stalls instead.","The pyruvate transporter does not depend on oxygen; what runs short is oxidized carriers, not substrate.","Acetyl-CoA can feed other pathways, but no oxygen sensor redirects it, and that would not explain the cycle halting.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g059","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A newborn is diagnosed with Edwards syndrome. Which chromosomal abnormality accounts for it? In the choices below, T followed by a number means trisomy of that chromosome.","opts":["T13","A deletion on chromosome 18","T21","T18","XXY"],"correct":3,"why":"Edwards syndrome is three copies of chromosome 18. The fastest way to keep this set straight is to memorize the three numbers as a block, 21 for Down, 18 for Edwards, 13 for Patau, and then read the number in each choice rather than the name. The choice naming trisomy 18 is the one.","traps":["Trisomy 13 is Patau syndrome, the most severe of the three.","This has the right chromosome but the wrong mechanism, since Edwards syndrome is an extra whole copy of 18 rather than a missing segment of it.","Trisomy 21 is Down syndrome.",null,"The 47,XXY karyotype is Klinefelter syndrome, a sex chromosome condition rather than an autosomal trisomy."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-4nakyd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Both C4 and CAM plants keep carbon dioxide concentrated around RuBisCO. What separates the two strategies?","opts":["C4 plants close their stomata at night, CAM plants close theirs at noon","C4 skips RuBisCO entirely, while CAM still relies on it","C4 separates them by time of day, CAM separates them by place in the leaf","C4 separates the steps by place, CAM separates them by time","C4 plants use PEP carboxylase, CAM plants use RuBisCO alone"],"correct":3,"why":"Anchor each one to a picture. Corn is C4: two cell types side by side, so the separation is spatial. A cactus is CAM: one cell doing two jobs at different hours, so the separation is temporal, and cactus and clock both starting with C is a workable hook.","traps":["C4 plants open their stomata by day like most plants, and CAM plants keep theirs shut through the heat of the day.","Both pathways rely on RuBisCO for the actual Calvin cycle, and the extra enzyme only concentrates carbon first.","This is the reversal to watch for, since the cactus is the one working on a clock and the corn is the one working in two different cells.",null,"Both pathways use PEP carboxylase for that first capture."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Skeletal muscle develops from which germ layer?","opts":["Mesoderm","Endoderm","Multiple layers","Neural crest","Ectoderm"],"correct":0,"why":"Germ layer questions get much simpler once you hold one sentence for each layer: ectoderm makes the outer covering and the nervous system, endoderm makes the lining of the gut tube and the organs that bud from it, and mesoderm makes everything in the middle. Muscle of all three types, bone, blood, kidney and heart are all middle layer structures. Skeletal muscle in particular traces back to the myotome portion of the somites, which are blocks of paraxial mesoderm.","traps":[null,"Endoderm lines the gut tube and buds off its glands, so it makes the lining while the muscle wrapped around the gut comes from mesoderm.","Skeletal muscle has a single origin in mesoderm, specifically the myotome of the somites in the trunk and limbs.","Neural crest supplies pigment cells, sensory and autonomic neurons and much of the connective tissue of the head, but it does not produce muscle fibers.","Ectoderm makes the nervous system and the outer covering, so it supplies the nerve that commands the muscle rather than the muscle itself."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-pw61ua","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Cholesterol sits wedged between phospholipids in an animal cell membrane. At body temperature it reduces fluidity, and at low temperature it keeps the membrane from stiffening. The best description of its role is:","opts":["It seals the membrane so that water cannot cross","It buffers fluidity against temperature change","It makes the membrane permanently more rigid","It makes the membrane permanently more fluid","It anchors the membrane to the cytoskeleton beneath it"],"correct":1,"why":"Cholesterol does not have one direction of effect, it has one job: holding fluidity steady. A warm membrane gets too loose and cholesterol restrains the tails, a cold membrane gets too ordered and cholesterol wedges in to stop tight packing. If a choice says cholesterol only increases or only decreases fluidity, half the temperature story is missing.","traps":["Water still crosses the bilayer and moves through aquaporins, cholesterol does not seal it out.",null,"This captures only the warm-temperature half of the effect.","This captures only the cold-temperature half of the effect.","Anchoring is the job of membrane proteins that bind cytoskeletal filaments, not of cholesterol."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-jthhks","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"A cactus opens its stomata only after dark. How does it still fix carbon in daylight?","opts":["It runs the light reactions at night and the Calvin cycle during the day","It ships the night's carbon to neighboring bundle sheath cells for daytime use","It takes up carbon dioxide through its roots while the stomata are shut","It locks the carbon into organic acids overnight and releases it by day","It stores carbon dioxide as a gas in large vacuoles until morning"],"correct":3,"why":"CAM is a night shift for gas exchange. Stomata open in the cool dark, carbon dioxide gets locked into malic acid and parked in the vacuole, then by day the acid hands that carbon back to RuBisCO while the stomata stay shut. Same trick as C4, concentrate carbon dioxide near the enzyme, but staged in time instead of in space.","traps":["The light reactions need light, so they cannot run in the dark no matter how the carbon is stored.","Handing carbon to bundle sheath cells is the C4 solution, which separates the steps by place rather than by time.","Roots take up water and minerals, not the carbon dioxide used in photosynthesis.",null,"Gas cannot be warehoused that way, since the carbon has to be locked into a molecule first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-a1ldow","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Nitric oxide released by an endothelial cell reaches the smooth muscle of a blood vessel and relaxes it. Which feature of this signal is most unusual compared with a typical hormone?","opts":["It is a gas that crosses membranes directly and acts on an enzyme inside the cell","It acts as a transcription factor once it reaches the nucleus","It is stored in secretory vesicles and released slowly by exocytosis over several hours","It travels through the blood to reach distant target organs","It binds a G-protein-coupled receptor on the muscle cell surface"],"correct":0,"why":"Nitric oxide is a small uncharged gas, so it needs no surface receptor and no transporter: it diffuses straight through the membrane. Inside the muscle cell it activates guanylyl cyclase, cGMP rises, and the muscle relaxes so the vessel widens. It also breaks down within seconds, which is why its effect stays local.","traps":[null,"Nitric oxide works through cGMP rather than by binding DNA.","Nitric oxide is made on demand and breaks down in seconds rather than being stored.","It is far too short lived for long-distance travel, its action is local.","Its receptor is the intracellular enzyme guanylyl cyclase, not a receptor at the surface."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p049","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Acetylcholine released by parasympathetic postganglionic neurons acts on which receptors at the target organ?","opts":["GABA receptors","Adrenergic receptors","Muscarinic receptors","Nicotinic receptors","NMDA receptors"],"correct":2,"why":"Parasympathetic traffic runs on acetylcholine at both of its synapses, so the receptor name is what tells the two apart. At the target organ, meaning heart, gut, and glands, the receptor is muscarinic, a G protein-coupled receptor that slows the heart and steps up digestion. Tie each word to its source: muscarine comes from a mushroom and acts on organs, while nicotine acts at the ganglia and at skeletal muscle.","traps":["GABA receptors handle inhibition inside the central nervous system and have no role in autonomic output to organs.","Adrenergic receptors bind norepinephrine and epinephrine, which is the sympathetic side of the autonomic system.",null,"Nicotinic receptors do bind acetylcholine, but they sit at the autonomic ganglia and at the neuromuscular junction, one synapse earlier than the target organ.","NMDA receptors are glutamate receptors involved in central excitation and learning rather than in parasympathetic organ control."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-hd8ex8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A sprinting muscle cell is fermenting. What does it make from pyruvate, which enzyme does it, and is any gas released?","opts":["Acetaldehyde, by pyruvate decarboxylase, with CO2 released","Acetyl-CoA, by pyruvate dehydrogenase, with CO2 released","Lactate, by pyruvate decarboxylase, with CO2 released","Lactate, by lactate dehydrogenase, with no gas released","Ethanol, by alcohol dehydrogenase, with CO2 released"],"correct":3,"why":"Human muscle runs lactic acid fermentation: NADH hands its electrons straight to pyruvate, lactate dehydrogenase catalyzes the transfer, and NAD+ comes back ready for glycolysis. Nothing is decarboxylated, so no carbon dioxide comes off and lactate keeps all three of pyruvate's carbons. The carbon count is the cleanest way to tell the two fermentations apart: three carbons in and three carbons out means lactate.","traps":["Acetaldehyde is the intermediate of alcoholic fermentation, again a yeast and plant pathway.","This is aerobic pyruvate oxidation in the matrix, the pathway that shuts down when oxygen runs short.","The enzyme and the gas both belong to the alcoholic route, and making lactate removes no carbon at all.",null,"Ethanol is the yeast and plant route, and animal cells have no pyruvate decarboxylase to start it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-ndmaar","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A few thousand epinephrine molecules reaching a liver cell can release hundreds of millions of glucose molecules. The main reason is that:","opts":["Each step in the cascade is an enzyme that activates many copies of the next","The receptor is a channel that admits glucose from outside","The hormone is stored and then released again by the target cell","Each hormone molecule is used over and over again by a single receptor protein","The hormone enters the cell and binds DNA directly"],"correct":0,"why":"Amplification is the payoff of using enzymes as relay runners. One active G protein switches on an adenylyl cyclase that makes many cAMP molecules, each protein kinase A phosphorylates many targets, and the numbers multiply at every stage. When a question marvels at how small the input was, the answer is almost always the enzyme cascade.","traps":[null,"The glucose released comes from the cell's own glycogen stores, not from outside.","Storing and rereleasing a hormone does not multiply the size of the response.","Even repeated binding by one molecule gives nothing like a millionfold gain, the multiplication happens downstream.","Epinephrine is not lipid soluble and works through a surface receptor, and direct DNA binding would not amplify this way."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g021","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Which description best fits an individual with Turner syndrome (45,X)?","opts":["Phenotypically female, with one Barr body in each cell","Phenotypically female; sterile, short stature","Phenotypically female; tall","Phenotypically male","Phenotypically male; tall"],"correct":1,"why":"Turner syndrome is a single X with no second sex chromosome, and without a Y there is no signal to build male structures, so the individual is phenotypically female. The exam features worth carrying are short stature, non-functional ovaries with infertility, and a webbed neck. The rule underneath is that the Y, not the number of X chromosomes, decides male development.","traps":["A 45,X cell has only one X and that single X stays active, so there is no inactivated X to form a Barr body.",null,"Tall stature points toward the extra X of triple X or the extra chromosome of Klinefelter, while Turner syndrome is characteristically short.","Male development requires the Y chromosome, and a 45,X karyotype has none.","This combines two features that do not apply here, since there is no Y to direct male development and stature is reduced rather than increased."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p021","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Bile is produced by which organ?","opts":["Spleen","Gallbladder","Pancreas","Small intestine","Liver"],"correct":4,"why":"Two organs share the bile story and the exam checks which does which. The liver's hepatocytes make bile continuously, then the gallbladder stores and concentrates it and squeezes it out when a fatty meal arrives. Made in the liver, kept in the gallbladder, used in the small intestine.","traps":["The spleen recycles worn out red blood cells and releases bilirubin, which the liver then takes up and puts into bile.","The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile between meals, and storing is not the same as producing.","The pancreas sends digestive enzymes and bicarbonate into the same stretch of intestine, which is easy to blur with bile, but it does not make bile.","The small intestine is where bile does its work on fat, not where it is made.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p024","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which class of antibody crosses the placenta and gives a newborn passive immunity?","opts":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgD","IgE"],"correct":1,"why":"Match each antibody class to its job. IgG is the most abundant class in blood, the workhorse of the secondary response, and the one built to be carried across the placenta, so a newborn starts life protected by the mother's IgG. IgA is the class delivered after birth in breast milk, a different route for the same idea.","traps":["IgA guards mucous surfaces and reaches the infant through breast milk rather than through the placenta.",null,"IgM is the first antibody made in a new infection, and as a large pentamer it is far too bulky to cross the placenta.","IgD sits on the surface of naive B cells as part of their receptor and is barely present in circulation.","IgE binds mast cells and drives allergic and antiparasitic responses, and it stays with the mother."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e022","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Allopatric speciation begins with which of the following events?","opts":["Random mating","Polyploidy","Part of the population shifting onto a new host plant while every individual stays in the same area","Geographic barrier","Hybrid zone"],"correct":3,"why":"The name carries the answer: allo means other and patria means homeland, so the two populations end up in different places. A river shifts its course, a mountain range rises, or a few individuals reach an island, and gene flow between the groups stops. Once gene flow stops, drift and different local selection pressures push the two groups apart until they can no longer interbreed even if they meet again.","traps":["Random mating keeps a population genetically mixed, which is what prevents a split rather than what starts one.","Polyploidy creates a new species in one generation with no separation at all, which is the sympatric route instead.","A shift onto a new host while everyone remains in the same area is the classic sympatric scenario, with no geographic separation involved.",null,"A hybrid zone forms later, when separated populations come back into contact, so it is an outcome of the process rather than its trigger."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p099","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which set of structures makes up the brainstem?","opts":["Hypothalamus","Cerebrum","Medulla, pons, midbrain","Cerebellum","Thalamus, hypothalamus and epithalamus"],"correct":2,"why":"The brainstem is the stalk connecting the brain to the spinal cord, and it is built from three pieces stacked top to bottom: midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata. Everything automatic that keeps you alive without thinking about it, breathing rate, heart rate, blood pressure, is run from here. A quick check is whether a structure sits on the stalk itself rather than hanging off it or resting above it.","traps":["The hypothalamus belongs to the diencephalon and controls the pituitary and homeostasis, just above the brainstem rather than within it.","The cerebrum is the large upper brain handling thought, voluntary movement and sensation, sitting well above the brainstem.",null,"The cerebellum attaches behind the brainstem and coordinates balance and smooth movement, but it is a separate structure rather than part of the stalk.","Those three together form the diencephalon, which sits between the cerebrum and the brainstem."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-zfom9x","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"One Krebs cycle enzyme is not floating in the matrix. It is built into the inner mitochondrial membrane and doubles as a complex of the electron transport chain. Which reaction does it catalyze?","opts":["Succinyl-CoA to succinate","Isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate","Citrate to isocitrate","Succinate to fumarate","Malate to oxaloacetate"],"correct":3,"why":"The enzyme is succinate dehydrogenase, which is also complex II of the electron transport chain. Because it is wedged into the membrane, the electrons it strips off go onto FAD and straight into the chain rather than onto NAD+. That membrane address is the reason the cycle's only FADH2 comes from this one step.","traps":["This is the substrate-level phosphorylation that makes GTP, and it takes place in the matrix.","This is the rate-limiting decarboxylation, run by a matrix enzyme that reduces NAD+.","This isomerization belongs to aconitase, a soluble matrix enzyme that just relocates a hydroxyl group.",null,"This last oxidation reduces NAD+ in the matrix and regenerates oxaloacetate for the next turn."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1f9fdnq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"In linear electron flow, each electron is boosted once at photosystem II and again at photosystem I. What is the minimum number of photons absorbed per molecule of oxygen released?","opts":["Two","Twelve","Four","Six","Eight"],"correct":4,"why":"Build the number up from the electrons. One oxygen molecule comes from two waters, which release four electrons between them, and each of those four gets lifted twice, so four times two is eight. Any photon counting question on the light reactions starts by counting electrons first.","traps":["Two is the number of water molecules split, not the number of photons absorbed.","Twelve overshoots, because each electron takes two uphill jumps rather than three.","Four counts the electrons, or one boost each, and forgets the second excitation at photosystem I.","Six does not match any step in the count of electrons or of boosts.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1f42c5x","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"FADH2 yields less ATP than NADH does. Why?","opts":["It is made in the cytosol and must be shuttled into the matrix first","It is a weaker reducing agent because it carries no phosphate group","It enters the chain at complex II, so it drives fewer proton pumps","It carries only one electron, while NADH carries two","It hands its electrons straight to oxygen, skipping the chain"],"correct":2,"why":"The yield difference comes down to how many pumps the electrons pass on the way down. NADH is oxidized at complex I and its electrons drive pumping at I, III, and IV, while FADH2 joins at complex II and only drives III and IV. Fewer protons pumped means a smaller contribution to the gradient, which is why NADH is worth about 2.5 ATP and FADH2 about 1.5.","traps":["The FADH2 from the Krebs cycle is made inside the matrix, and shuttles are a separate issue affecting cytosolic NADH.","Neither carrier uses a phosphate group to hold electrons, so that is not the distinction.",null,"Both carriers deliver two electrons, so the electron count is not where they differ.","Nothing reaches oxygen without passing through complexes III and IV first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_el-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"An electroplating bath of CuSO4 deposits Cu via Cu^2+ + 2 e- -> Cu(s) at a constant current of 0.50 A. To deposit exactly 1.0 g of Cu (M = 63.5 g/mol) using F = 96485 C/mol, compute the time required from t = nFm/(IM) and convert to minutes.","opts":["100 min","50 min","200 min","25 min","400 min"],"correct":0,"why":"Moles Cu = 1.0/63.5 = 0.01575; moles e- = 0.0315; charge Q = 0.0315 x 96485 = 3039 C; t = Q/I = 3039/0.50 = 6078 s ~ 101 min ~ 100 min.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#wl7b3c","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 1.5 m aqueous solution of glucose? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-2.79 °C","-0.768 °C","2.79 °C","-4.19 °C","-5.58 °C"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (1)(1.86)(1.5) = 2.79 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 2.79 = -2.79 °C.","traps":[null,"You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","glucose is a molecular solute. It does not split into ions, so i = 1."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#14njfl4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH and a molar mass of 65 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["CH","C₄H₄","C₁₀H₁₀","C₅H₅","C₆H₆"],"correct":3,"why":"Empirical mass = 13. 65 / 13 = 5, so multiply every subscript by 5: C₅H₅.","traps":["That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier.","Multiplier is one too low. Compute the empirical formula mass carefully, then divide.","You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass.",null,"Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ph-e11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, 50.0 mL of 0.10 M HCl is mixed with 50.0 mL of 0.10 M NaOH in a beaker, producing 100.0 mL of solution containing only NaCl and water. Considering that HCl + NaOH -> NaCl + H2O fully neutralizes (equimolar strong acid + strong base), what is the final pH?","opts":["1.0","3.0","7.0","10","13"],"correct":2,"why":"Equal moles of strong acid and strong base produce a salt of a strong acid and strong base (NaCl), which is pH-neutral. The resulting solution is pure NaCl(aq) at pH = 7.0 at 25 °C.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_pd5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"On the CO2 phase diagram, the triple point lies at 5.11 atm and 216.6 K, while the critical point is at 73 atm and 304 K. A sample of CO2 is held at 1.00 atm and 25°C (298 K), conditions that fall well below the triple-point pressure but above its temperature. What phase is the CO2 in?","opts":["Gas","Supercritical fluid","Solid","Liquid","Mixture of solid and liquid"],"correct":0,"why":"At 1 atm (below 5.11 atm triple-point pressure) liquid CO2 is unstable, so the diagram shows only solid below the sublimation curve and gas above. At 298 K and 1 atm we are above the sublimation curve, so CO2 exists as a gas.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-e18","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, MW = 180.0 g/mol) is the primary blood sugar. A 36.0 g sample contains 12 H atoms per glucose molecule. Use n_H = (m / MW) × 12 to compute the moles of hydrogen.","opts":["0.40","1.2","2.4","4.8","12"],"correct":2,"why":"n_glucose = 36/180 = 0.200 mol; n_H = 0.200 × 12 = 2.40 mol. Forgetting the ×12 stoichiometry per molecule gives the 0.20 trap; mistakenly multiplying by 6 (for C₆) gives 1.2.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A laboratory report adds three independently measured volumes: 1.234 mL (4 sig figs), 5.6 mL (2 sig figs), and 12.34 mL (4 sig figs). The calculator returns 19.174. Apply the addition rule (smallest decimal places) to round the sum to its proper precision.","opts":["19","19.17","19.174","19.2","19.20"],"correct":3,"why":"Decimal places: 1.234 (3), 5.6 (1), 12.34 (2). The sum keeps the smallest = 1 decimal place. 19.174 rounds to 19.2.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:p-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Which has greater 1st IE: N or O?","opts":["N","Cannot tell","Neither, carbon is higher than both","O","Same"],"correct":0,"why":"N has half-filled 2p³ (extra stable). Removing an e⁻ from O's 2p⁴ (paired) is easier than from N's half-full.","traps":[null,"This one is settled, and the half filled 2p set is the reason nitrogen comes out on top.","Carbon comes in near 1086 kJ/mol, below both, so reading the period trend from right to left is what puts it on top.","The left to right trend does point here, and nitrogen's half filled 2p³ is unusually hard to disturb.","They are not equal: nitrogen comes in near 1402 kJ/mol and oxygen near 1314."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:bf6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A buffer is made from 0.20 M NH₃ + 0.20 M NH₄Cl (pKb of NH₃ = 4.74). What is the pH?","opts":["4.74","4.98","7.00","9.26","11.26"],"correct":3,"why":"For an NH₃/NH₄⁺ buffer: pH = pKa(NH₄⁺) + log([NH₃]/[NH₄⁺]). pKa(NH₄⁺) = 14 − pKb(NH₃) = 14 − 4.74 = 9.26. With equal concs: log(1) = 0. pH = 9.26.","traps":["That's pKb, not pKa.","That treats the solution as ammonium chloride alone, ignoring the ammonia that makes it a buffer.","Equal amounts of a base and its conjugate acid do not land at neutral, since the pH sits at the pKa of the ammonium ion.",null,"That is the pH of 0.20 M ammonia by itself, which ignores the ammonium ion already in the flask."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"Pure water at 25°C has vapor pressure P° = 23.8 torr. A NaCl solution is prepared so the mole fraction of solvent water is chi_solvent = 0.90 (treating NaCl as a non-volatile, non-dissociating solute for this calculation). Apply Raoult's law P_solution = chi_solvent · P° to find the new vapor pressure.","opts":["23.8 torr","2.4 torr","26.4 torr","19.0 torr","21.4 torr"],"correct":4,"why":"P_solution = 0.90 x 23.8 = 21.42 torr ~ 21.4 torr. The 2.4 torr drop is the colligative vapor-pressure lowering. (A real NaCl solution would dissociate into 2 ions per formula unit, doubling the apparent solute fraction.)","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Acetylene (H−C≡C−H) is the linear hydrocarbon used in welding torches that produces flames near 3100°C in oxygen. Counting the C≡C triple bond and both C−H bonds, how many π bonds are present in one molecule of acetylene?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Each triple bond consists of one σ + two π bonds, while each single bond is purely σ. Acetylene therefore has 3 σ bonds (2 C-H + 1 C-C σ) and 2 π bonds, both within the C≡C unit. The carbons are sp hybridized, leaving two perpendicular 2p orbitals to overlap into the two π bonds.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:gb-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A reaction is spontaneous at all T. Sign of ΔH and ΔS?","opts":["−H, +S","−H, with ΔS of either sign","+H, +S","−H, −S","+H, −S"],"correct":0,"why":"For ΔG always negative, need −H AND +S - both terms favor.","traps":[null,"A negative ΔH is half of it, and paired with a negative ΔS the reaction still quits once the temperature climbs.","Positive ΔH with positive ΔS only turns spontaneous once T is high enough for TΔS to win.","Negative ΔH with negative ΔS is spontaneous only at low T, where the entropy penalty stays small.","Positive ΔH with negative ΔS is never spontaneous, since both terms push ΔG up."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Use Hess's law to combine: (1) C(s) + ½O₂(g) → CO(g), ΔH₁ = −110 kJ/mol; (2) CO(g) + ½O₂(g) → CO₂(g), ΔH₂ = −283 kJ/mol. Adding (1) and (2) gives C(s) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g). What is ΔH for this overall reaction?","opts":["+393 kJ/mol","−110 kJ/mol","−283 kJ/mol","−173 kJ/mol","−393 kJ/mol"],"correct":4,"why":"Hess's law: ΔH_overall = ΔH₁ + ΔH₂ = (−110) + (−283) = −393 kJ/mol. Subtracting instead of adding gives −173; reversing one reaction's sign gives wrong sign. The two-step path equals the direct path because enthalpy is a state function.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#1xvu5bk","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻² M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["1","1.3","12","12.7","13"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻² = 1.0 × 10⁻¹ M. pOH = 1, pH = 14 − 1 = 13.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The chlorate ion ClO3- is drawn for a Lewis structure exercise. Cl contributes 7 valence electrons, each O contributes 6, and the -1 charge adds 1 more electron. How many total valence electrons must appear in the completed structure?","opts":["24","25","26","28","32"],"correct":2,"why":"Total valence e- = 7 (Cl) + 3 x 6 (O) + 1 (charge) = 7 + 18 + 1 = 26. Distribute these as bonds and lone pairs to satisfy octets (with formal-charge minimization on Cl).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:nr-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"For Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, [Cu²⁺] = 0.001 M, n = 2, E° = +0.34 V. Find E.","opts":["0.31 V","0.43 V","0.25 V","0.34 V","0.16 V"],"correct":2,"why":"Q = 1/[Cu²⁺] = 1000. E = 0.34 − (0.0296)(3) = 0.34 − 0.0888 ≈ 0.25 V.","traps":["0.31 V subtracts the 0.0296 term once and leaves out the log Q = 3 multiplier.","0.43 V flips the sign of the log term: Q is 1/[Cu²⁺] = 1000, so log Q is +3 and the correction comes off E°.",null,"0.34 V is the standard-state value, which only holds when Cu²⁺ sits at 1 M.","0.16 V uses the full 0.0592 instead of 0.0592 divided by the 2 electrons, so the correction is twice as large as it should be."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Naturally occurring chlorine consists of two isotopes: ³⁵Cl with mass 34.97 amu at 75.0% abundance and ³⁷Cl with mass 36.97 amu at 25.0% abundance. Using the weighted-average formula AW = Σ(fᵢ · mᵢ), what is the average atomic mass of Cl reported on the periodic table?","opts":["36.5 amu","35.5 amu","37.0 amu","35.0 amu","36.0 amu"],"correct":1,"why":"Average atomic mass = (0.750 × 35) + (0.250 × 37) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.5 amu. The 75/25 abundance ratio places Cl's average mass closer to 35 (the more abundant) but not exactly at 35.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gl-e11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A fixed sample of ideal gas initially has P1 = 2.0 atm, V1 = 10. L, and T1 = 300. K. The gas is then warmed and allowed to expand to a final volume V2 = 20. L at a final temperature T2 = 600. K. Using the combined gas law, what is the final pressure P2?","opts":["4.0 atm","3.0 atm","0.50 atm","2.0 atm","1.0 atm"],"correct":3,"why":"Combined gas law: P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2, so P2 = P1 x (V1/V2) x (T2/T1) = 2.0 x (10/20) x (600/300) = 2.0 atm. The volume doubles (halving P) but T doubles (doubling P), so P stays the same.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf006","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"For the combustion of methane: CH4(g) + 2 O2(g) → CO2(g) + 2 H2O(l), ΔH° = −890 kJ/mol and ΔS° = −243 J/(mol·K). At what temperature (approximate) does the spontaneity of the reaction reverse? (Assume ΔH° and ΔS° are temperature-independent.)","opts":["About 273 K (0°C)","About 3,660 K - far above any practical temperature","The reaction is never spontaneous at any temperature","About 27 K - only at cryogenic temperatures","The reaction is always spontaneous; spontaneity never reverses"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 0 when T = ΔH/ΔS = 890,000 / 243 ≈ 3663 K. Below this T, ΔG < 0 (spontaneous); above, ΔG > 0. In practice, methane combustion is always spontaneous at attainable temperatures.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rl-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction A -> products at 298 K has [A]_0 = 2.0 M and rate constant k = 0.10 s^(-1). Using the first-order half-life formula t_(1/2) = 0.693/k (which is independent of [A]_0), how long does it take for [A] to reach 1.0 M?","opts":["3.5 s","10 s","20 s","0.693 s","6.93 s"],"correct":4,"why":"t_(1/2) = ln(2)/k = 0.693/0.10 s^(-1) = 6.93 s. First-order half-life is independent of [A]_0. Confusing 1/k with the formula gives 10 s.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#qrgh47","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.0231 min⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["30 min","86.6 min","0.016 min","0.0333 min","43.3 min"],"correct":0,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.0231 = 30 min. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":[null,"You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.","You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath.","Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.","That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:at-e9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Average atomic mass of Cl if isotopes are ³⁵Cl (75%) and ³⁷Cl (25%)?","opts":["35.0","35.5","36.0","36.5","37.0"],"correct":1,"why":"Avg = 0.75(35) + 0.25(37) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.5.","traps":["35.0 takes the most abundant isotope on its own and skips the weighting.",null,"36.0 averages the two masses evenly, which would need a 50-50 split rather than 75 and 25.","Swapping the percentages, 25 percent of 35 plus 75 percent of 37, gives 36.5 and tilts the average toward the rarer isotope.","37.0 is the heavier isotope alone, and it makes up only a quarter of the sample."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = -120 kJ/mol and ΔS = -150 J/(K·mol). Spontaneity requires ΔG = ΔH - T·ΔS < 0. Set ΔG = 0 to find the crossover temperature, then state the temperature range over which the reaction is spontaneous.","opts":["Above 800 K only","Spontaneous at all T","Below 80 K","Below 120 K","Below 800 K"],"correct":4,"why":"Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = (-120,000 J)/(-150 J/K) = 800 K. Below 800 K, the favorable enthalpy dominates the unfavorable entropy. Above 800 K, T·|ΔS| exceeds |ΔH| and the reaction becomes nonspontaneous.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-e16","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Ethane combusts in air according to the balanced equation 2 C₂H₆(g) + 7 O₂(g) → 4 CO₂(g) + 6 H₂O(g). Starting with exactly 1.00 mol of C₂H₆, use the mole ratio of O₂ : C₂H₆ from the balanced equation to determine how many moles of O₂ are required.","opts":["1.0","2.0","3.0","3.5","7.0"],"correct":3,"why":"Stoichiometric ratio = 7 mol O₂ / 2 mol C₂H₆ = 3.5. So 1.00 mol C₂H₆ needs 1.00 × 3.5 = 3.5 mol O₂. Forgetting to halve the ratio gives the 7.0 trap; reading 7:2 inverted gives 3.0 or 2.0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A 1.00 m aqueous solution of KCl (which dissociates completely into K⁺ and Cl⁻, so van't Hoff factor i = 2) is prepared. Pure water freezes at 0.00°C with Kf = 1.86°C·kg/mol. Using ΔTf = i·Kf·m, what is the freezing point of this solution?","opts":["0.00°C","−3.72°C","−7.44°C","−1.86°C","−5.58°C"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔTf = (2)(1.86)(1.00) = 3.72°C. New Tf = 0.00 − 3.72 = −3.72°C. Forgetting i = 2 (treating KCl as a nonelectrolyte) gives −1.86°C; using i = 3 gives −5.58.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:x7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A 0.10 M weak acid HA has Ka = 1×10⁻⁴. % dissociation?","opts":["0.032%","0.32%","3.2%","10%","32%"],"correct":2,"why":"x = √(Ka·C) = √(10⁻⁵) = 3.16×10⁻³. %diss = (3.16×10⁻³ / 0.10)×100 = 3.16% ≈ 3.2%.","traps":["That is the fraction dissociated written as though it were already a percent, so the factor of one hundred never got applied.","That comes from dividing the 3.2×10⁻³ by 1.0 M instead of by the 0.10 M actually given.",null,"Ten percent comes from taking the square root of Ka alone and leaving the concentration out of the root.","Those digits are ten times too large, which is the root of 10⁻³ rather than 10⁻⁵."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1ldwwc0","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 1 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 127 °C and 4 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["131 L","2.61 L","12.3 L","831 L","8.21 L"],"correct":4,"why":"T = 127 + 273 = 400 K. V = nRT / P = (1)(0.0821)(400) / 4 = 8.21 L.","traps":["You multiplied by pressure. V = nRT / P, so pressure goes underneath.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lc4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"An equilibrium mixture of N2(g) + 3 H2(g) ⇌ 2 NH3(g) sits in a sealed 5.00 L vessel at 500 K. A chemist injects 1.0 mol of inert argon while keeping the volume constant at 5.00 L. Considering that K_p depends only on partial pressures of N2, H2, and NH3 (not on Ar), which way does the equilibrium shift?","opts":["Shifts forward (toward NH3)","No shift","Equilibrium dissociates","Shifts forward, then reverse","Shifts reverse (toward N2 + H2)"],"correct":1,"why":"Adding inert gas at constant V does not change the partial pressures or molar concentrations of N2, H2, or NH3, so Q is unchanged and Q = K still holds. Le Chatelier's principle predicts no shift. (At constant pressure, dilution would shift toward more moles of gas.)","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"originals:ab-ec59a41c94","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Blood is buffered by carbonic acid and bicarbonate. If a person hyperventilates and blows off a large amount of CO2, what happens to blood pH?","opts":["It does not change, because blood is buffered","It rises, because removing CO2 lowers carbonic acid","It falls, because CO2 is a base","It rises, because hyperventilating adds extra bicarbonate to the blood","It falls, because less bicarbonate is made"],"correct":1,"why":"CO2 + H2O is in equilibrium with H2CO3, which is in equilibrium with H+ and HCO3-. Blow off CO2 and the chain shifts toward CO2, which uses up H+. Fewer H+ means a higher pH. That is respiratory alkalosis.","traps":["Buffers limit changes. A large loss of CO2 still shifts the pH.",null,"CO2 forms carbonic ACID in water. Removing it removes acid.","Breathing faster does not add bicarbonate. Bicarbonate actually dips a little, because H+ and HCO3- combine to replace the CO2 that was lost. The pH rises because that shift uses up H+.","Losing CO2 pulls the equilibrium away from H+, which makes the blood less acidic, not more."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#xp7b40","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 51 g of NH₃? (molar mass 17 g/mol)","opts":["6.02 × 10²³","1.81 × 10²⁴","9","5.42 × 10²⁴","3.07 × 10²⁵"],"correct":3,"why":"51 g / 17 g/mol = 3 mol NH₃. Times 3 hydrogen per molecule = 9 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 5.42 × 10²⁴.","traps":["You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each NH₃ holds 3 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 3.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.",null,"You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-j7pele","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"cis-1-Bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane reacts with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC=CC1","C=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCCC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CCOC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"tBu locks equatorial; in the cis isomer Br is forced axial, the geometry E2 needs (anti-periplanar axial beta-H on C2/C6). Strong base on a secondary axial halide gives fast E2 to 4-tert-butylcyclohexene (option A). Alcohol/ether are minor substitution; exocyclic methylene cannot form; alkane is reduction.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](Br)CC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-146gkua","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene oxide (drawn) is treated with sodium azide (NaN3) in DMF. Which structure correctly shows the major product, including its stereochemistry?","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","OC1CCC(O)C1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","[N-]=[N+]=NC1CCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"Azide opens the epoxide by anti (backside) SN2, placing azide and OH trans: trans-2-azidocyclopentanol. C is a diol (no nitrogen), D has no hydroxyl (ring not opened to an alcohol), E is a six-membered ring (wrong ring size).","traps":null,"qs":"C1CC2OC2C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1mc2sbd","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Propanal (shown) is stirred with excess methanol and a trace of acid (HCl). Treating the aldehyde with two equivalents of alcohol under acid gives which product class?","opts":["CCC(O)OC","CCC(O)O","CCCO","CCC(OC)OC","CCC(=O)OC"],"correct":3,"why":"An aldehyde + 2 eq alcohol under acid forms an acetal with two OR groups on the original carbonyl carbon: CCC(OC)OC. CCC(O)OC is the hemiacetal (one equivalent, intermediate); CCC(=O)OC is an ester (wrong connectivity); CCC(O)O is the gem-diol hydrate; CCCO is a reduction product.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-11po0a5","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"(E)-Pent-3-en-2-one is treated with KCN (a soft nucleophile) and then aqueous acid. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CC(C#N)CC(C)=O","CCCC(C)=O","CCC(C#N)CC=O","C/C=C/C(C)(O)C#N","CC(=O)C(C)CC#N"],"correct":0,"why":"Cyanide is a soft nucleophile and adds 1,4. CN bonds to the beta carbon (C4, bearing the terminal CH3) giving CH3-CO-CH2-CH(CN)-CH3 = option 0 (verified: carbonyl->nitrile bond distance = 3, i.e. CN on beta; formula C6H9NO = stem + HCN). Option 1 is the 1,2-cyanohydrin (disfavored for soft CN, retains alkene). Option 2 is a wrong skeleton (CN at gamma/alpha-side). Option 3 is a rearranged aldehyde. Option 4 is simple reduction with no nitrile.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/C(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ykwv5m","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Acetanilide (N-phenylacetamide) is treated with Br2 in acetic acid. Which is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1Br","CC(=O)Nc1cccc(Br)c1","CC(=O)N(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc(Br)cc1","Nc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":3,"why":"The amide N lone pair donates into the ring, making NHCOCH3 an activating o/p director; para dominates (steric). Keyed option A = para, correct. Meta wrong director, N-bromination wrong site, free aniline requires hydrolysis (does not occur).","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-12wre4j","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Toluene (shown) is treated with Cl2 and UV light (hv). At which type of position does chlorination occur, and what is the name of this reaction?","opts":["Benzylic position; radical (free-radical) halogenation of the methyl C-H","Benzylic position; nucleophilic substitution (SN2)","Ring carbon (meta); electrophilic aromatic substitution","No reaction occurs because the aromatic ring is unreactive toward Cl2 without a Lewis acid catalyst","Ring carbon (ortho/para); electrophilic aromatic substitution promoted by the light"],"correct":0,"why":"Cl2 with light (no Lewis-acid catalyst) means radical conditions. The benzylic C-H is abstracted because the resulting benzylic radical is resonance-stabilized by the ring, giving benzyl chloride. Ring substitution (EAS) requires Cl2 with FeCl3/AlCl3, not light.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1s6ocho","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Aniline (drawn below) has its nitrogen attached directly to a benzene ring. Compared with the nitrogen in a simple sp3 alkylamine, the aniline nitrogen is best described as:","opts":["Purely sp and linear at nitrogen","sp3, with the lone pair fully delocalized into the aromatic ring the way pyridine's lone pair is","Purely sp3 and identical in geometry to a simple alkylamine","Closer to sp2/planar, because its lone pair partially conjugates with the aromatic ring","sp2, with the ring carbon bearing the nitrogen rehybridizing to sp3"],"correct":3,"why":"The aniline N lone pair conjugates (donates) into the ring, flattening the nitrogen toward planarity and giving it sp2 character relative to an sp3 amine, which weakens its basicity. It is not sp/linear, and the ipso carbon stays sp2. Distractor 4 confuses aniline (exocyclic donor N) with pyridine (ring N).","traps":null,"qs":"Nc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-uqdgqy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"1-Phenyl-1,2-epoxypropane (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. Which is the major product?","opts":["COC(c1ccccc1)C(C)O","COC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","OC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)Cc1ccccc1","COC(C)C(OC)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Both epoxide carbons are secondary. Under basic conditions the SN2-like attack goes to the less-hindered, non-benzylic CH(CH3); methoxide adds there and OH stays on the benzylic carbon: PhCH(OH)-CH(OCH3)CH3 = COC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1 (option A, connectivity verified). B is the acidic regiochemistry (attack at the benzylic/positive-stabilized carbon, verified), C is the diol, D double-substitutes, E has lost an oxygen.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1OC1c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-o828tv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (ethynylcyclohexane, drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","opts":["O=CCC1CCCCC1","OC=CC1CCCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","OCCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"This is a terminal alkyne (C#C-) bearing a cyclohexyl group; hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, so the carbonyl ends up on the terminal carbon, giving cyclohexylacetaldehyde (a two-carbon aldehyde side chain). The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov product; cyclohexanecarbaldehyde (ring-CHO) has lost a carbon and the wrong skeleton; the alcohol is over-reduction; the enol is the intermediate.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CC1CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-c74e4a1474","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"Which substituent in the para position makes a phenol the most acidic?","opts":["A hydrogen, meaning unsubstituted phenol","A nitro group (NO2)","A methoxy group (OCH3)","An amino group (NH2)","A methyl group (CH3)"],"correct":1,"why":"Ask what the substituent does to the phenoxide. A para nitro group pulls the negative charge all the way onto its own oxygens by resonance, so the anion is far more stable and the phenol is more acidic. Groups that push electron density in do the opposite.","traps":["An electron-withdrawing group beats no substituent.",null,"A methoxy group DONATES electron density by resonance, which makes the phenoxide less stable.","An amino group is a strong electron donor by resonance, which makes the phenol less acidic.","An alkyl group donates electron density, which makes the phenol slightly LESS acidic."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-1dfn5t3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"The tertiary amide shown is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","opts":["OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","CN(C)c1ccccc1","CN(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide to the amine (C=O becomes CH2, nitrogen retained, no C-N cleavage), so N,N-dimethylbenzamide gives N,N-dimethylbenzylamine (CN(C)Cc1ccccc1). No benzyl alcohol/benzaldehyde (C-N not cleaved), the carbonyl carbon is not lost, and the hemiaminal is not isolable.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(N(C)C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-jfeiyo","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"You must convert 1-nitro-4-propylbenzene (shown) into 4-aminobenzoic acid. Which sequence of reagents accomplishes BOTH the side-chain and the nitro-group transformations in a workable order?","opts":["(1) H2/Pd to reduce NO2 to NH2; then (2) HNO3/H2SO4 to turn the propyl chain into the carboxylic acid","(1) Sn/HCl, then base, to make the NH2 group first; then (2) hot KMnO4 with an H3O+ workup on the propyl chain","(1) Sn/HCl only (it also oxidizes the propyl chain to COOH)","(1) hot KMnO4 only (it also reduces the nitro group)","(1) hot KMnO4, H3O+ to make the COOH; then (2) Sn/HCl, then base to reduce NO2 to NH2"],"correct":4,"why":"Two changes: propyl (has benzylic H) is oxidized to COOH by hot KMnO4, and nitro is reduced to NH2 by Sn/HCl. Oxidize first, since hot KMnO4 would destroy/oxidize an amine; then reduce the surviving nitro. No single reagent does both.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"own-organic:ace-0010","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2 in dichloromethane. Where do the two bromines end up?","opts":["On opposite faces of the ring","On opposite faces only if the flask is heated","On the same face of the ring","One bromine adds and the other stays in solution","Both attached to the same carbon"],"correct":0,"why":"The first bromine closes into a three-membered bridge across one face of the ring, so the only face still open when the second bromine arrives is the other one. Anti addition is not a rule to memorize, it is what the bridge leaves room for.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCCC1","os":false,"src":"own-organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-fe4b8d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)=C","CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","C=CC(C)C"],"correct":2,"why":"Tertiary halide + bulky strong base (tert-butoxide) gives E2 with Hofmann selectivity (less-substituted, more accessible beta-H), yielding 2-methyl-1-butene. The Zaitsev alkene 2-methyl-2-butene is disfavored by the bulky base; bulky ether/tertiary alcohol are substitution products; last is wrong skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC(C)(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-2335q9","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then Zn/AcOH (reductive workup). Which structure is the product?","opts":["CC1CCCCC1=O","O=CCCCCC=O","CC(O)CCCCCO","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CCCCC=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the endocyclic C=C and OPENS the ring to an acyclic chain. Reductive (Zn/AcOH) workup makes the more-substituted (methyl-bearing) carbon a methyl ketone and the CH carbon an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (CC(=O)CCCCC=O, C7H12O2). Option 2 omits the methyl, option 3 is oxidative over-oxidation to an acid, option 4 is a diol, and option 5 wrongly keeps the ring intact (impossible for an endocyclic alkene). Index 0 correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-pnq96k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"trans-3-Hexene is subjected to ozonolysis with an OXIDATIVE workup (O3, then H2O2). What are the organic products?","opts":["CCC=O","CC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"Cleavage of the symmetric internal alkene gives two identical 3-carbon CH fragments; oxidative (H2O2) workup carries each aldehyde on to a carboxylic acid, giving two equivalents of propanoic acid. Propanal is the reductive-workup answer; the E geometry is irrelevant since the sp2 carbons are destroyed.","traps":null,"qs":"CC/C=C/CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-nymgh3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is treated with excess acetaldehyde and NaOH, then heated. Acetaldehyde is in excess so it provides the enolate. Which structure is the major crossed product, and what governs its alkene geometry?","opts":["O=C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC=O","O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=CCCc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Benzaldehyde (no alpha H) is the electrophile; the acetaldehyde enolate is the nucleophile. option B minor Z, option C the beta-hydroxy addition product, option D (phenylacetaldehyde)/option E (3-phenylpropanal) wrong connectivity. Geometry rationale is qualitative (conjugation/sterics), not FMO. Keyed answer correct.","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1op9odj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"In a Gabriel synthesis using potassium phthalimide (shown) followed by hydrazinolysis, which alkyl halide will give the highest yield of the desired primary amine?","opts":["2-chloro-2-methylbutane (a tertiary halide)","2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)","vinyl bromide (a vinyl halide)","1-bromobutane (a primary alkyl halide)","bromobenzene (an aryl halide)"],"correct":3,"why":"The phthalimide anion alkylates via SN2, so it needs an unhindered substrate; 1-bromobutane (primary) gives the best yield. Tertiary halides undergo E2 elimination, and aryl/vinyl halides are unreactive toward SN2.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1c2ccccc2C(=O)[N-]1.[K+]","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1ii13f","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction sequence: the alkene shown is treated with (1) O3, then (2) (CH3)2S (dimethyl sulfide). Which set of carbonyl products forms?","opts":["CC=O.CCC=O","CC(C)=O.CC=O","CC(C)O.CCO","CC(=O)O.CC(C)=O","CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C. Reductive workup ((CH3)2S) stops at carbonyls. The trisubstituted carbon becomes acetone and the CH3CH= carbon becomes acetaldehyde, so CC(C)=O.CC=O is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC=C(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1lfvsj4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Salicylaldehyde (shown) has an intramolecular hydrogen bond between its phenol O-H and the adjacent aldehyde oxygen. Compared with the IR of a simple phenol, what is the expected effect of this intramolecular hydrogen bonding on the O-H stretch?","opts":["The O-H stretch disappears entirely from the spectrum once the internal hydrogen bond forms","The O-H stretch is broadened and shifted to LOWER wavenumber (below ~3200 cm-1)","The O-H stretch sharpens and shifts to HIGHER wavenumber (above 3600 cm-1)","The O-H stretch becomes a sharp doublet, much like the two N-H bands of a primary aromatic amine","The O-H band moves to ~2250 cm-1, overlapping the nitrile region"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydrogen bonding weakens the O-H bond, lowering its frequency and broadening the band. The intramolecular H-bond in salicylaldehyde persists even when dilute, so O-H is broad and shifted to lower wavenumber vs a free O-H (~3600).","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-vqku6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"The molecule drawn is cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane. Which single statement BEST classifies this molecule with respect to chirality and optical activity?","opts":["It is a meso compound: it has stereocenters but an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and optically inactive","It is chiral and optically active, existing as a pair of non-superimposable enantiomers that each rotate plane-polarized light in opposite directions","It is a constitutional isomer of 1,4-dichlorocyclohexane and is optically active","It exists only as a single non-superimposable mirror image, so it has no achiral stereoisomer at all","It has no stereocenters at all, so chirality is not possible"],"correct":0,"why":"So cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane is meso, achiral, optically inactive - option A correct. option C wrongly denies stereocenters; option D confuses constitutional vs stereoisomerism.","traps":null,"qs":"C1C[C@H](Cl)C[C@H](Cl)C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1khayk8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A single enantiomer of 2-phenylpropanoic acid (stereocenter shown) is heated with methanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which structure is the major product, accounting for the stereochemistry at the alpha carbon?","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Fischer esterification cleaves the acyl carbon-oxygen bond of the acid; the bond to the alpha stereocenter is never broken, so its spatial configuration is fully retained. The inversion structure (B) is wrong because no bond to the stereocenter is broken; (C) uses ethanol; (D) loses the stereochemistry; (E) is a chain-extended skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-sm043k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (drawn), then NaOH/H2O, then H3O+/heat. What is the major product of this intramolecular dialkylation?","opts":["OC(=O)C1(C(=O)O)CCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","OC(=O)C1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Both alpha-C-H positions are alkylated by the two ends of the dibromide, closing a ring; malonate carbon plus four CH2 units form a five-membered ring; after hydrolysis/decarboxylation one COOH remains = cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. opt[0] matches. Distractors: cyclobutane (miscount), cyclohexane (extra carbon), gem-diacid (no decarboxylation), open-chain adipic acid (no cyclization).","traps":null,"qs":"BrCCCCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-b7gq59","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated sequentially: (1) NaOEt then CH3I; (2) NaOEt then CH3CH2I; then (3) dilute H2SO4 and heat. What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)C","CCC(CC)C(C)=O","CCC(C)C(=O)CC(C)=O","CCC(C)C(C)=O","CCCCC(C)=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Sequential methylation then ethylation puts a methyl and an ethyl on the central carbon; hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH(CH3)-CH2CH3 = 3-methyl-2-pentanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: 2-hexanone (one alkylation), non-decarboxylated diketone, 3-ethyl-2-pentanone (wrong group, two ethyls), and 4-methyl-2-pentanone (wrong substitution position).","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1nqrfbl","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The alkene shown (1-ethylcyclohexene) is treated with BH3-THF followed by H2O2/NaOH. Considering BOTH regiochemistry (anti-Markovnikov) and stereochemistry (syn addition), which description matches the major product?","opts":["The ethyl group is oxidized to a carboxylic acid","OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon (the CH), and H and OH add to the same face (syn), giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol","OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon, but H and OH add to opposite faces of the ring (anti), giving cis-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol","OH adds to the more substituted ring carbon that bears the ethyl group (Markovnikov), with H and OH delivered to the same face of the ring","Two OH groups add cis across the double bond"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted ring CH, not the carbon bearing the ethyl) and syn (H and OH add to the same face). On the ring, syn addition of H and OH places the new OH and the boron-derived H on the same face, which makes the OH trans to the ethyl substituent, giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC1=CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-163l4so","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"The keto-aldehyde 4-oxopentanal (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CHO, drawn) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C. Which carbonyl is reduced preferentially, and what is the reason?","opts":["Both are reduced equally; NaBH4 shows no selectivity","Neither is reduced; NaBH4 only reduces esters","The aldehyde is reduced first because aldehydes are more electrophilic and less sterically hindered than ketones","The aldehyde is reduced first because its carbonyl carbon is the more sterically hindered of the two and binds borohydride more tightly","The ketone is reduced first, because the two alkyl groups on a ketone make its carbonyl carbon more electrophilic than an aldehyde carbon"],"correct":2,"why":"Aldehydes are both more electrophilic (one electron-donating alkyl group vs. two for the ketone) and less sterically hindered, so with a limited amount of NaBH4 the aldehyde is reduced selectively to give the hydroxy-ketone. Ketones are less, not more, electrophilic. The reasoning in the steric distractor is reversed.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1rhxjv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3CH2Br; (3) H2, Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the final major product?","opts":["CC/C=C/CCCC","C/C=C\\CCCCC","CC/C=C\\CCCC","CCC#CCCCC","CCCCCCCC"],"correct":2,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the C6 acetylide with ethyl (C2) to give 3-octyne; step 3 (Lindlar) does syn reduction to cis (Z)-3-octene. Trans needs Na/NH3; octane is over-reduced; [3] is the alkyne (skipped Lindlar); [4] is cis-2-octene (wrong position).","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ym5nrn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Propene is treated with HBr in the presence of organic peroxides (ROOR). What is the major product?","opts":["CC(Br)C","C=CCBr","CC(Br)CBr","CCCO","CCCBr"],"correct":4,"why":"HBr/peroxides is a radical chain (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to the less substituted carbon (forms the more stable secondary radical), so Br ends on the terminal carbon: 1-bromopropane (opt[0]). opt[1] is the Markovnikov product (ionic HBr), opt[2] is the Br2 dibromide, opt[3] is allyl bromide (allylic substitution), opt[4] is an alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17oj8iy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"3-Methylpent-1-ene reacts with HCl. Account for Markovnikov addition and any hydride shift. Which is the major product, and what stereochemistry is expected at the new C-Cl center?","opts":["CCC(C)CCCl","CCC(Cl)CC","CCC(C)C(C)Cl","CCCC(C)(Cl)C","CCC(Cl)(C)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov gives a secondary cation at C2; a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 (tertiary C-H) makes a tertiary cation at C3. Chloride adds to give 3-chloro-3-methylpentane (option A), whose central carbon bears two ethyls, a methyl, and Cl, so it is not a stereocenter (the stereochem part of the prompt resolves to no stereocenter). option B is unrearranged, option C is anti-Markovnikov, option D is a wrong-skeleton C6 isomer; option E is C5H11Cl (missing a carbon). Single correct answer.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1k54mp1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"(S)-3-chloro-3-methylhexane (drawn, a tertiary chloride) is stirred in methanol (CH3OH) at room temperature. Which structure best represents the substitution product?","opts":["CCC[C@](C)(CC)OC","CCCC(C)(O)CC","CCCC(C)(CC)OC","CC=C(C)CCC","CCC[C@@](C)(CC)OC"],"correct":2,"why":"Tertiary substrate + weak neutral nucleophile (MeOH) + polar protic = SN1 via planar carbocation, attacked from both faces = racemic. option D is the alcohol (wrong nucleophile), option E an elimination alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC[C@@](C)(Cl)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#af82js","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (2 × 10¹)(6 × 10⁵) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["1.2 × 10⁵","1.2 × 10⁶","1.2 × 10⁷","12 × 10⁶","8 × 10⁷"],"correct":2,"why":"2 × 6 = 12 and 10¹ × 10⁵ = 10⁶. 12 × 10⁶ is not proper form, so write 1.2 × 10⁷. Answer: 1.2 × 10⁷.","traps":["The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.",null,"Right size, wrong form. In proper scientific notation the front number sits between 1 and 10.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1107","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A race route is 37.8 kilometers long, and a runner has covered 15.6 kilometers of it. How many kilometers are left?","opts":["2.22","22.2","22.3","53.4","222"],"correct":1,"why":"Stack the numbers with the decimal points in line and subtract: 8 tenths minus 6 tenths is 2 tenths, then 37 minus 15 is 22, giving 22.2. Rounding to 38 minus 16 gives about 22, so the size checks out.","traps":["You placed the point one spot too far left; 37.8 and 15.6 differ by more than 22, not by about 2.",null,"You slipped in the tenths column; 8 tenths minus 6 tenths is 2 tenths.","You added the two numbers instead of subtracting them.","You dropped the decimal point entirely, which scales the answer up by ten."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1715","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A rack holds 3^2 trays and each tray holds 3^1 samples. How many samples fit in the rack?","opts":["3","9","12","27","729"],"correct":3,"why":"Same base × adds exponents: 3^(2+1) = 3^3 = 27.","traps":["You subtracted the exponents, which is what division does.","You multiplied the exponents, so you stayed at 3^2.","You added the two values, 9 and 3, instead of multiplying them.",null,"You multiplied the bases as well as adding the exponents, which gives 9^3."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1053","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A crew used 2/4 of a can of stain to cover 3/7 of a deck. What fraction of the deck would a full can cover?","opts":["5/28","6/11","7/6","6/28","6/7"],"correct":4,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 3/7 times 4/2 gives 12/14. Both numbers are even, so in lowest terms that is 6/7. A quick sanity check is that dividing by something smaller than 1 has to make the answer bigger, and 6/7 is indeed above 3/7.","traps":["You added the tops (3 + 2) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (7 + 4).","That flips the first fraction instead of the second. The one being divided by is the one that turns over.","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1058","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"One beaker holds 4/12 of a liter and a second holds 15/12 of a liter. The first amount is what fraction of the second?","opts":["19/144","60/24","12/5","60/144","4/15"],"correct":4,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 4/12 times 12/15. The two 12s cancel on sight, which leaves 4/15 without any heavy arithmetic. Cancelling before you multiply is what keeps numbers like 180 off your page.","traps":["You added the tops (4 + 15) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (12 + 12).","That flips the first fraction instead of the second. Check the size: dividing by something bigger than one gives an answer smaller than what you started with.","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#x565wy","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $50 item is marked UP by 25% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 50%. What is the final price?","opts":["$25.00","$31.25","$37.50","$46.88","$62.50"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 50 × 1.25 × 0.5 = $31.25. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1739","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"A thermostat is set to 10 degrees, and the room temperature of x degrees is exactly 4 degrees away from that setting. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["6 or -14","14","14 or 6","-6 or 6","4 or -4"],"correct":2,"why":"|x − 10| = 4 means x − 10 = ±4, so x = 14 or 6.","traps":["That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","You solved x - 10 = 4 and stopped, and the branch x - 10 = -4 gives 6 as well.",null,"That pairs 6 with its negative, and the other branch is 10 plus 4, which is 14.","You set x equal to plus or minus 4 and never undid the minus 10 inside the bars."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#4jxc6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 72 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["259 m/s","72000 m/s","20 m/s","0.02 m/s","1200 m/s"],"correct":2,"why":"72 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 20 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":["Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.",null,"That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1133","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"What is 35 percent of 225?","opts":["7.88","78.75","146.25","260","7875"],"correct":1,"why":"35% of 225 = 35/100 × 225 = 78.75.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 65 percent left over rather than the 35 percent the question asks for.","You added the 35 to the 225 instead of taking 35 percent of it.","You multiplied 35 by 225 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1024","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A commute takes 2/5 of an hour by bus and 1/4 of an hour on foot. How many hours is the whole commute?","opts":["13/20","9/10","12/20","3/9","2/20"],"correct":0,"why":"LCD = 20. 2/5 = 8/20, 1/4 = 5/20. Combine: 13/20 = 13/20.","traps":[null,"You rescaled 1/4 as 10/20, which is a half; a quarter is 5/20.","You scaled 1/4 by 4 instead of by 5; a quarter is 5/20.","You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 3 over 9.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1161","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An $89 pair of shoes is discounted 22 percent. What is the sale price?","opts":["19.58","67","69.42","108.58","111"],"correct":2,"why":"New = 89 × (1 - 22/100) = 89 × 0.78 = 69.42.","traps":["That is 22 percent of 89, the size of the discount rather than the price left after it.","You subtracted 22 dollars instead of 22 percent, and 22 percent of 89 is 19.58.",null,"That puts 22 percent on instead of taking it off.","You added 22 dollars, and the problem takes 22 percent off."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp003","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A bag contains 5 red marbles and 7 blue marbles. Three marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that exactly 2 are red and 1 is blue?","opts":["15/44","7/12","7/22","5/12","21/44"],"correct":2,"why":"Use combinations: P = C(5,2) · C(7,1) / C(12,3) = (10)(7) / 220 = 70/220 = 7/22.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1700","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"On a mapping grid, a straight cable runs between (-9,-12) and (-16,-14). What is its slope, to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["-9","-0.29","0.08","0.29","3.5"],"correct":3,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -2/-7 = 0.29.","traps":["-9 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them.","-0.29 keeps one negative and drops the other, and two negatives divided give a positive.","0.08 comes from writing -16 minus 9 for the run instead of -16 minus -9, so the run lands at -25.",null,"3.5 divides the run by the rise, -7 over -2, which is the slope upside down."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1210","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A car odometer reads 1,906 miles at the start of a trip that covers 44 miles. What does it read at the end?","opts":["1862","1890","1940","1950","1990"],"correct":3,"why":"Work it out: 1906 + 44 = 1950. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 1906 minus 44, so the plus got read as a minus.","That is below 1906, and adding 44 has to move the total up.","That is 1900 plus 40, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 1,950.",null,"That adds about 85, nearly double the 44 on the page."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1nphbtr","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"In triangle ABC, angle A = 40°, side a = 6, and side b = 8. Using sin 40° = 0.643, what is sin B?","opts":["0.482","0.643","0.766","0.802","0.857"],"correct":4,"why":"The Law of Sines rearranges to sin B equals b times sin A divided by a, so it is 8 times 0.643 divided by 6, which is about 0.857. Because side b is longer than side a, angle B has to be the larger angle, so sin B should land above sin 40 rather than below it. That size check catches the most common version of this slip immediately.","traps":["This puts the sides in the wrong spots, computing 6 times sin 40 divided by 8.","This reuses sin 40 without scaling by the ratio of the sides.","This is cos 40 rather than any sine.","This scales the angle itself by 8 over 6 and then takes the sine, but the Law of Sines scales the sines, not the angles.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:g2d5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A square has side 4. If you double the side, the area becomes…","opts":["×4","×8","×2","×64","×16"],"correct":0,"why":"Area scales as length squared. Double the side, so area × 2² = ×4.","traps":[null,"That's cubed, for volume.","Linear scaling, but area is squared.","That is the new area in square units, not the factor the area grew by.","Raised the factor of 2 to the fourth power, scaling the figure twice over."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1356","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A 44-gram sample is separated in a ratio of 2 to 9. How many grams are in the smaller portion?","opts":["4","8","11","22","36"],"correct":1,"why":"Total parts = 11; one part = 44/11 = 4. Smaller = 2·4 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1b1xn3t","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$2000 is invested at 5% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 2 years?","opts":["$205.00","$2100.00","$2200.00","$2205.00","$2315.25"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 2000 × (1.05)² = $2205.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1184","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A monthly utility bill of $125 rises 10%, then falls 25% from that higher amount. What is the bill in dollars, to the nearest cent?","opts":["93.75","103.13","106.25","137.5","171.88"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply factors: 125 × 1.1 × 0.75 = 103.13. Naïvely adding gives 106.25, which is wrong.","traps":["That applies only the 25 percent cut and leaves the rise out.",null,"You added the two changes into a net 15 percent drop, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That is the price after the 10 percent rise, one step short of the answer.","That raises the second step by 25 percent instead of cutting by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1458","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A museum floor is mapped on a grid, with one exhibit at (-8, 7) and another at (0, 12). In a straight line, about how far apart are they?","opts":["3.61","8","9.43","13","89"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((8)² + (5)²) = √89 = 9.43.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#18ntb5a","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 180 miles at 30 mph and returns the same 180 miles at 60 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["52.5 mph","42.4 mph","20 mph","45 mph","40 mph"],"correct":4,"why":"Time out = 180/30 = 6 h, time back = 180/60 = 3 h. Average = 360 / 9 = 40 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1392","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One assistant can stock a supply room in 5 hours and another can stock it in 8 hours. Working together, how many hours does the job take?","opts":["3.08","5","6.15","6.5","13"],"correct":0,"why":"1/T = 1/5 + 1/8 = 13/40. T = 40/13 = 3.08 hr.","traps":[null,"Reporting the faster worker's solo time; the help pulls it lower still.","Using 2ab/(a+b), the average speed shortcut, where shared work calls for ab/(a+b).","Averaging the two times instead of combining the rates.","Adding the two times; together they finish sooner than either one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1718","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"What is the value of 2^1 ÷ 2^1?","opts":["2","4","1/2","0","1"],"correct":4,"why":"Same base ÷ subtracts: 2^(1-1) = 2^0 = 1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lem5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies 4x + 7 = 2(x − 3) + 19?","opts":["x = 0","x = 6","x = 4.5","x = 9","x = 3"],"correct":4,"why":"Distribute right: 4x + 7 = 2x − 6 + 19 = 2x + 13. Subtract 2x: 2x + 7 = 13. Subtract 7: 2x = 6. x = 3.","traps":["Substituting 0 leaves 7 on the left and 2(−3) + 19 = 13 on the right.","That is the 6 from 2x = 6, so the last divide by 2 never happened.","2(x − 3) came out as 2x − 3, but 2 times −3 is −6, and that extra 3 pushes the answer to 4.5.","The −6 was folded in as +6, making the right side 2x + 25.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1282","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A crate holds 4 large vials and 8 small vials. How many vials are in 5 such crates?","opts":["12","28","55","60","65"],"correct":3,"why":"Distribute: 5·4 + 5·8 = 20 + 40 = 60.","traps":["That is just the 4 + 8 inside the parentheses, so the 5 outside never got multiplied in.","Distributing the 5 only to the 4 gives 20 + 8 = 28, leaving the 8 unmultiplied.","That is 5 times 11, so the sum inside came out one short of 4 + 8 = 12.",null,"Adding the outside 5 after multiplying counts it twice, since 5 times 12 is already 60."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pco1557","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Disease prevalence 10%, test sensitivity 95%, specificity 80%. P(disease | positive test)?","opts":["0.1","0.35","0.65","0.8","0.95"],"correct":1,"why":"Build the denominator before reaching for the formula. Out of a thousand people, a hundred have the disease and ninety five of those test positive, while nine hundred do not and a hundred and eighty of those test positive anyway. That is two hundred and seventy five positives, of which ninety five are real, so the answer is 0.345, which is 0.35. A test that sounds accurate still misses badly when the disease is rare, because the healthy group is so much larger.","traps":["0.1 is the prevalence, the chance of disease before any test result.",null,"0.65 is the share of positives that are false alarms, 0.18 over 0.275, the complement of the answer.","0.8 is the specificity, a fact about people who do not have the disease.","0.95 is the sensitivity, the chance of a positive when the disease is there, which is this question reversed."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1078","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Convert 1/12 to a decimal (3 dp).","opts":["0.083","0.12","0.25","0.333","0.5"],"correct":0,"why":"1/12 = 0.083. Long-divide if you don't have it memorized.","traps":[null,"You read the 12 straight into the decimal instead of dividing 1 by 12.","That is 1/4, three times the size of a twelfth.","That is 1/3, four times the size of a twelfth.","That is 1/2, which would need the bottom number to be 2."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1206","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A rail line carries 2,460 riders a day. How many riders is that over 51 days?","opts":["123000","124000","125000","125460","126000"],"correct":3,"why":"Work it out: 2460 × 51 = 125460. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 2460 times 50, which leaves one more 2,460 to add.","That sits 1,460 under the product, so part of the multiplication went missing.","That is 2500 times 50, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 125,460.",null,"That rounds both numbers up, landing 540 over the true product."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#3tuzdz","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 3 red and 3 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["1/2","1/6","2/5","1/5","1/4"],"correct":3,"why":"First red: 3/6. Second red: 2/5. Multiply: 1/5.","traps":["That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble.","That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.",null,"That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1476","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A shift lasts 3.6 hours. How many minutes is that?","opts":["0.06","21.6","63.6","186","216"],"correct":4,"why":"1 hr = 60 min, so 3.6 hr × 60 = 216 min.","traps":["You divided the 3.6 by 60 instead of multiplying; minutes are smaller than hours, so the count grows.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You added 60 to the 3.6 instead of multiplying the 3.6 by it.","You read 3.6 hours as 3 hours and 6 minutes; the 0.6 is six tenths of an hour, which is 36 minutes.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#y9j3s6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 7 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 3rd place be awarded?","opts":["6","21","35","210","343"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 7P3 = 7 × 6 × 5 = 210.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 3 people. You still have to choose them from 7.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#skajfl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² − 5x + 4 = 0?","opts":["4","−4","3","−5","5"],"correct":4,"why":"It factors as (x − 1)(x − 4), so the roots are 1 and 4. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 5, product = c/a = 4.","traps":["That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum.","Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-mixture#2r0aa","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"4 L of a 5% acid solution is mixed with 1 L of a 35% acid solution. What is the concentration of the mixture?","opts":["11%","13.8%","20%","29%","55%"],"correct":0,"why":"Acid: 5% of 4 plus 35% of 1 = 0.55 L. Total volume = 5 L. Concentration = 11%.","traps":[null,"You divided by the product of the volumes. Divide by the SUM: total volume.","You averaged the two percents. The mixture leans toward whichever solution you used MORE of, so weight by volume.","You swapped the volumes. Pair each concentration with its own volume.","You found the total acid but never divided by the total volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1659","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Three tent poles measure 17, 14, and 3 units. Can they be joined end to end to form a triangle?","opts":["No","Only if right triangle","Only if it is obtuse","Yes","Cannot determine"],"correct":0,"why":"|17−14| = 3 and 17+14 = 31. 3 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":[null,"There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 17, tying it rather than beating it.","There is no corner to open: 14 and 3 lie flat along the 17 rather than rising above it.","14 and 3 add to exactly 17, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it.","The three lengths settle it: 14 plus 3 equals 17, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1285","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"Each of 4 clinics received 5 crates of supplies and returned 2 of them. How many crates did the clinics keep in total?","opts":["3","8","12","16","18"],"correct":2,"why":"Distribute: 4·5 - 4·2 = 20 - 8 = 12.","traps":["That is just the 5 - 2 inside the parentheses, so the 4 outside never got multiplied in.","That is 4 times 2 on its own, so the 4 times 5 piece got dropped.",null,"Only a single 4 came off the 20 instead of 4 times 2, which leaves 16.","Distributing the 4 only to the 5 gives 20 - 2 = 18, leaving the 2 unmultiplied."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1397","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"One student can enter a stack of survey forms in 5 hours and another can enter the same stack in 4 hours. Working together, how many hours does the stack take?","opts":["0.45","2.22","4","4.5","9"],"correct":1,"why":"1/T = 1/5 + 1/4 = 9/20. T = 20/9 = 2.22 hr.","traps":["Stopping at the combined rate of 9/20 of the job per hour instead of flipping it to get the hours.",null,"Reporting the faster worker's solo time; the help pulls it lower still.","Averaging the two times instead of combining the rates.","Adding the two times; together they finish sooner than either one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#139t9q","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 60 and standard deviation 5. About what percent of scores fall above 65?","opts":["16%","32%","34%","68%","84%"],"correct":0,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 16%.","traps":[null,"That is BOTH tails together. Only the upper tail is asked for: halve it.","That is the slice between the mean and one SD. The question asks for the TAIL beyond it.","That is the middle band within one SD. The tail is what is left over, split in two.","That is everything BELOW the cutoff. The question asks for above."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1695","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A drainage run on a plan goes from (3,-2) to (6,-4). What is its slope, to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["-1.5","-0.67","-0.22","0.67","1"],"correct":1,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -2/3 = -0.67.","traps":["-1.5 divides the run by the rise, 3 over -2, which is the slope upside down.",null,"-0.22 adds the two x values for the run, 3 plus 6, and the run is their difference, 6 minus 3.","0.67 has the right size and the wrong sign, since y drops from -2 to -4.","1 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1418","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A photo lab combines 200 mL of a 10% developer solution with 150 mL of a 20% developer solution. What is the approximate percent concentration of the combined solution?","opts":["14.29","15","15.71","50","52.5"],"correct":0,"why":"Solute total = 20 + 30 = 50 mL in 350 mL → 14.29%.","traps":[null,"Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.","Paired each percent with the other solution's volume, putting the 20 percent on the 200 mL pour.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture.","Took 15 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 350 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:men1229","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A conveyor scans 101 parcels a minute. How many parcels does it scan in 7 minutes?","opts":["697","700","707","717","807"],"correct":2,"why":"101 ≈ 100; 100×7 = 700, then correct by 7","traps":["That drops below 700, but 101 is above 100, so a 7 goes on rather than off.","That is 100 times 7, one 7 short of the full product.",null,"That corrects by 17 when the extra 1 in 101 is worth a single 7.","An extra hundred appeared, since 100 times 7 is 700, not 800."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261002"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s14"}},{"id":"FULL_3","name":"Full-Length Test 3","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-5-nd28q3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A compound makes the thylakoid membrane freely permeable to protons. What is the immediate effect on an illuminated chloroplast?","opts":["Water splitting stops while NADPH production continues","Electron transport continues while ATP synthesis collapses","Electron transport halts while ATP synthesis continues","The Calvin cycle speeds up because more protons reach the stroma","Both electron transport and ATP synthesis speed up together"],"correct":1,"why":"Separate the two halves of chemiosmosis: the chain that builds the gradient and the turbine that spends it. Poking holes in the membrane lets protons slip back without turning ATP synthase, so the pumping continues and the payoff disappears. The same logic applies to any uncoupler in any membrane, including the mitochondrion.","traps":["Water splitting keeps feeding electrons into photosystem II, and NADPH production continues right along with it.",null,"The carriers keep working as long as light is hitting them, because the leak destroys the gradient rather than the chain.","Protons that leak past ATP synthase do no work, and the Calvin cycle still needs the ATP it is no longer getting.","Electron transport may run slightly faster with no gradient pushing back, but ATP synthesis cannot rise without one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p017","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"During muscle contraction, which region of the sarcomere stays the same length?","opts":["I-band","The zone where thick and thin filaments overlap","Sarcomere","H-zone","A-band"],"correct":4,"why":"Sliding filament means the filaments themselves never shorten, they slide past each other. The A band is defined by the length of the thick myosin filaments, and since myosin does not change length, the A band does not either. Everything that shrinks is a gap: the I band is thin filament with no myosin over it and the H zone is myosin with no actin over it, so both narrow as the actin slides inward.","traps":["The I band is the stretch of thin filament not yet overlapped by myosin, and it narrows as the filaments slide together.","The overlap region grows during contraction as more thin filament slides in alongside the myosin.","The whole sarcomere shortening is what contraction is, since the Z discs are pulled toward each other.","The H zone is the middle stretch of myosin with no actin over it, and it shrinks as actin is pulled inward.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following organisms shows a Type I survivorship curve?","opts":["Hydra","Oysters","Humans, large mammals","Annual plants","Most insects and other invertebrates"],"correct":2,"why":"A survivorship curve is a picture of when in life the deaths happen. Type I organisms produce few offspring and care for them, so most survive through early and middle life and the curve stays high before dropping late, which is the human and large mammal pattern. Type II is a straight diagonal, a roughly constant chance of dying at any age. Type III falls off a cliff at the start, the shape for organisms that release enormous numbers of eggs or seeds and invest nothing afterward.","traps":["Hydra face roughly the same chance of dying at any age, which gives the straight Type II line.","Oysters release huge numbers of larvae and lose almost all of them early, which is the Type III shape.",null,"Annual plants make many seeds and lose most of them as seedlings, another Type III pattern.","Most insects produce many offspring with very heavy early mortality, so they sit on the Type III curve."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1lljp1j","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Assuming every salt dissociates completely, which of these solutions has the highest osmolarity?","opts":["0.1 M CaCl2","0.2 M NaCl","0.1 M glucose","0.15 M KCl","0.3 M glucose"],"correct":1,"why":"Convert each solution to particles per liter, then compare. Glucose stays as one particle, so 0.3 M glucose is 300 mOsm, calcium chloride releases three particles so 0.1 M is 300 mOsm, potassium chloride releases two so 0.15 M is 300 mOsm, and sodium chloride releases two so 0.2 M is 400 mOsm. Spotting that three of them tie at 300 is the fastest way through.","traps":["Calcium chloride releases three particles, so 0.1 times 3 gives 300 mOsm.",null,"This is only 100 mOsm, the lowest of the group.","Potassium chloride releases two particles, so 0.15 times 2 gives 300 mOsm.","Glucose does not dissociate, so this one is 300 mOsm."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g019","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Down syndrome results from which chromosomal abnormality?","opts":["Monosomy 21","XXY","Trisomy 18","Trisomy 21","Monosomy X"],"correct":3,"why":"This is straight recall on the autosomal trisomies, and the one to lock down first is that Down syndrome is three copies of chromosome 21. A clean way to hold the set is by number: 21 is Down, 18 is Edwards, 13 is Patau. The choice naming three copies of chromosome 21 is the one.","traps":["This has the right chromosome but the wrong direction, since Down syndrome is an extra copy and the loss of a whole autosome is not survivable.","The 47,XXY karyotype is Klinefelter syndrome, again a sex chromosome count rather than anything involving chromosome 21.","Trisomy 18 is Edwards syndrome, a different and far more severe autosomal trisomy.",null,"Monosomy X is Turner syndrome, a sex chromosome condition rather than an autosomal trisomy."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-slhyd5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"Sketch a cell just entering mitosis, before the nuclear envelope has come apart. Which set of events belongs to that first stage?","opts":["Sister chromatids come apart and are dragged toward opposite spindle poles","Chromatin condenses, the nucleolus fades, and the spindle begins to form","Each chromosome gains a second chromatid as its DNA is copied","Chromosomes line up in single file along the equator of the cell","Nuclear envelopes reassemble and the chromosomes unwind once again"],"correct":1,"why":"Prophase is the packing stage. Loose chromatin winds up into chromosomes you can actually see, the nucleolus disperses because ribosome production stops, and the centrosomes move apart while the spindle takes shape. Nothing has been sorted yet, because the envelope is still intact. Read the stages as one continuous motion, packing, then attaching, then lining up, then pulling, then unpacking, and each event lands in only one place.","traps":["Chromatid separation is anaphase, several steps later, and it requires the chromosomes to be aligned first.",null,"DNA is copied in S phase during interphase, so a cell entering mitosis already carries two chromatids per chromosome.","Lining up at the equator is metaphase, and it can only happen once the envelope is gone and microtubules have reached the chromosomes.","Reassembling envelopes and unwinding chromosomes is telophase, the reversal of what prophase has just done."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1hymbd2","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"At which point in the Calvin cycle does NADPH hand over its electrons?","opts":["When protons cross ATP synthase into the stroma","When RuBisCO joins carbon dioxide to RuBP","When two G3P are joined to form glucose","When five G3P are rearranged into three RuBP","When 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate is reduced to G3P"],"correct":4,"why":"NADPH is a reducing agent, so hunt for the step where a carbon actually gains electrons and hydrogens. That is the conversion of the phosphorylated acid into the aldehyde G3P, the one place in the cycle where carbon becomes more reduced. Name the three moves in order: fixation adds carbon, reduction adds electrons, regeneration rebuilds the acceptor.","traps":["That is the light reaction step that makes ATP, on the other side of the membrane from the Calvin cycle's chemistry.","Fixation needs no energy input from either carrier, since RuBisCO just joins the two molecules together.","Joining G3P into glucose happens after the cycle and is not where NADPH is spent.","Regeneration costs ATP, but no electrons change hands during it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-yiltle","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"The outer mitochondrial membrane is the gate for the intrinsic pathway, and two opposing families of proteins compete over it. A tumor cell is found to make far more Bcl-2 protein than normal. What is the effect on that cell?","opts":["It blocks cytochrome c release, so the cell survives damage that should kill it","It punches pores in the mitochondrial membrane, so the cell dies more readily than usual","It arrests the cell in G1, so the tumor ends up growing more slowly over time","It forces the cell into the extrinsic pathway, so death receptors take the decision over","It activates caspase-8 through the death receptor, which speeds apoptosis up"],"correct":0,"why":"Two families, one gate. Bax and Bak open the outer mitochondrial membrane and let cytochrome c out, and Bcl-2 is the antiapoptotic partner that holds them in check. Too much Bcl-2 means the gate never opens, so a cell carrying damage that should have ended it survives and keeps dividing, which is why this protein is overproduced in certain cancers. Sort the family by the first letters: Bax breaks the gate open, Bcl-2 bolts it closed.","traps":[null,"Pore formation is the job of the opposing family, Bax and Bak, and Bcl-2 exists specifically to keep those proteins in check.","Bcl-2 acts on cell death rather than on the cell cycle, and a cell that resists dying accumulates rather than slows down.","Blocking one pathway does not redirect a cell into the other, and the extrinsic pathway needs a ligand from outside to start it.","Caspase-8 belongs to the extrinsic pathway at the cell surface, and Bcl-2 acts at the mitochondrion instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-idaha0","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A yeast culture is kept strictly anaerobic and fed 5 molecules of glucose. Every glucose runs through glycolysis and then alcoholic fermentation. How much ATP does the culture net?","opts":["20 ATP","35 ATP","150 ATP","5 ATP","10 ATP"],"correct":4,"why":"Fermentation contributes no ATP of its own, so the entire yield is the net 2 ATP per glucose from glycolysis. Five glucose at 2 ATP each is 10. When a question puts a cell in anaerobic conditions, reset your count to glycolysis alone and leave the electron carriers out of it.","traps":["This uses the gross figure of 4 ATP per glucose and forgets the 2 invested up front.","This cashes in the 2 NADH per glucose at 2.5 ATP each, but with no oxygen there is no chain to cash them in on.","This is roughly the aerobic yield of about 30 per glucose, which requires oxygen and the full chain.","This counts one ATP per glucose, but the net from glycolysis is 2 per glucose.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p082","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Vaccination protects a person against a later infection primarily through which mechanism?","opts":["Antibiotics","Strengthening innate barriers such as skin, mucus, and stomach acid","Inducing memory B/T cells","Killing pathogens directly","Direct antibody injection"],"correct":2,"why":"A vaccine is a rehearsal. It presents antigen without causing real disease, so the B and T cells that recognize that antigen expand and leave behind long lived memory cells, and a later real exposure then meets a secondary response that is faster, larger, and mostly IgG. Active immunity like this takes a week or two to build but it lasts, so the choice about inducing memory cells is what a vaccine actually does.","traps":["Antibiotics treat a bacterial infection already underway and do nothing for viruses or for a future exposure.","Innate barriers are already in place and are not antigen specific, so strengthening them could not explain protection against one particular pathogen.",null,"A vaccine contains nothing that kills a pathogen; it presents antigen so that your own cells learn the target.","Injecting antibodies is passive immunity, which works within hours but fades in weeks and leaves no memory behind."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-a90f3aa0fb","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which statement about cortisol is correct?","opts":["It is an amino acid derivative from the adrenal medulla that raises heart rate within seconds","It strengthens the immune response during stress","It is a steroid from the adrenal cortex that lowers blood glucose","It is released in response to TSH from the anterior pituitary","It is a steroid from the adrenal cortex that raises blood glucose"],"correct":4,"why":"Cortisol is the long-haul stress hormone. ACTH from the anterior pituitary tells the adrenal cortex to release it. It keeps fuel available by raising blood glucose and it quiets the immune system.","traps":["That describes epinephrine. Cortisol is a CORTEX steroid and acts over hours.","Cortisol suppresses inflammation and immunity, which is why related drugs are used as anti-inflammatories.","Cortisol RAISES blood glucose by promoting new glucose production.","The signal for cortisol is ACTH, not TSH.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g023","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Red-green color blindness is X-linked recessive. A colorblind man has children with a woman who has normal color vision and is not a carrier. What percentage of their sons is expected to be colorblind?","opts":["0%","25%","50%","75%","100%"],"correct":0,"why":"Track the X chromosomes and the question answers itself. A son receives his single X from his mother and a Y from his father, so the father's affected X never reaches a son at all. This mother carries two normal alleles, so every son gets a normal X and normal color vision. The daughters are the interesting ones: each gets the father's affected X plus a normal X from the mother, making them unaffected carriers.","traps":[null,"One box out of four looks like a tidy Punnett square answer, but the affected allele is not present in the mother at all.","Half the sons being affected would require the mother to be a carrier so that she could pass an affected X to about half of them.","Three quarters of the sons could only be affected if the mother carried the affected allele, and she carries two normal ones.","This assumes the father hands his X chromosome to his sons, when in fact sons receive his Y."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1xq2vux","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A liver cell is flush with ATP and has very little AMP. What happens to glycolysis, and through which enzyme?","opts":["It slows down, because ATP inhibits pyruvate kinase, the committed step","It speeds up, because ATP activates phosphofructokinase","It speeds up, because ATP is the substrate of the investment phase","It slows down, because ATP inhibits phosphofructokinase","It is unaffected, because glycolysis is controlled only by how much glucose is available"],"correct":3,"why":"This is feedback inhibition asked in a cell-biology costume. Phosphofructokinase catalyzes the committed step, so it is the valve the cell turns: ATP and citrate close it while AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate open it. Any time a question hands you the energy state of a cell and asks about glycolysis, go straight to that enzyme.","traps":["Right logic, wrong enzyme: the committed, rate-limiting step of glycolysis is the one phosphofructokinase runs.","ATP does bind phosphofructokinase at a regulatory site, but it turns the enzyme down rather than up.","ATP is consumed in the investment phase, but being a substrate there does not set the pathway's rate.",null,"Substrate supply matters, but the pathway also reads the cell's energy state through feedback inhibition."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A horse and a donkey mate and produce a mule, which grows into a healthy adult but cannot reproduce. This illustrates which type of reproductive isolation?","opts":["Temporal isolation, in which the two species breed at different times of year","Postzygotic - inviability","Postzygotic - hybrid sterility","Prezygotic - gametic","Prezygotic - behavioral"],"correct":2,"why":"Split the timeline at fertilization. Anything that stops a zygote from forming, such as different habitats, different breeding seasons, incompatible courtship, or gametes that cannot fuse, is prezygotic. Here a zygote clearly formed and grew into an adult, so the barrier acted afterward, and the specific failure is in meiosis: a horse has 64 chromosomes, a donkey has 62, and the mule's 63 cannot pair up evenly to make working gametes.","traps":["Temporal isolation would keep the two species from ever breeding together, but this cross produced offspring.","Hybrid inviability means the hybrid dies as an embryo or before reaching maturity, while a mule is healthy and long lived.",null,"Gametic isolation means sperm and egg never fuse, yet fertilization plainly succeeded in this cross.","Behavioral isolation means the two species never court each other successfully, and these two mated."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Bryophytes such as mosses are unique among land plants in being which of the following?","opts":["Able to fertilize without free water, because their sperm are carried by wind","Gametophyte-dominant","Seed-producing","Vascular","Sporophyte-dominant"],"correct":1,"why":"The question is testing which generation you actually see when you look at a moss. The green carpet underfoot is the haploid gametophyte, and the little stalk with a capsule on top is the sporophyte growing out of it and depending on it for nutrition. Every other land plant flips that arrangement, with a large dominant sporophyte and a tiny gametophyte. So the choice naming gametophyte dominance is the one that sets bryophytes apart.","traps":["Moss sperm still swim through a film of water to reach the egg, so free water is required rather than optional.",null,"Bryophytes release spores, not seeds; seeds arrive later, with gymnosperms and angiosperms.","Mosses have no xylem or phloem, which is why they stay low and close to moisture; the vascular plants are everything else.","A dominant sporophyte is the pattern in ferns, gymnosperms and flowering plants, where the large green body is the diploid generation."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-y5m7wf","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Linear chromosomes lose a little sequence each round of replication, because the extreme end of the lagging strand has nowhere to place a final primer. Some cells avoid that loss using telomerase. What does telomerase do?","opts":["It caps the end with protein so ordinary polymerase can place a primer there","It removes the last RNA primer and fills the gap with DNA, the way DNA polymerase I does","It joins the two chromosome ends together so that no sequence can be lost","It carries its own RNA template and extends the 3' end with repeat sequence","It copies the missing end from the homologous chromosome, which serves as a template"],"correct":3,"why":"Telomerase is a reverse transcriptase that carries a short RNA template inside itself and uses it to add telomeric repeats onto the 3' overhang. That lengthened overhang gives the lagging strand machinery room to prime, so the chromosome stops getting shorter. Notice what it does not do: it never restores lost gene sequence, it extends the buffer at the tip. Look for it in germ cells, stem cells, and many cancer cells, and not in most somatic cells.","traps":["A protein cap does protect the end, but it cannot create the 3' hydroxyl that priming requires, so the shortening would continue.","Removing and replacing a primer works in the middle of a strand, but at the very end there is no upstream 3' hydroxyl to extend from, which is the whole problem.","Joining chromosome ends is exactly the accident telomeres exist to prevent, and a fused chromosome tears apart at the next anaphase.",null,"Using the homolog as a template happens in some repair pathways, and telomerase needs no second chromosome because it brings its own template."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e025","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Gause's competitive exclusion principle states that:","opts":["Two species with identical niches cannot coexist long-term","Competition leads to extinction","Two species can always coexist","Two species whose niches overlap at all cannot coexist in the same habitat","Mutualism wins"],"correct":0,"why":"This principle is about a limiting resource, not about species disliking each other. If two species need exactly the same thing and one is even slightly better at obtaining it, that small edge compounds generation after generation until the other is pushed out of the habitat. What you usually observe in nature instead is resource partitioning, where the two shift to slightly different niches, different perch heights, feeding times, or prey sizes, and both persist. So when a stem shows two species coexisting on an overlapping resource, look for how they divide it.","traps":[null,"The loser is excluded from that habitat, which usually means local displacement rather than the species going extinct everywhere.","This ignores what happens when two species depend on the same limiting resource and nothing separates them.","Partial overlap is common and stable; the principle only bites when the niches are effectively identical on the limiting resource, and partitioning handles the rest.","Mutualism is a separate kind of interaction and does not determine the outcome of competition for one resource."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-jwpv4p","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Bread dough rises because the yeast in it is fermenting. Which products of that pathway explain both the rise and the alcohol that bakes off?","opts":["Lactate and water","Acetyl-CoA and carbon dioxide","Ethanol and carbon dioxide","Ethanol and oxygen","Pyruvate and hydrogen gas"],"correct":2,"why":"Alcoholic fermentation takes two steps. Pyruvate loses a carbon as carbon dioxide to become acetaldehyde, and then acetaldehyde accepts the electrons from NADH to become ethanol, which regenerates NAD+. The gas is what lifts the dough, and it is also the clean point of contrast with lactate fermentation, which releases no gas at all.","traps":["Lactate fermentation releases no gas, so nothing would inflate the dough.","Acetyl-CoA is an aerobic intermediate that stays inside mitochondria and never piles up as a product.",null,"Fermentation consumes no oxygen and produces none, since oxygen is exactly what the cell has run out of.","Pyruvate is the starting material for fermentation rather than its end product, and yeast release no hydrogen gas."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1rvt47w","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A cell with an internal osmolarity of 300 mOsm is placed in a 0.15 M solution of NaCl, which dissociates completely. What happens to the cell volume?","opts":["It swells, because sodium enters and water follows it in","It swells, because 150 mOsm outside is well below the 300 mOsm inside","It stays the same, because the outside is also 300 mOsm","It shrinks, because 300 mOsm of NaCl outdraws the cytoplasm","It shrinks, because 0.15 M NaCl comes to 600 mOsm"],"correct":2,"why":"The whole question turns on counting particles rather than molecules. Each NaCl unit splits into two particles in solution, so 0.15 M times 2 gives 0.30 osmol per liter, which is 300 mOsm and matches the cell exactly. Equal osmolarity on both sides means no net water movement and no volume change. Multiply molarity by the number of ions a salt releases before comparing anything.","traps":["Sodium does not freely leak in here, and water moves by osmosis rather than chasing a solute that stayed outside.","This forgets that NaCl splits into two particles, so it uses 150 mOsm instead of 300 mOsm.",null,"The outside is 300 mOsm, which equals the inside rather than exceeding it.","This multiplies by four, 0.15 M NaCl yields 0.30 osmol per liter."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The acrosome is the enzyme filled cap at the tip of a sperm cell. Which organelle does it come from?","opts":["Centrosome","Mitochondrion","Lysosome","Golgi apparatus","Rough endoplasmic reticulum"],"correct":3,"why":"This question rewards you for knowing the secretory pathway rather than a stray fact. Proteins headed for a membrane bound compartment are made at the rough endoplasmic reticulum, then sorted and packaged by the Golgi, which buds off the vesicles, and the acrosome is exactly such a vesicle, one enormous one sitting over the sperm nucleus. When the sperm meets the egg coat the acrosome empties its enzymes to clear a path, which is the same logic as a lysosome, another Golgi product.","traps":["The centriole a sperm carries goes on to organize the zygote's first spindle, so it is a microtubule organizing center rather than a membrane bound sac of enzymes.","Mitochondria in a sperm are packed into the midpiece to power the tail, a different part of the cell with a different job.","The acrosome behaves like a giant lysosome because it is loaded with hydrolytic enzymes, which is why this is tempting, but lysosomes themselves also bud from the Golgi.",null,"The hydrolytic enzymes are translated on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, but the vesicle that becomes the acrosome is packaged and pinched off by the Golgi."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-glmnle","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Acetylcholine released at the neuromuscular junction binds a receptor on the muscle fiber that is itself an ion channel. The immediate result is:","opts":["Calcium pumped out of the fiber by an ATPase","A rise in cAMP inside the fiber","Transcription of new receptor genes","Autophosphorylation of the receptor on several tyrosine side chains","Sodium flowing into the fiber and depolarizing the membrane"],"correct":4,"why":"This is a ligand-gated ion channel, so binding and response are the same event: the channel opens and ions move down their electrochemical gradients. Sodium is high outside and the inside is negative, so sodium rushes in and the membrane depolarizes within milliseconds. No second messenger is needed, which is why this is the fastest receptor type there is.","traps":["Pumping calcium out costs ATP and is not what this ligand binding triggers.","A rise in cAMP needs a G-protein-coupled receptor and takes far longer than a channel opening.","Gene transcription runs on the steroid timescale, not the millisecond response of a channel.","Autophosphorylation on tyrosines belongs to receptor tyrosine kinases such as the insulin receptor.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p001","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Oxygenated blood returning from the lungs enters which part of the heart first?","opts":["The coronary sinus","Left atrium","Left ventricle","Right atrium","Right ventricle"],"correct":1,"why":"Trace one drop of blood. It leaves the lungs loaded with oxygen in the pulmonary veins, and those veins empty into the left atrium, which passes it through the mitral valve into the left ventricle for the trip out to the body. The right side of the heart handles blood coming back from the body and sends it to the lungs, so anything arriving from the lungs lands on the left.","traps":["The coronary sinus drains used blood from the heart's own muscle into the right atrium, not oxygenated blood from the lungs.",null,"The left ventricle does receive that oxygenated blood, but only after the left atrium has taken it in and passed it through the mitral valve.","The right atrium collects blood returning from the body through the venae cavae, blood that has already given up its oxygen.","The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs, so it handles blood on the way there rather than on the way back."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-533cecbb82","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following does NOT contribute to genetic variation among gametes or offspring?","opts":["Cytokinesis at the end of meiosis II","Mutation in a germ cell","Independent assortment in metaphase I","Random fertilization","Crossing over in prophase I"],"correct":0,"why":"Cytokinesis only splits the cytoplasm around chromosomes that have already been sorted, so it does not shuffle or change any alleles. The shufflers are crossing over, independent assortment and random fertilization, and mutation is the original source of brand new alleles. One more fact worth keeping: DNA is copied once, before meiosis I, and never between meiosis I and meiosis II. That is how the chromosome number ends up halved.","traps":[null,"A mutation in a germ cell CAN be passed on, so it does add variation.","Independent assortment DOES shuffle maternal and paternal chromosomes.","Which sperm meets which egg DOES add variation.","Crossing over DOES create new allele combinations."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-5-wo30xs","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The reaction center of photosystem II is called P680 and the one in photosystem I is called P700. What do those numbers tell you?","opts":["The energy an electron carries as it leaves each center","The order in which the two photosystems were discovered","The wavelength in nanometers each reaction center absorbs best","The number of chlorophyll molecules packed into each antenna complex","The number of protons each center pumps per electron passed"],"correct":2,"why":"The P stands for pigment and the number is a wavelength in nanometers, both sitting in the red end of the spectrum. Shorter wavelength means higher energy photons, so P680 works with slightly more energetic light than P700 does. Keeping those two numbers straight is also a reliable way to keep the two photosystems straight.","traps":["The names track the light being absorbed, not an energy value carried by an electron.","The Roman numerals in photosystem I and II record discovery order, while the P numbers are about absorbed light.",null,"Antenna complexes hold hundreds of pigment molecules, and the name does not record that count.","Neither reaction center is itself a proton pump, since that work happens at the cytochrome complex."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-qp7f5n","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"DNA polymerase sometimes inserts a base that does not pair with the template. Most of those mistakes are caught by the polymerase itself within a fraction of a second, before the fork moves on. Which activity of the enzyme performs that correction?","opts":["5' to 3' exonuclease activity, which chews forward past the mismatch","Endonuclease activity, which cuts the strand in the middle and starts the whole fragment again","3' to 5' exonuclease activity, which backs up and removes the wrong nucleotide","Helicase activity, which unwinds the mismatched stretch so it can re-pair","Ligase activity, which reseals the backbone straight over the mismatch"],"correct":2,"why":"Proofreading is just polymerase running in reverse. The wrong nucleotide sits at the 3' end, the same end the enzyme was adding to, so the enzyme backs up in the 3' to 5' direction, clips it off, and resumes forward synthesis. The direction of the exonuclease is the whole answer, and it always runs opposite to the direction of synthesis. That single backward step removes the great majority of the errors polymerase makes.","traps":["This is the right kind of activity pointed the wrong way, since chewing forward would destroy template that has not been copied yet.","Cutting in the middle of a strand is repair on a much larger scale, not the instant correction polymerase makes at its own active site.",null,"Unwinding does nothing about a nucleotide that is already covalently joined into the new strand.","Sealing the backbone would lock the error in permanently, which is the opposite of proofreading."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-d1bcg6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"A macrophage extends its membrane around a whole bacterium and takes it in inside a large vesicle. Compared with pinocytosis, this process:","opts":["Requires no membrane at all, since the bacterium dissolves in the cytoplasm on contact","Moves material out of the cell instead of into it","Uses a carrier protein in place of a vesicle","Consumes no ATP, because the membrane simply flows around the target","Brings in a large solid particle rather than a droplet of extracellular fluid"],"correct":4,"why":"Both are endocytosis, so both build a vesicle out of the plasma membrane and both cost energy. The split between them is the cargo: phagocytosis takes a large solid particle, pinocytosis takes a gulp of surrounding fluid with whatever is dissolved in it. Cell eating versus cell drinking is the pairing to keep.","traps":["The bacterium is wrapped in membrane inside a phagosome, it is not dissolved into the cytoplasm.","Both phagocytosis and pinocytosis bring material in, exocytosis is the outward one.","Carrier proteins handle single small solutes, a whole bacterium goes nowhere near one.","Both forms of endocytosis need ATP for the cytoskeletal work of reshaping the membrane.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p057","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Approximately what percentage of the sodium filtered at the glomerulus is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule?","opts":["~95%","~65%","~45%","~25%","~5%"],"correct":1,"why":"The nephron reabsorbs sodium in a predictable order, and the proximal convoluted tubule is the workhorse. It reclaims about two thirds of the filtered sodium, with water and much else following along, then the thick ascending limb takes roughly a quarter and the distal tubule and collecting duct fine tune the last few percent. The choice sitting near two thirds matches that bulk reabsorption role.","traps":["Nearly all filtered sodium is eventually reabsorbed across the whole nephron, but no single segment accounts for that much on its own.",null,"Just under half undersells the proximal tubule, whose brush border and dense mitochondria let it reclaim about two thirds before the filtrate reaches the loop.","Roughly a quarter is the thick ascending limb's share through its sodium potassium two chloride transporter, which makes it the second largest site.","About 5 percent is the distal convoluted tubule's share, the fine tuning end of the nephron rather than the bulk."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p067","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Melatonin, the hormone whose release rises in darkness and helps set the sleep and wake cycle, is secreted by which structure?","opts":["Liver","Thymus","Anterior pituitary","Hypothalamus","Pineal gland"],"correct":4,"why":"The exam checks that you can attach each hormone to its gland. Melatonin comes from the pineal gland, a small structure sitting deep in the brain behind the thalamus, and its output climbs in darkness and falls in light. Picture the pineal as the brain's light meter and melatonin as the night signal it sends.","traps":["The liver clears and modifies many hormones, and clearing melatonin is not the same as making it.","The thymus is where T cells mature, an immune organ rather than a source of melatonin.","The anterior pituitary releases growth hormone and the tropic hormones, and melatonin is not among them.","The hypothalamus sets many rhythms and drives the pituitary, but it does not produce melatonin itself.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-iupkei","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Researchers grow algae in water whose oxygen atoms carry a heavy isotope label, and feed them ordinary unlabeled carbon dioxide. Where does the label first show up?","opts":["In the phosphate groups of newly made ATP","In the carbon dioxide the algae take in from the air","In the glucose the algae build","In the oxygen gas bubbling off the algae","In the chlorophyll of the algae's thylakoid membranes"],"correct":3,"why":"The real question is which reactant donates the oxygen atoms in the gas. Water is the molecule torn apart at photosystem II, so the oxygen released came from water, while carbon dioxide's oxygen ends up in sugar. When a labeling experiment confuses you, follow the atom rather than the molecule.","traps":["ATP is assembled from ADP and phosphate already sitting in the stroma, not from the atoms of the split water.","Carbon dioxide is a starting material taken from the air, not a product that could collect the label.","The oxygen atoms in glucose trace back to carbon dioxide, which was deliberately left unlabeled here.",null,"Chlorophyll was built long before the experiment began and is not remade out of the labeled water."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1btzdwa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Three turns of the Calvin cycle produce six G3P, and only one of them leaves. What becomes of the other five?","opts":["They are exported to the cytosol and stored there as starch","They are oxidized back to carbon dioxide to recover the ATP that was spent","They move into the thylakoid lumen to feed the next round of light reactions","They are rearranged, with three more ATP, into three molecules of RuBP","They are reduced further by NADPH into two molecules of glucose"],"correct":3,"why":"The cycle has to pay itself back before it pays anything out. Fifteen carbons sitting in five G3P get reshuffled into three five carbon RuBP, and that reshuffle costs three ATP, which is why the ATP bill runs higher than the NADPH bill. Count carbons on both sides, fifteen and fifteen, and regeneration stops feeling like sleight of hand.","traps":["Starch is stored in the stroma, and only the single exported G3P ever leaves the cycle.","Burning them would undo the entire point of fixing that carbon in the first place.","Nothing from the Calvin cycle feeds back into the lumen; the traffic runs the other way, as ATP and NADPH into the stroma.",null,"Five three carbon molecules cannot become two six carbon sugars, and glucose assembly happens outside the cycle anyway."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-11ex6ax","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate is cut in half partway through glycolysis. What two molecules does that cut produce?","opts":["Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate and dihydroxyacetone phosphate","Two molecules of glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate directly","Two molecules of dihydroxyacetone phosphate","Two molecules of pyruvate","One glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate and one 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate"],"correct":0,"why":"Aldolase makes the cut, and the two halves are not identical: one is glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate and the other is dihydroxyacetone phosphate. Triose phosphate isomerase then converts the second into the first, which is why everything downstream gets counted twice per glucose. Store it as a cut that gives two different pieces, followed by an isomerase that makes them the same.","traps":[null,"Both halves do end up as this molecule, but only after the isomerase acts; the cut itself gives two different products.","This is the mirror image of the other symmetric answer: only one half comes out as dihydroxyacetone phosphate, and it is then converted rather than duplicated.","Pyruvate is the end product of the entire pathway, several steps past the cut.","1,3-bisphosphoglycerate is not made until the payoff phase begins, one step later."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g041","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A large population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium for a gene with two alleles, A and a. If the frequency of the A allele (p) is 0.7, what is the expected frequency of heterozygous (Aa) individuals?","opts":["0.09","0.21","0.30","0.42","0.49"],"correct":3,"why":"Hardy-Weinberg hands you two equations, and the whole game is knowing which one a question wants. Allele frequencies add to one, so p of 0.7 makes q 0.3. Genotype frequencies also add to one, split as p squared for AA, 2pq for Aa, and q squared for aa, so heterozygotes are 2 times 0.7 times 0.3. That factor of 2 is the piece most often dropped, and it is there because a heterozygote can receive the A from either parent.","traps":["This is q squared, the frequency of homozygous recessive individuals.","This is p times q without the factor of 2, which leaves out the second way a heterozygote can be assembled.","This is q, the frequency of the a allele, and an allele frequency is not a genotype frequency.",null,"This is p squared, the frequency of homozygous dominant individuals."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d039","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Chytrids (phylum Chytridiomycota) are set apart from all other fungi by which feature?","opts":["Flagellated spores","Asexual only","Photosynthesis","Multicellularity","Cell walls built from cellulose instead of chitin"],"correct":0,"why":"This question is pointing at the one fungal lineage that kept a swimming stage. Chytrids release zoospores that each carry a single posterior flagellum, something no other fungal group does, and it suits their life in water and saturated soil. Anchor it to the sound of the name: chytrids swim.","traps":[null,"Chytrids reproduce both sexually and asexually, and asexual reproduction is common across the entire kingdom anyway.","No fungus photosynthesizes; all of them absorb nutrients from outside the body, which is why the swimming spore is the real distinction.","Many fungi are multicellular, including molds and every mushroom former, so that cannot be what makes chytrids distinct.","Fungal walls, chytrids included, are built of chitin, while cellulose walls belong to plants and to the fungus-like water molds."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1hr4sgo","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Oligomycin plugs the proton channel of ATP synthase while leaving the electron transport complexes themselves untouched. What happens to the rate of electron flow through the chain?","opts":["It slows, because oxygen can no longer accept electrons","It increases, because protons are no longer being used up","It increases briefly and then stops as the matrix becomes acidic","It is unchanged, because the chain and ATP synthase work independently of each other","It slows, because the gradient builds and pumping against it gets harder"],"correct":4,"why":"This is respiratory control, and the gradient is the link between the two halves. With the synthase plugged, protons have no route back in, the gradient grows steeper, and the pumps eventually cannot push against it, so electron flow and oxygen consumption both fall. Contrast that with an uncoupler, which drains the gradient and makes the chain race.","traps":["Oxygen is still present and complex IV is not the target, since the block sits downstream at the synthase.","Protons piling up in the intermembrane space makes the next pump stroke harder, not easier.","Protons are pumped out of the matrix, so the matrix would become less acidic rather than more.","The two are coupled through the gradient, which is precisely what the phrase oxidative phosphorylation is telling you.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In a developing bird embryo, which extraembryonic structure collects and stores nitrogenous waste?","opts":["Yolk sac","Allantois","Amnion","Chorion","The albumen surrounding the embryo"],"correct":1,"why":"A bird embryo is sealed inside a shell, so its waste has nowhere to go, and this question asks how it copes. Birds and reptiles excrete uric acid, which is nearly insoluble and low in toxicity, and they pack it into the allantois, a sac growing out of the gut that serves as a built in waste bin until hatching. Four membranes, four jobs: the amnion cushions, the chorion exchanges gas, the yolk sac feeds, and the allantois stores waste.","traps":["The yolk sac supplies food, delivering yolk nutrients to the growing embryo, which is traffic running in the opposite direction.",null,"The amnion is the fluid filled sac that cushions the embryo and keeps it from drying out, so it holds water rather than waste.","The chorion lines the inside of the shell and handles gas exchange with the outside air, working alongside the allantois but storing nothing.","Albumen is the egg white, a water and protein reserve supplied by the mother, not an embryonic structure that collects waste."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-16zwvy5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Helicase has just pried the two parental strands apart at the fork. Left alone, those exposed single strands would snap back together or fold back on themselves before polymerase could use them. What keeps them available?","opts":["Primase stays bound along the entire length of each exposed template strand","Topoisomerase clamps the two strands apart by cutting and holding the backbone open","Histones wrap each single strand into a nucleosome until polymerase arrives there","Single-strand binding proteins coat the separated strands and hold them open","Ligase coats the exposed strands and seals them against re-pairing with each other"],"correct":3,"why":"This is about keeping the template accessible, which is a job of holding rather than cutting. Single-strand binding protein coats the separated strands, blocks them from re-annealing or folding into hairpins, and keeps them straight enough for polymerase to read. Sort the fork proteins by verb and they stop blurring together: helicase opens, single-strand binding protein holds, topoisomerase relieves twist, primase starts, polymerase builds, ligase seals.","traps":["Primase touches down only where a primer is needed and then leaves, so it cannot hold a whole strand open.","Topoisomerase does cut and reseal DNA, but it works ahead of the fork on supercoiling rather than on the separated strands behind it.","Nucleosomes package double-stranded DNA and would hide the template rather than expose it.",null,"Ligase seals nicks in a backbone, has no role in holding template apart, and does not coat DNA."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p047","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"What is the approximate resting membrane potential of a typical neuron?","opts":["-70 mV","-55 mV","0 mV","+70 mV","+30 mV"],"correct":0,"why":"At rest the inside of a neuron is negative relative to the outside, landing near -70 millivolts. Two things put it there: potassium leak channels let potassium drift out down its gradient and leave negatively charged proteins behind, and the sodium-potassium pump exports three sodium for every two potassium it imports. Lock in the sign first, negative inside, then the size.","traps":[null,"Around -55 millivolts is the threshold a neuron must reach in order to fire, which sits above the resting value rather than at it.","Zero would mean no charge separation across the membrane, leaving the neuron with no gradient to work with.","Positive 70 millivolts has about the right magnitude but the wrong sign, and a resting neuron is negative on the inside.","Positive 30 millivolts is roughly the peak reached at the top of an action potential, not the resting state."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p089","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which layer of the epidermis holds the dividing stem cells that replace keratinocytes lost from the skin surface?","opts":["Stratum lucidum","Stratum spinosum","Stratum basale","Stratum granulosum","Stratum corneum"],"correct":2,"why":"Skin replaces itself from the bottom up, so the dividing layer has to sit at the bottom. The stratum basale rests on the basement membrane and holds the keratinocyte stem cells along with melanocytes, and each daughter cell pushed upward fills with keratin and dies by the time it reaches the surface. Running the order from deep to superficial, basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum in thick skin, corneum, puts the choice naming the basale at the dividing end.","traps":["The lucidum is a thin clear band of dead cells found only in thick skin such as the palms and soles.","The spinosum sits just above the basale and its cells are held together by desmosomes, but the dividing stem cells stay anchored to the basement membrane below it.",null,"In the granulosum the cells are already filling with keratohyalin granules and losing their organelles on the way to dying.","The corneum is the outermost layer of dead, flattened, keratin filled cells that are shed, so nothing divides there."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A pedigree shows that affected individuals are predominantly male, and that no affected father ever passes the condition to his sons. Which inheritance pattern best fits this pedigree?","opts":["Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","Y-linked","Mitochondrial","X-linked dominant"],"correct":1,"why":"Two clues do all the work here. No father to son transmission tells you the gene is not on the Y and is not being carried through males on an autosome, because a father gives his son a Y chromosome rather than an X. A strong male excess tells you one copy is enough to show the trait, which is what happens when the gene sits on the X and males have only one X to begin with. Put those together and you land on X-linked recessive every time.","traps":["Autosomal recessive conditions strike males and females at about the same rate, and affected fathers can pass the allele to sons.",null,"A Y-linked trait would pass from every affected father to every one of his sons, which is exactly what this pedigree never shows.","Mitochondrial conditions come only from the mother and affect sons and daughters alike, with no male bias.","X-linked dominant also blocks father to son transmission, but it usually produces more affected females than males rather than a male excess."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-19awm96","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"At a synapse, calcium entering the axon terminal triggers vesicles of neurotransmitter to release their contents into the cleft. During that release, the vesicle membrane:","opts":["Fuses with the plasma membrane and becomes part of it","Is digested by lysosomal enzymes","Is pumped out by the sodium potassium pump","Pinches off and floats away into the synaptic cleft as a free bubble","Passes intact through the plasma membrane"],"correct":0,"why":"Exocytosis is fusion, not ejection. The vesicle membrane joins the plasma membrane, the inside of the vesicle opens to the outside world, and the cell surface grows by exactly that patch, which is later recovered by endocytosis. Contents go out, membrane stays.","traps":[null,"Lysosomal digestion is how a cell breaks down internalized material, not how it secretes.","That pump moves ions only and has nothing to do with vesicle traffic.","Membrane does not survive as a free bubble in the cleft, it merges into the cell surface.","A whole vesicle cannot pass through an intact bilayer."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_xp009","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"10.0 g of N₂ and 4.00 g of H₂ are placed in a sealed reactor and heated until the reaction N₂(g) + 3 H₂(g) → 2 NH₃(g) goes to completion. Identify the limiting reagent and calculate the maximum mass of NH₃ formed.","opts":["Both consumed simultaneously; 14.0 g NH₃","H₂ limits; 22.4 g NH₃","N₂ limits; 17.0 g NH₃","H₂ limits; 11.3 g NH₃","N₂ limits; 12.1 g NH₃"],"correct":4,"why":"mol N₂ = 10/28 = 0.357. mol H₂ = 4/2 = 2.00. Stoichiometric ratio H₂:N₂ = 3:1, so H₂ needed for all N₂ = 3 × 0.357 = 1.07 mol < 2.00 available. N₂ limits, so mol NH₃ = 2 × 0.357 = 0.714, so mass = 0.714 × 17 = 12.1 g.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A 50.0 mL aliquot of 4.0 M sucrose solution is poured into a graduated cylinder, and water is added to dilute the solution to a final concentration of 2.0 M. Assuming volumes are additive and temperature is constant, what volume of water must be added?","opts":["25 mL","200 mL","150 mL","50 mL","100 mL"],"correct":3,"why":"M1V1 = M2V2 gives V2 = (4.0)(50.0)/(2.0) = 100. mL final volume. The water added is V2 - V1 = 100 - 50 = 50 mL. Forgetting the subtraction step gives 100 mL.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:p-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Which has the LARGEST 2nd ionization energy: Na, Mg, Al, K?","opts":["All four are about equal","Al","Na","Mg","K"],"correct":2,"why":"IE2 = energy to remove the SECOND electron (after IE1 has already pulled one off). The rule: IE jumps when the next electron must come from a noble-gas core. Na is [Ne]3s¹ → Na⁺ is already [Ne]. Pulling another electron off Na⁺ means cracking the full octet - huge jump. Mg, Al still have valence electrons after IE1. K is [Ar]4s¹ → K⁺ is also [Ar], so K shows a similar jump, but Na⁺ is smaller and has higher effective nuclear charge, so its IE2 is the biggest.","traps":["They are far apart: about 1451 kJ/mol for magnesium against about 4562 for sodium, because only sodium and potassium have to break into a noble gas core.","Aluminium goes from Al⁺ ([Ne]3s²) to Al²⁺ ([Ne]3s¹), still inside the 3s subshell, so no core is being broken.",null,"Magnesium's second removal takes 3s¹ down to the neon core, still a valence electron, so at about 1451 kJ/mol its IE2 is the gentlest of the four.","K is a great candidate - K⁺ is [Ar], so its IE2 also jumps. But Na⁺ is smaller and more tightly held than K⁺, so Na has the higher IE2. (Across a period IE rises; down a group IE falls.)"],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ar2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"For a reversible reaction, the energy diagram shows Ea(forward) = 50 kJ/mol and Ea(reverse) = 80 kJ/mol. Since ΔH = Ea(forward) − Ea(reverse), the reaction's overall enthalpy change can be calculated. What is ΔH for the forward reaction?","opts":["+130 kJ/mol","−30 kJ/mol","+30 kJ/mol","+50 kJ/mol","−130 kJ/mol"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔH = Ea(fwd) − Ea(rev) = 50 − 80 = −30 kJ/mol. Negative ΔH means the forward reaction is exothermic: the products sit lower in energy than reactants by 30 kJ/mol. Adding the two Ea's (130) is the wrong operation.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:ph-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Mix 50 mL of 0.10 M HCl with 50 mL of 0.20 M NaOH. Final pH?","opts":["1.30","7.00","12.70","13.00","13.30"],"correct":2,"why":"mol H⁺ = 0.005, mol OH⁻ = 0.010. Excess OH⁻ = 0.005 mol in 100 mL. [OH⁻] = 0.05 M. pOH = −log(0.05) = 1.30. pH = 14 − 1.30 = 12.70.","traps":["That is the pH of the acid by itself, and the base here is present in twice the moles.","Equal moles would give 7; not equal here.",null,"That leaves the extra 0.005 mol in the original 50 mL instead of the combined 100 mL.","That uses the full 0.20 M hydroxide, ignoring both the acid it neutralized and the doubled volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_xp006","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A 2.50 L container holds N₂ at 1.00 atm and 25 °C. The container is sealed and the temperature is increased to 100 °C. Simultaneously, an additional 0.020 mol of N₂ is added (no leaks). What is the new pressure?","opts":["5.50 atm","0.75 atm","2.49 atm","4.00 atm","1.50 atm"],"correct":4,"why":"Initial: PV=nRT, so n = PV/RT = (1.00)(2.50)/(0.0821 × 298) = 0.1023 mol. Add 0.020 mol, so n_new = 0.1223 mol. New T = 373 K. P = nRT/V = (0.1223)(0.0821)(373)/2.50 ≈ 1.50 atm. (The +0.020 mol contributes substantially despite the higher T.) Re-check: actually 0.1223 × 0.0821 × 373 / 2.5 = 1.498 atm - answer is 1.50 atm.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-cell-potential#tl00x0","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Standard reduction potentials:\n\nZn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn E° = −0.76 V\nNi²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni E° = −0.25 V\n\nWhat is E°cell for the galvanic cell built from these two half-cells?","opts":["+0.51 V","−0.51 V","+0.77 V","−1.01 V","+1.02 V"],"correct":0,"why":"Cathode = higher reduction potential (Ni). Anode = Zn. E°cell = (-0.25) − (-0.76) = +0.51 V. Red cat, an ox: reduction at the cathode, oxidation at the anode.","traps":[null,"A galvanic cell runs by itself, so E°cell must be POSITIVE. The higher reduction potential is the cathode.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You added the two table values. E°cell = E°cathode MINUS E°anode, both read as reductions.","You multiplied E° by the electron coefficients. Potential is intensive: never scale it when you balance."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"When ADDING measured values, the result must be rounded to the same DECIMAL PLACE as the input with the fewest decimals (not fewest sig figs). Compute 25.34 + 1.2 + 0.123 and report with correct precision.","opts":["26.66","26.663","26.6630","26.7","27"],"correct":3,"why":"Sum = 26.663. The least precise addend is 1.2 (one decimal place), so the sum must be rounded to one decimal place: 26.663 → 26.7. Addition uses decimal places (not sig figs); multiplication uses sig figs.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#xyx8qr","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"2 H₂ + O₂ → 2 H₂O\n\nIf 5 mol of H₂ reacts with 1 mol of O₂, how many moles of H₂O can form?","opts":["5 mol","6 mol","2 mol","1 mol","3 mol"],"correct":2,"why":"H₂: 5 / 2 = 2.5. O₂: 1 / 1 = 1. The smaller one limits. Product = 1 × 2 = 2 mol.","traps":["You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield.","You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.",null,"You compared raw moles. Divide each by its coefficient first, then pick the smaller.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:qn5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A photon ejects an electron from hydrogen (IE = 13.6 eV) with kinetic energy of 5.0 eV. Photon energy?","opts":["8.6 eV","18.6 eV","15.2 eV","13.6 eV","5.0 eV"],"correct":1,"why":"Photoelectric effect: E_photon = work function (IE) + KE_ejected = 13.6 + 5.0 = 18.6 eV.","traps":["This subtracts the kinetic energy instead of adding it, and the photon pays the binding energy first with the leftover becoming speed.",null,"This uses 10.2 eV, the cost of lifting the electron only to n=2, when setting it free costs the full 13.6 eV.","This pays only the ionization energy and leaves nothing for the 5.0 eV the electron carries away.","This is the kinetic energy on its own, before adding the 13.6 eV the photon spent freeing the electron."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#1xyppx6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.0693 min⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["0.048 min","14.4 min","0.1 min","10 min","28.9 min"],"correct":3,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.0693 = 10 min. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath.","That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.",null,"You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Phosphorus pentachloride PCl5 in the gas phase has a trigonal-bipyramidal geometry with three equatorial P-Cl bonds at 120° and two axial bonds at 90°. The central P forms 5 sigma bonds with no lone pairs (steric number = 5). Which hybridization scheme correctly accommodates these 5 bonding orbitals?","opts":["sp^3","sp^3 d","sp","sp^2","sp^3 d^2"],"correct":1,"why":"Steric number 5 demands 5 hybrid orbitals: one s + three p + one d = sp^3 d. (sp^3 d^2 would be 6 hybrids for octahedral SF6.) The mixing of 3d orbitals with s and p enables the expanded octet around P.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#173pz7a","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 0.5 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 27 °C and 1 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["1250 L","12.3 L","18.5 L","1.11 L","24.6 L"],"correct":1,"why":"T = 27 + 273 = 300 K. V = nRT / P = (0.5)(0.0821)(300) / 1 = 12.3 L.","traps":["You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.","You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Two related reactions are tabulated at 298 K: (1) A -> B with ΔH1 = -50 kJ; (2) 2 B -> C with ΔH2 = +30 kJ. Apply Hess's law (multiply step 1 by 2 to match B stoichiometry, then add to step 2) to compute ΔH for the combined reaction 2 A -> C.","opts":["+30 kJ","-130 kJ","-20 kJ","+70 kJ","-70 kJ"],"correct":4,"why":"Multiply (1) by 2: 2 A -> 2 B, ΔH = 2(-50) = -100 kJ. Add (2): 2 B -> C, ΔH = +30 kJ. Sum: 2 A -> C, ΔH = -100 + 30 = -70 kJ.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-e9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A 25.0 mL aliquot of 0.40 M KCl solution is pipetted into a clean Erlenmeyer flask at 25 °C as part of a precipitation experiment. Considering only the dissolved KCl (assume complete dissociation but molarity refers to the formula unit), how many moles of KCl are present?","opts":["0.025 mol","0.010 mol","0.10 mol","0.40 mol","0.005 mol"],"correct":1,"why":"moles = M x V(L) = 0.40 mol/L x 0.0250 L = 0.010 mol. Forgetting to convert 25.0 mL to 0.0250 L gives 10 mol, an obviously wrong magnitude.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ca5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"A 0.500 g sample of benzoic acid is combusted in a sealed bomb calorimeter (constant volume) with heat capacity C_cal = 5.00 kJ/°C. The combustion releases 2.50 kJ of heat, all absorbed by the calorimeter assembly. What temperature change ΔT does the calorimeter experience?","opts":["2.5°C","0.50°C","5.0°C","12.5°C","25°C"],"correct":1,"why":"For a bomb calorimeter, q_rxn = -C_cal·ΔT. Solving for ΔT: ΔT = q_absorbed/C_cal = 2.50 kJ / 5.00 kJ/°C = 0.500°C. Note that constant-volume calorimetry uses the calorimeter constant C_cal directly, not m·c, since C_cal already lumps in all components.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#xz6wsz","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 16 g of CH₄? (molar mass 16 g/mol)","opts":["6.02 × 10²³","4","9.63 × 10²⁴","2.41 × 10²⁴","1.51 × 10²³"],"correct":3,"why":"16 g / 16 g/mol = 1 mol CH₄. Times 4 hydrogen per molecule = 4 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 2.41 × 10²⁴.","traps":["That is the number of MOLECULES. Each CH₄ holds 4 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 4.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.","You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.",null,"You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A reaction at 1 atm has both ΔH > 0 (endothermic) and ΔS > 0 (positive entropy change). Using the equation ΔG = ΔH − T·ΔS, the spontaneity depends on temperature. Under what temperature regime is the reaction spontaneous (ΔG < 0)?","opts":["Spontaneous only at high T","Spontaneous only at low T","Spontaneous only at T = ΔH/ΔS","Always spontaneous at any T","Never spontaneous at any T"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. With ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0, raising T makes the −TΔS term dominate negatively. Spontaneity (ΔG < 0) requires T > ΔH/ΔS.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#y94x96","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 2 m aqueous solution of Na₂SO₄? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-14.9 °C","11.2 °C","-3.07 °C","-3.72 °C","-11.2 °C"],"correct":4,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (3)(1.86)(2) = 11.2 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 11.2 = -11.2 °C.","traps":["You counted one ion too many. Write the dissociation out and count the particles.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C.","You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","You forgot the van't Hoff factor. Na₂SO₄ splits into 3 ions, so i = 3.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ph-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 0.010 M Ba(OH)2 solution is prepared in water at 25 °C, where Ba(OH)2 dissociates completely to give 1 Ba(2+) and 2 OH- per formula unit. Therefore [OH-] = 0.020 M. Using pOH = -log[OH-] and pH + pOH = 14, what is the pH?","opts":["11.0","12.0","12.3","12.7","13.0"],"correct":2,"why":"[OH-] = 2 x 0.010 = 0.020 M; pOH = -log(0.020) = 1.70. Therefore pH = 14 - 1.70 = 12.30. Forgetting the factor of 2 (using 0.010 M) gives pH = 12.0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, a galvanic cell has E°_cell = 0.50 V and n = 2 electrons transferred per cell reaction. Using the relation between E° and K: log K = nE°/0.0592, calculate the equilibrium constant K for the cell reaction.","opts":["10^5","10","10^(-17)","10^17","10^10"],"correct":3,"why":"log K = nE°/0.0592 = (2)(0.50)/0.0592 = 16.9 ≈ 17. Therefore K ≈ 10^17. Forgetting n (using just E°/0.0592) gives ~10^8.5; sign errors give 10^(-17).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = −40.0 kJ/mol and ΔS = −100 J/(K·mol). Both ΔH and ΔS are negative. Using ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, the reaction is spontaneous (ΔG < 0) only when |TΔS| < |ΔH|. Below what temperature is the reaction spontaneous?","opts":["T < 400 K","T < 1000 K","Always spontaneous","T < 100 K","T < 40 K"],"correct":0,"why":"Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = (−40000 J)/(−100 J/K) = 400 K. Below 400 K, the −TΔS term (positive) is smaller than |ΔH|, so ΔG < 0. Watch units: convert kJ to J before dividing, otherwise you get 0.4 K (a common error).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:b-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Which has the SHORTEST C−C bond?","opts":["Benzene (1.5 order)","Ethene (C=C)","Cyclopropane (strained ring)","Ethyne (C≡C)","Ethane (C-C)"],"correct":3,"why":"Bond length follows bond order: the more pairs shared between two atoms, the harder they are pulled together. Ethyne's triple bond comes in near 1.20 Å, ethene's double near 1.34 Å, benzene's bonds near 1.40 Å at an order of 1.5, and ethane's single near 1.54 Å. Count the pairs between the two carbons first, then rank: the shortest bond is always the one with the highest order.","traps":["Benzene's bonds average about 1.40 Å, longer than ethene's double bond and well past ethyne's triple.","The double bond pulls in to about 1.34 Å, shorter than ethane but not the shortest here.","The ring is strained by its 60 degree angles rather than by short bonds, and its C-C sits near 1.51 Å.",null,"Ethane's single bond is the longest of the set at about 1.54 Å."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"You compute osmotic pressure with π = i·M·R·T. For the answer to come out in atm with M in mol/L and T in K, R must have units:","opts":["J/(mol·K)","cal/(mol·K)","L·atm/(mol·K)","mmHg·L/(mol·K)","atm·mol/(L·K)"],"correct":2,"why":"π [atm] = i · M [mol/L] · R · T [K]. Solving for R's units: atm / (mol/L · K) = atm·L/(mol·K) = L·atm/(mol·K). That is the gas constant 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K). Picking R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) gives π in J/L (not atm).","traps":["R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) is the SI value, but it does not give atm directly. Use 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K) when the answer must be in atm.","Calorie units are obsolete for this and would produce the wrong magnitude - π in cal/L is not standard.",null,"The structure is right but the pressure unit is not; R = 62.4 mmHg·L/(mol·K) returns the answer in mmHg, and this question asks for atm.","Units do not balance: this would give π in (atm·mol/(L·K)) × (mol/L) × K = atm·mol²/L² - not a pressure unit."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"originals:ab-77b0d210b1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"0.10 mol of a weak acid HA (pKa 5.00) is mixed with 0.050 mol of NaOH in 1.0 L of water. What is the pH of the resulting solution?","opts":["1.30","4.70","5.00","5.30","7.00"],"correct":2,"why":"Do the reaction first, then the equilibrium. NaOH converts 0.050 mol of HA into A-, leaving 0.050 mol HA and 0.050 mol A-. Equal amounts means pH equals pKa, so 5.00. Half neutralizing a weak acid is a classic way to make a buffer.","traps":["You treated 0.050 M as strong acid. The NaOH is consumed and what remains is a mix of HA and A-.","You used a base to acid ratio of 0.5. After the reaction there is 0.050 mol of HA and 0.050 mol of A-, a ratio of 1.",null,"You used a base to acid ratio of 2. Half of the HA remains, so the two are equal.","Partial neutralization of a weak acid does not give a neutral solution. It gives a buffer."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#12tb68e","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["3","3.3","10","10.7","11"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M. pOH = 3, pH = 14 − 3 = 11.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:at-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Ion isoelectronic with Ar AND with charge +2?","opts":["Ca²⁺","S²⁻","Mg²⁺","Sc³⁺","K⁺"],"correct":0,"why":"Ar has 18 e⁻. Ca²⁺ has 20−2 = 18 e⁻ AND charge +2.","traps":[null,"S²⁻ has 18 electrons as well, and its charge is negative rather than positive.","Mg²⁺ has the 2 plus charge but only 10 electrons, which matches neon rather than argon.","Sc³⁺ also reaches 18 electrons, though it sits at a 3 plus charge.","K⁺ does carry argon's 18 electrons, and its charge is 1 plus rather than 2 plus."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_m7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Combustion analysis of an unknown carbohydrate yields mass percentages of 40.0% C, 6.7% H, and 53.3% O. Assuming a 100 g sample (40.0 g C, 6.7 g H, 53.3 g O) and dividing each by atomic mass to find moles, what is the empirical formula?","opts":["CH₂O","C₆H₁₂O₆","C₂H₄O₂","CHO","C₃H₆O₃"],"correct":0,"why":"Moles: C 40.0/12 = 3.33; H 6.7/1 = 6.7; O 53.3/16 = 3.33. Dividing by smallest (3.33) gives 1:2:1 → CH₂O. C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose) is the molecular formula but not the empirical formula; the empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ic-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A weak acid HA dissociates as HA(aq) ⇌ H+(aq) + A-(aq) at 25 °C, with Ka = 1.0x10^(-4) and [HA]_0 = 0.05 M, [H+]_0 ≈ 0, [A-]_0 = 0. Using the small-x approximation in an ICE table, calculate the equilibrium [H+].","opts":["5.0x10^(-3) M","7.1x10^(-4) M","1.0x10^(-2) M","1.4x10^(-3) M","2.2x10^(-3) M"],"correct":4,"why":"Ka = x^2 / (0.05 − x) ≈ x^2 / 0.05. So x^2 = (1.0x10^(-4))(0.05) = 5.0x10^(-6); x = sqrt(5.0x10^(-6)) ≈ 2.24x10^(-3) M. Forgetting to multiply by [HA]_0 gives 1x10^(-2).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"For the gas-phase reaction A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 298 K, the equilibrium constant is K_1 = 5.0. The chemist now multiplies all stoichiometric coefficients by 2 to write 2A(g) ⇌ 2B(g) at the same temperature. What is the new equilibrium constant K_2?","opts":["K_1^2 = 25","K_1 = 5.0","K_1/2 = 2.5","1/K_1 = 0.20","2K_1 = 10"],"correct":0,"why":"Multiplying coefficients by n raises K to the n-th power: K_new = K_old^n. Here n = 2, so K_2 = (5.0)^2 = 25. Doubling K (giving 10) is the most common error.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"}],"ochem":[{"id":"originals:ab-a2e657aa1b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"The hydrogens on the carbon next to a ketone carbonyl have a pKa near 20, far lower than the pKa of an ordinary alkane. Why?","opts":["Alpha hydrogens form hydrogen bonds to the carbonyl oxygen, which holds them loosely enough for a base to remove one","The carbonyl carbon is sp3 hybridized, which strains the alpha C-H bonds and lets them break easily","The conjugate base is an enolate, and its negative charge is shared with the carbonyl oxygen by resonance","The C-H bond is shorter next to a carbonyl","The carbonyl oxygen donates its lone pairs into the alpha C-H bond and weakens it enough to release the proton"],"correct":2,"why":"Pull off an alpha hydrogen and the lone pair left on carbon sits right next to the C=O pi bond. Resonance moves the negative charge onto the oxygen, which is happy to hold it. That stabilized anion, the enolate, is the starting point for aldol and Claisen chemistry.","traps":["C-H hydrogens do not take part in hydrogen bonding in a meaningful way.","The carbonyl carbon is sp2. Hybridization of that carbon is not the reason.",null,"The driving force is the stability of the conjugate base.","The carbonyl group WITHDRAWS electron density, which is what stabilizes the anion."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-11y5yyy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl oxalate (drawn) is treated with ethyl acetate and sodium ethoxide, followed by acidic workup. What is the major crossed-Claisen product?","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)OCC","O=CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha H and acts only as the electrophilic acceptor. Distractors: self-Claisen product, aldehyde variant, beta-hydroxy diester, double-addition over-condensation. All valid, exactly one correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1e26x8u","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Using the 2^n rule, how many distinct stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane (shown)?","opts":["1","2","3","4","6"],"correct":2,"why":"There are n = 2 stereocenters, so 2^n predicts a maximum of 4. But the two ends are identical (both CH(Cl)CH3), giving an internal mirror plane in the (R,S) configuration, which is a single achiral meso compound. This is the classic symmetry case where the real count is less than 2^n.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1bxsg2d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"4-Methyl-1-pentene is treated with HBr and a peroxide (radical conditions). Identify the major product.","opts":["CC(C)CC(C)Br","CC(C)CC(Br)CBr","CC(C)CCCBr","CCC(Br)C(C)C","CCCC(C)(C)Br"],"correct":2,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov (peroxide) addition: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to give the more stable secondary radical at C2, then H caps C2, so Br is on the primary terminal carbon (1-bromo-4-methylpentane). The isopropyl branch is a spectator. The Markovnikov option puts Br on C2; the rest are rearranged, falsely tertiary, or dibromide structures.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCC(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-23bj23","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Predict the major product. 3-Hexyne (CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH3) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation: (1) BH3.THF, (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the single major product and note why an aldehyde is not an option here.","opts":["CCCCCC=O","CCC=C(O)CC","CCC(=O)C(=O)CC","CCCC(=O)CC","CCCCC(C)=O"],"correct":3,"why":"3-Hexyne is internal and symmetrical, so hydration (by either method) can only give a ketone, and both orientations converge on 3-hexanone. Hydroboration's anti-Markovnikov selectivity is irrelevant for a symmetrical internal alkyne. An aldehyde (hexanal) is impossible without a terminal C-H; 2-hexanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the intermediate; the 1,2-diketone is over-oxidation.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC#CCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1ix28dz","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Consider the bromonium-ion mechanism of Br2 addition to an alkene. Which statement BEST describes the curved arrow for the ring-opening step that sets the stereochemistry (substrate shown: cyclohexene)?","opts":["Bromide attacks a bromonium-ion carbon from the face opposite the C - Br+ bond (backside), giving anti (trans) addition","Bromide attacks the same face of the ring that the bromonium bridge occupies, so both bromines end up on one side, giving syn (cis) addition","Bromide adds to the less hindered carbon by a concerted syn pathway, giving the cis product","A free, planar carbocation forms first, so the bromide can add to either face equally (no stereochemical preference)","The pi electrons attack bromide directly, expelling Br+ from the molecule and giving a Markovnikov rearranged monobromide product"],"correct":0,"why":"The stereochemistry-setting step is SN2-like backside attack of bromide on the bridged bromonium carbon, inverting that center and placing the two bromines anti (trans). option A is correct; option B/option 5 give the wrong (syn/cis) outcome, option D reverses the electron flow, option E wrongly describes an open carbocation. Identifies a single arrow only (no full mechanism), substrate is drawn, in DAT scope.","traps":null,"qs":"C1CCC=CC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1jgftmx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol (shown) is treated with hot concentrated H2SO4. Which alkene is the major product?","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","C=C(C)C(C)(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Loss of water gives a secondary cation adjacent to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a more stable tertiary cation, then Zaitsev elimination gives tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (option A). option C is the non-rearranged terminal alkene, option B is a rearranged less-substituted alkene, option D is substitution (no Cl), option E is saturated.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1x3lymn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"5,5-Dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) is treated with the anion of nitromethane (-CH2NO2), a stabilized carbon nucleophile, then mild acid. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["O=C1C(C[N+](=O)[O-])C(C)(C)CC=C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(ON=O)C1","OC1(C[N+](=O)[O-])CC(C)(C)CC=C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(C[N+](=O)[O-])C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CCC1C[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":3,"why":"The nitronate is a soft, stabilized carbon nucleophile and adds 1,4: its carbon bonds to the beta carbon (C3), and the enolate is protonated to restore the saturated ketone = option 0 (verified: nitromethyl carbon sits at the beta position, 2 bonds from the carbonyl carbon). Option 1 is the 1,2-adduct (allylic alcohol, alkene retained). Option 2 attaches nitromethyl at the alpha carbon and keeps the alkene. Option 3 shows nitrite O-N=O (O-attachment, no C-C bond). Option 4 places the new C-C bond at the alpha carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CC(C)(C)CC=C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1v0wmr4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this E2 reaction?","opts":["C=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)(C)C=C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CCOC(C)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"E2 is concerted with no carbocation, so no rearrangement. The only beta-H is on the CH3 of the C-Br carbon (the other neighbor is the quaternary tert-butyl carbon, no H), giving 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (option A). option B (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene) is the rearranged Zaitsev trap requiring a hydride/methyl shift, which E2 cannot do.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)C(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ykycfl","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A student wants to make a six-membered cyclic beta-ketoester by a Dieckmann condensation. Of the diesters below, which gives a six-membered ring (cyclohexanone) product, while diethyl succinate (shown) fails to give a useful Dieckmann product?","opts":["Diethyl succinate (1,4-diester)","Diethyl adipate (1,6-diester)","Diethyl malonate (1,3-diester)","Diethyl oxalate (1,2-diester)","Diethyl pimelate (1,7-diester)"],"correct":4,"why":"Ring size tracks chain length: a 1,7-diester (pimelate) closes to a favorable six-membered ring. Succinate (drawn, 1,4) would form a strained three-membered ring and fails, adipate (1,6) gives a five-membered ring, and malonate/oxalate are too short to cyclize. Only pimelate gives the six-membered cyclohexanone beta-ketoester.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1cv6f2u","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: (E)-1-phenylpropene (trans-beta-methylstyrene) is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O. Which set of products forms?","opts":["CC(=O)O.O=C(O)c1ccccc1","C=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CCC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the internal C=C. The phenyl-bearing carbon becomes benzaldehyde and the methyl-bearing carbon becomes acetaldehyde.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-185lgi6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene reacts with acrolein (prop-2-enal) thermally. Predict the major product (apply the 'ortho' regiochemistry rule).","opts":["COC=CC=CCCC=O","COC1C=CC1C=O","COC1C=CCCC1C=O","COC1C=CC(C=O)CC1","COC1C=CCC(C=O)C1"],"correct":2,"why":"A 1-substituted (terminal) diene with a mono-substituted dienophile follows the 'ortho' rule: the methoxy and the CHO end up on adjacent (1,2) carbons of the cyclohexene, with the new ring double bond between the former diene C2-C3. option A is the 1,2 product. option B is the 1,3 ('meta') and option C the 1,4 ('para') isomer (wrong regiochemistry), option D is a 4-membered ring (wrong skeleton), option E never cyclized.","traps":null,"qs":"COC=CC=C.C=CC=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-f00uzq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with 1 equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized allylic cation, and under thermodynamic control the 1,4-addition product dominates. Which structure is that major 1,4-product (note its alkene geometry)?","opts":["C/C=C/CBr","CCC(Br)C","C=CC(C)Br","C/C=C\\CBr","C=CCCBr"],"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives allylic cation CH3-CH(+)...CH=CH2; bromide capture at the 1,4-terminus gives 1-bromo-2-butene, with the more stable (E) alkene under thermodynamic control: C/C=C/CBr. (Z) is wrong geometry; C=CC(C)Br is the kinetic 1,2-product; CCC(C)Br (2-bromobutane) is wrong skeleton; C=CCCBr (4-bromo-1-butene) is anti-Markovnikov.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1wg15sh","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Nitrobenzene (shown) is treated with Sn (or Fe) and HCl, then made basic. What functional group is produced?","opts":["A carboxylic acid (-COOH)","A primary aromatic amine (-NH2)","An amide (-CONH2)","A hydroxyl group (-OH, phenol), from hydrolysis of the nitro group","A nitroso group (-N=O), from a partial reduction that stops one step short"],"correct":1,"why":"Sn/HCl (or Fe/HCl, H2/Pd, SnCl2) reduces an aromatic nitro group to a primary aromatic amine; nitrobenzene gives aniline. Standard nitro-to-amine reduction.","traps":null,"qs":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1g31xwm","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt). Remembering that E2 on a cyclohexane requires the leaving group and the beta-hydrogen to be trans-diaxial (anti-periplanar), which is the MAJOR alkene product?","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1C=CCCC1","CCOC1CCCCC1C","CC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"E2 needs Br axial with an anti-periplanar axial beta-H. In the trans-1,2 isomer, when Br is axial the methyl is also axial, so C2 has no axial H. Elimination must go toward C6, giving 3-methylcyclohexene (option A). The Zaitsev product 1-methylcyclohexene (option B) cannot form because the required C2-H is equatorial.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1x7pi97","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"An unknown gives a 1H NMR with three signals: a 3H singlet near 2.5 ppm and two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (~7.4 and ~8.1 ppm). Of the two candidate structures, the one drawn is p-nitrotoluene; the contrast candidate is tert-butanol (CC(C)(C)O). Which structure is consistent with the spectrum?","opts":["Neither structure fits, because p-nitrotoluene is para-disubstituted and would give four separate aromatic signals rather than two doublets","The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene): CH3 singlet plus two aromatic doublets from the para ring","The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene), except that its methyl signal must integrate for 9H rather than 3H against the two aromatic doublets","tert-Butanol, because its 9H singlet matches the 3H methyl peak","tert-Butanol, because it produces two aromatic doublets"],"correct":1,"why":"p-Nitrotoluene has a benzylic CH3 (3H singlet, ~2.5 ppm) and a para-disubstituted ring giving an AA'BB' pattern that reads as two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (the H ortho to NO2 shifted downfield ~8.1 ppm). tert-Butanol has no aromatic H and would show only a 9H singlet plus OH, so it cannot match.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1hta81k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with a strong base under E2 conditions. E2 requires the C-H and C-Br bonds to be anti-periplanar (both axial). Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","opts":["CC1CCCCC1O","CC1CCCC=C1","CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"In the trans isomer, when Br is axial the C2 methyl is equatorial, so the C2 hydrogen is equatorial and cannot be anti-periplanar to Br. The Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene is geometrically blocked; methylenecyclohexane and the unreacted/substitution products are wrong. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CCCCC1Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1boz2p7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Two keto acids are considered for thermal decarboxylation: the gamma-keto acid shown (4-oxopentanoic / levulinic acid) and 3-oxo-3-phenylpropanoic acid (a beta-keto acid). Which statement is correct?","opts":["Only the gamma-keto acid decarboxylates, because only its carbonyl and carboxyl are spaced to close a ring, and the product is a cyclic lactone rather than a simple ketone","The gamma-keto acid decarboxylates faster, because the extra distance between its carbonyl and its carboxyl relieves strain in the cyclic transition state that delivers the proton","Neither decarboxylates; a ketone cannot assist loss of CO2","Only the beta-keto acid decarboxylates readily on heating; the gamma-keto acid does not, because its carbonyl is too far for a six-membered cyclic transition state","Both decarboxylate equally well because both contain a ketone"],"correct":3,"why":"Facile thermal decarboxylation requires the second carbonyl to be beta to the carboxyl so a six-membered cyclic transition state can form. The beta-keto acid satisfies this; in the gamma-keto acid the ketone is one carbon too far, so no low-energy cyclic TS exists.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-qjs2wp","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane, drawn) is treated with sodium iodide in acetone. Which statement best explains the observed outcome?","opts":["It undergoes fast E2 elimination because acetone removes a beta-hydrogen, giving the more substituted Zaitsev alkene as the major product","It reacts rapidly by SN1 because the neighboring tert-butyl group stabilizes the primary carbocation by hyperconjugation from all three of its adjacent methyl groups","It reacts rapidly by SN2 because primary substrates always favor SN2","It rearranges to a tertiary iodide by a hydride shift from the neighboring quaternary carbon before iodide attacks, so substitution occurs at the rearranged center","The reaction is extremely slow because the adjacent quaternary carbon sterically blocks SN2 backside attack, even though the carbon bearing Br is primary"],"correct":4,"why":"Neopentyl is the textbook 'primary but SN2-resistant' case: the neighboring quaternary carbon's methyls block backside approach (Finkelstein/NaI-acetone are SN2 conditions). SN1 (option B) fails for a primary cation; option C ignores sterics; the carbon bearing Br has no beta-H positioned for E2 and NaI is not a base (option D); the carbocation/hydride-shift path (option E) does not operate under SN2 conditions.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)CBr","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-15myqzk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Benzamide (C6H5CONH2) is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","opts":["Cc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene while retaining the C-N bond, converting C(=O)NH2 into CH2NH2 to give benzylamine (NCc1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (C-N cleavage), aniline (wrong skeleton), benzaldehyde (incomplete), and toluene (full deoxygenation). Exactly one correct.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(N)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1k3iorm","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"On a silica-gel TLC plate developed in a nonpolar solvent, four compounds are spotted: octanoic acid (carboxylic acid), octylamine (the amine drawn), 1-octanol (alcohol), and octane (alkane). Which compound is expected to travel the FARTHEST up the plate (highest Rf)?","opts":["All four travel equally","Octane (the alkane)","1-Octanol (the alcohol)","Octanoic acid (the carboxylic acid)","Octylamine (the amine)"],"correct":1,"why":"On polar silica, the least polar compound interacts least with the stationary phase and moves with the nonpolar mobile phase, giving the highest Rf; nonpolar octane wins over the polar acid, amine, and alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCCCCCN","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-14hd2mz","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"1,3-Butadiene is heated with maleic anhydride (the dienophile shown). What is the major cycloaddition product?","opts":["O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2CCCCC12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21","O=C1OC(=O)C2C=CCCC12"],"correct":0,"why":"The electron-poor C=C of maleic anhydride is the dienophile; cycloaddition with butadiene builds a cyclohexene ring fused to the anhydride (cis-4-cyclohexene-1,2-dicarboxylic anhydride, C8H8O3) with the new alkene across the ring from the fusion carbons. Verified option A. Distractors: phthalic anhydride (aromatic), saturated (no alkene), alkene at wrong position (option D), wrong ring size (option E).","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1f5q9m8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A student attempts a Friedel-Crafts acylation on the molecule shown (aniline) using CH3COCl and AlCl3 and obtains essentially no ring-acylated product. What is the best explanation?","opts":["The basic -NH2 nitrogen complexes with the Lewis acid AlCl3, forming a strongly deactivating -NH2-AlCl3 group that shuts down EAS","Aniline is aromatic, and an aromatic ring cannot undergo electrophilic substitution without permanently losing its aromatic stabilization","The reaction gives only the meta product, which the student discarded","The amino group withdraws so much electron density from the ring that the aromatic system becomes too electron-poor for any electrophile to attack","Acetyl chloride is too unreactive to form an acylium ion"],"correct":0,"why":"FC reactions fail on aniline because the Lewis-basic amino N coordinates to AlCl3; the resulting positively charged -NH2-AlCl3 nitrogen strongly deactivates the ring (and consumes catalyst). The free -NH2 is normally a strong activator, so the ring is not inherently electron-poor.","traps":null,"qs":"Nc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1p3zjw7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"The product shown is alpha-methylstyrene (2-phenylpropene). Which carbonyl/ylide combination gives it via a Wittig reaction?","opts":["Acetophenone + Ph3P=O","Propiophenone (PhCOCH2CH3) + Ph3P=CH2","Acetone + Ph3P=CHPh","Benzaldehyde (PhCHO) + Ph3P=CHCH3","Acetophenone (PhCOCH3) + Ph3P=CH2"],"correct":4,"why":"The =CH2 comes from Ph3P=CH2 and the C(CH3)(Ph)= carbon from the carbonyl, requiring acetophenone. Benzaldehyde + ethylidene gives beta-methylstyrene (different connectivity); acetone + benzylidene gives PhCH=C(CH3)2; propiophenone gives a different alkene; Ph3P=O is a byproduct.","traps":null,"qs":"C=C(C)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-108s0sf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"The acid chloride shown is treated with excess dimethylamine, (CH3)2NH. What is the organic product of this nucleophilic acyl substitution?","opts":["CC(=O)N(C)C","CC(=O)O","CC(=O)N","CC(=O)OC(=O)C","CC(=O)OCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Acetyl chloride + secondary amine (CH3)2NH gives the N,N-disubstituted amide N,N-dimethylacetamide. Excess amine neutralizes HCl.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)Cl","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1iyt40m","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Optically pure (R)-3-phenylbutan-2-one (shown) is stirred in aqueous NaOH and then re-isolated. The alpha carbon bearing the phenyl group is the stereocenter. What is the stereochemical outcome and why?","opts":["The compound is destroyed by a haloform-type C-C cleavage at the stereocenter","A single diastereomer forms because a new stereocenter is created at the carbonyl carbon","The product is racemic (R and S in equal amounts) because base-promoted enolization at that alpha carbon makes it sp2 and planar, and reprotonation occurs equally on both faces","The (R) configuration is fully retained, because enolization occurs only toward the methyl group on the far side of the carbonyl, so the stereocenter is never flattened and never reprotonated","It is converted entirely to the (S) enantiomer, because hydroxide attacks the stereocenter from the face opposite the proton and displaces it with clean backside inversion of configuration"],"correct":2,"why":"The stereocenter is the alpha carbon and bears an alpha H. Aqueous NaOH (no halogen present) enolizes it, making it sp2/planar and erasing chirality; reprotonation on either face gives a 50:50 (R)/(S) racemate. option A correct. Not an SN2 inversion, no new stereocenter at the carbonyl, and haloform cleavage is impossible here because there is no halogen and the stereocenter is not a methyl-ketone CH3.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(C)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-11r58ig","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"2,2-Dimethylpropan-1-ol (neopentyl alcohol, shown) is treated with concentrated HBr. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)=CC","CC(C)(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"Neopentyl alcohol with HBr cannot easily do SN2 (hindered) or form a primary cation, so a 1,2-methyl shift gives a stable tertiary cation; bromide traps it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (option A). option B is the non-rearranged neopentyl bromide, option C a different rearranged skeleton, option D an alkene, option E the chloride. Neopentyl rearrangement is a classic textbook example.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)CO","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-150rzos","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"On a silica TLC plate, geraniol (shown) travels a distance of 2.0 cm while the solvent front travels 8.0 cm from the origin. What is the Rf value, and how would switching to a MORE POLAR solvent system affect it?","opts":["Rf = 0.75; a more polar solvent would decrease the Rf","Rf = 0.25; a more polar solvent would decrease the Rf","Rf = 0.40; a more polar solvent would not change the Rf","Rf = 4.0; a more polar solvent would increase the Rf","Rf = 0.25; a more polar solvent would increase the Rf"],"correct":4,"why":"Rf = spot distance / solvent-front distance = 2.0/8.0 = 0.25. A more polar mobile phase competes for the polar silica sites and elutes the polar (-OH) compound farther, increasing Rf.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)=CCCC(C)=CCO","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17kba84","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"3-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2, which adds anti across the triple bond. Which structure is the major product, including geometry?","opts":["CCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)CC","CCCC(Br)(Br)CC","CCC(Br)C(Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(\\Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(/Br)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"Anti Br2 addition places the two bromines trans. On each internal carbon Br>ethyl, so trans-Br => E. Others are over-brominated or saturated wrong skeletons. (E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene is the answer.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC#CCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-xzfj6l","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with (1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O then (2) NaBH4. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["OCC(C)(C)CC","CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(O)(C)C(C)C","OCC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration is Markovnikov with NO rearrangement because the bridged mercurinium ion blocks a free cation. OH adds to the more-substituted alkene carbon, leaving the t-butyl group intact, giving 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol (CC(O)C(C)(C)C). option B is the rearranged product you would only get under acid (the trap); option C is anti-Markovnikov; option D is a diol; option E is the wrong skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:psm3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A savings balance of $500 falls 40 percent and then grows 40 percent from that lower balance. What is the balance then?","opts":["$200","$300","$420","$500","$700"],"correct":2,"why":"×0.60 × 1.40 = ×0.84. $420. Big swings, so big asymmetry.","traps":["That is the 40 percent that came off, not the 60 percent that stayed.","That is the price after the 40 percent cut, one step short of the answer.",null,"You added minus 40 and plus 40 into no change, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That applies only the 40 percent rise and leaves the cut out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:cou1580","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"From 6 items, how many different ordered arrangements of 2 can be formed?","opts":["8","12","15","30","36"],"correct":3,"why":"nPr = 6!/(6-2)! = 30.","traps":["Added 6 and 2.","Multiplied 6 by 2 instead of 6 by 5.","That counts unordered pairs; when order matters each pair counts twice.",null,"Treated it as 6 choices for both slots, which allows the same item twice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1kcjvh","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$2000 is invested at 10% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 3 years?","opts":["$662.00","$2420.00","$2600.00","$2662.00","$2928.20"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 2000 × (1.1)³ = $2662.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1694","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A line passes through (-8,8) and (-5,5). What is its slope?","opts":["-3","-1","0","1","3"],"correct":1,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -3/3 = -1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1330","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"If -3x < 1, which inequality must be true of x?","opts":["x < -0.33","x = -0.33","x < 0.33","x > 0.33","x > -0.33"],"correct":4,"why":"Divide both sides by -3 (negative) and FLIP: x > -0.33.","traps":["You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative.","You solved the boundary equation -3x = 1 instead of the range of values.","You divided by 3 rather than -3, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.","You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 1/-3.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#rqbqco","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² + x − 20 = 0?","opts":["−1","1","−20","20","9"],"correct":0,"why":"It factors as (x − 4)(x + 5), so the roots are 4 and −5. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = −1, product = c/a = −20.","traps":[null,"Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a.","That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1261","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What value of x satisfies 9x + 3 = x - 8?","opts":["-2.38","-1.38","-0.63","-0.38","1.38"],"correct":1,"why":"Move the x terms together and the numbers together. Subtract 1x from both sides for 8x + 3 = -8, then subtract 3 for 8x = -11. Divide by 8 and x is -11/8, which is -1.375 and rounds to -1.38. Keep the fraction exact through the work and round only at the finish, so the rounding never leaks into an earlier step.","traps":["That is -19 divided by 8, but the constants combine to -11, not -19.",null,"The 3 was added to the -8 instead of subtracted, leaving 8x = -5.","That is -3 divided by 8, so the -8 on the right never made it across.","Dividing 11 by 8 gives 1.38, but 8x = -11 makes x negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:men1234","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A commute is 23 kilometers each way. How many kilometers is the round trip?","opts":["36","40","46","56","146"],"correct":2,"why":"(20 + 3) × 2 = 40 + 6","traps":["That is ten light, since 40 plus 6 is 46.","That doubles the 20 and leaves the 3 times 2 out.",null,"That is ten over, since 40 plus 6 is 46.","An extra hundred appeared, since 20 times 2 is 40 and 3 times 2 is 6."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1352","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A 36-mile delivery route is split between two drivers in a ratio of 2 to 7. How many miles does the shorter leg cover?","opts":["4","8","9","18","28"],"correct":1,"why":"Total parts = 9; one part = 36/9 = 4. Smaller = 2·4 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1303","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is the value of 3² + 6 × 5?","opts":["33","36","39","45","90"],"correct":2,"why":"Exponents go first, then multiplication, then addition: 3 squared is 9, 6 times 5 is 30, and 9 + 30 = 39. Watch the square itself, because 3 squared means 3 times 3, not 3 times 2. The line is two independent blocks that meet only at the final plus sign.","traps":["You dropped the square and used a plain 3, giving 3 + 30.","You read 3 squared as 3 times 2 = 6; squaring means 3 times 3 = 9.",null,"You added 3 and 6 first and then multiplied by 5, which skips both the square and the ranking.","You multiplied all three numbers and ignored the square entirely."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp005","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A right triangle has legs of length 3 and 4 (and hypotenuse 5). It is inscribed in a CIRCLE such that the hypotenuse is a diameter. What is the area of the region INSIDE the circle but OUTSIDE the triangle?","opts":["(25π/4) − 6","5π − 12","25π − 6","(25π/2) − 6","6 − (25π/4)"],"correct":0,"why":"Hypotenuse = diameter = 5, so radius = 2.5. Circle area = π(2.5)² = 25π/4. Triangle area = (1/2)(3)(4) = 6. Region = 25π/4 − 6.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#3jvdp0","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 5 red and 3 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["5/16","5/8","4/7","25/64","5/14"],"correct":4,"why":"First red: 5/8. Second red: 4/7. Multiply: 5/14.","traps":["You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too.","That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:vs3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":1,"q":"A sphere has a radius of 3, and its volume is (4/3)πr³. Approximating π as 3, what is its volume?","opts":["108","27","36","108π","9"],"correct":0,"why":"(4/3)(3)(27) = 4 × 27 = 108. (Or with π: 36π.)","traps":[null,"Cubed the radius and stopped, without the 4/3 or the π.","Left π out and stopped at 4/3 times 27.","Used 3 for π and then kept a π on the end as well, counting it twice.","Squared the radius and stopped there."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-mixture#1ytvgkf","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"3 gallons of a 20% acid solution is mixed with 2 gallons of a 80% acid solution. What is the concentration of the mixture?","opts":["36.7%","44%","50%","56%","220%"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid: 20% of 3 plus 80% of 2 = 2.2 gallons. Total volume = 5 gallons. Concentration = 44%.","traps":["You divided by the product of the volumes. Divide by the SUM: total volume.",null,"You averaged the two percents. The mixture leans toward whichever solution you used MORE of, so weight by volume.","You swapped the volumes. Pair each concentration with its own volume.","You found the total acid but never divided by the total volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1332","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"Which inequality is equivalent to -3x < 0?","opts":["x > 0","x ≥ 0","x < 0","x = 0","x ≤ 0"],"correct":0,"why":"Divide both sides by -3 (negative) and FLIP: x > 0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1378","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A boat runs at 30 miles per hour for 3.5 hours. How many miles does it travel?","opts":["8.57","75","90","105","135"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 30 × 3.5 = 105 mi.","traps":["Dividing 30 by 3.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 2.5 hours instead of 3.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 3 hours flat.",null,"Using 4.5 hours instead of the 3.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1660","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Three dowels measure 15, 7, and 7 centimeters. Can the three dowels form a triangle?","opts":["Only if right triangle","Only if it is obtuse","Cannot determine","Yes","No"],"correct":4,"why":"|15−7| = 8 and 15+7 = 22. 7 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["No angle rescues these lengths: 7 and 7 still cover only 14, short of 15.","Angles come after the sides close, and 7 plus 7 never reaches 15, so no obtuse version exists either.","Three side lengths are all the rule needs: 7 plus 7 is 14, and that has to beat 15.","The two shorter sides, 7 and 7, reach only 14, which cannot span the 15, so the ends never meet.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1203","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A walking route covers 712 meters of gravel and then 66 meters of boardwalk. How long is the route in meters?","opts":["720","770","772","778","820"],"correct":3,"why":"Work it out: 712 + 66 = 778. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That barely moves off 712, so the 66 never really went on.","That is 710 plus 60, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 778.","That adds the 60 and leaves the 6 out.",null,"That adds about 110, well past the 66 on the page."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pfl1515","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A clinic uses a fair coin flip to send each of five patients to one of two waiting rooms. What is the probability that exactly one flip lands heads?","opts":["0.03","0.08","0.16","0.31","0.84"],"correct":2,"why":"C(5,1) × (1/2)^5 = 5 × 0.0313 = 0.1563.","traps":["0.03 is the chance of one particular pattern, 1/32, and the single head has five places it can sit.","0.08 carries one extra factor of 1/2, which stretches the run to six flips instead of five.",null,"0.31 is the chance of exactly two heads, ten of the thirty-two patterns, while exactly one head has five of them.","0.84 is the complement, the chance of anything other than exactly one head."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1027","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"What is the sum of 9/15 and 10/14?","opts":["19/29","97/70","90/210","50/35","46/35"],"correct":4,"why":"LCD = 210. 9/15 = 126/210, 10/14 = 150/210. Combine: 276/210 = 46/35.","traps":["You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 19 over 29.","One of the rescalings slipped; in seventieths the pieces are 42 and 50, which total 92/70.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You multiplied each top by its own bottom instead of by the other fraction's bottom.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1160","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A $37 water bill rises 33 percent. What is the new bill?","opts":["4","12.21","38.22","49.21","70"],"correct":3,"why":"New = 37 × (1 + 33/100) = 37 × 1.33 = 49.21.","traps":["You subtracted 33 dollars from 37, and this price is rising rather than falling.","That is 33 percent of 37 on its own, the increase before it joins the price.","That multiplies by 1.033, a 3.3 percent rise; 33 percent means multiplying by 1.33.",null,"You added 33 dollars instead of 33 percent."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:le7","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies 5(2x − 4) = 30?","opts":["x = 2","x = 7","x = 1","x = 3","x = 5"],"correct":4,"why":"Distribute: 10x − 20 = 30. Add 20: 10x = 50. Divide: x = 5. (Or divide both sides by 5 first: 2x − 4 = 6 → 2x = 10, so x = 5.)","traps":["Substituting 2 gives 5(4 − 4) = 0, not 30.","Both terms got multiplied by 10, but the 4 only takes the 5 out front, giving 10x − 20 = 30.","Distributing gave 10x + 20, but 5 times −4 is −20.","Dividing by 5 first gives 2x − 4 = 6, and stopping at 2x = 6 skips adding the 4 back.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1127","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"What is 35 percent of 476?","opts":["16.66","166.6","309.4","511","16660"],"correct":1,"why":"35% of 476 = 35/100 × 476 = 166.6.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 65 percent left over rather than the 35 percent the question asks for.","You added the 35 to the 476 instead of taking 35 percent of it.","You multiplied 35 by 476 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1026","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A storeroom holds 6/15 of a pallet of gloves and 2/5 of a pallet of masks. How many pallets of stock is that?","opts":["4/5","8/15","12/75","8/20","1"],"correct":0,"why":"6/15 reduces to 2/5, so this is two fifths plus two fifths, which is 4/5. Spotting that the two fractions are the same size saves the whole conversion.","traps":[null,"You put 2/5 over 15 but left the 2 on top; two fifths is 6/15.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 8 over 20.","You reduced 6/15 to 3/5 instead of 2/5, which pushes the total up to a whole."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#y5q2s1","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 180 miles at 50 mph and returns the same 180 miles at 75 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["61.2 mph","68.8 mph","62.5 mph","30 mph","60 mph"],"correct":4,"why":"Time out = 180/50 = 3.6 h, time back = 180/75 = 2.4 h. Average = 360 / 6 = 60 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#6668h","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (3 × 10¹)(7 × 10¹) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["2.1 × 10³","2.1 × 10²","21 × 10²","2.1 × 10¹","1 × 10³"],"correct":0,"why":"3 × 7 = 21 and 10¹ × 10¹ = 10². 21 × 10² is not proper form, so write 2.1 × 10³. Answer: 2.1 × 10³.","traps":[null,"You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","Right size, wrong form. In proper scientific notation the front number sits between 1 and 10.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#1la5g4q","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 7 students, in how many ways can a committee of 2 be chosen?","opts":["2","14","21","42","49"],"correct":2,"why":"Order does not matter: 7C2 = 42 / 2! = 21.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 2 people. You still have to choose them from 7.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.",null,"You used a permutation. A committee has no positions, so order does NOT matter: divide out the orderings.","You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1417","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A kitchen mixes 200 mL of a 10% vinegar dressing with 250 mL of a 5% vinegar dressing. What is the approximate percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["7.22","7.5","7.78","32.5","33.75"],"correct":0,"why":"Solute total = 20 + 12.5 = 32.5 mL in 450 mL → 7.22%.","traps":[null,"Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.","Paired each percent with the other solution's volume, putting the 10 percent on the 250 mL pour.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture.","Took 7.5 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 450 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-missing-value-mean#1xxivpg","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A student scored 92, 71, 87, 72 on 4 quizzes. What score is needed on the next quiz to bring the average to exactly 83?","opts":["10","83","85","93","98"],"correct":3,"why":"Needed total = 83 × 5 = 415. Current total = 322. Next score = 415 − 322 = 93.","traps":["You used 4 quizzes. After the next one there are 5.","Scoring the target only works if you were already AT the target. You have to make up the gap on every earlier quiz too.","You averaged the old average with the new score. The old average counts for several quizzes, not one.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Required total = target × number of quizzes; subtract what you already have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1108","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A crate weighs 24.9 kilograms, and 1.1 kilograms of packing material is taken out. What does the crate weigh now in kilograms?","opts":["2.38","23.8","23.9","26.0","238"],"correct":1,"why":"Line the decimal points up and subtract: 9 tenths minus 1 tenth is 8 tenths, and 24 minus 1 is 23, so the answer is 23.8. Taking a little over 1 away from nearly 25 should land just under 24, which it does.","traps":["You placed the point one spot too far left; the gap between 24.9 and 1.1 is close to 24.",null,"You subtracted 1.0 rather than 1.1, leaving one tenth too much.","You added the two numbers instead of subtracting them.","You dropped the decimal point, which makes the answer ten times too big."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1638","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Daily visitors to a library average 100 with a standard deviation of 20. What is the z score of a day with 77 visitors?","opts":["-23","-1.15","-0.23","1.15","3.85"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (77 − 100)/20 = -1.15.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, stopping before the division by 20.",null,"Divided the 23-point gap by the mean of 100 instead of by the standard deviation of 20.","Right size, wrong side: 77 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative.","Divided 77 by 20 without first subtracting the mean."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1719","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"Expressed as a decimal, what is the value of 3^5 ÷ 3^6?","opts":["0.33","3","972","177147","205891132094649"],"correct":0,"why":"Same base, so the exponents subtract: 3^(5-6) = 3^-1. A negative exponent means one over that power, so this is 1/3, which rounds to 0.33. When the bottom exponent is the bigger one, expect an answer below 1.","traps":[null,"You subtracted the exponents the wrong way round, as 6 - 5 rather than 5 - 6.","You added 243 and 729 instead of dividing them.","You added the exponents instead of subtracting, which gives 3^11.","You multiplied the exponents, which gives 3^30."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1ntn2bl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"Two sides of a triangle measure 5 and 7, and the angle between them is 60 degrees. How long is the third side?","opts":["√109, about 10.4","√74, about 8.6","2","12","√39, about 6.2"],"correct":4,"why":"The Law of Cosines gives the third side squared as 25 plus 49 minus 2 times 5 times 7 times cos 60. Cos 60 is 1/2, so the subtracted piece is 70 times 1/2, which is 35, leaving 74 minus 35, or 39, and the side is √39, about 6.2. A 60 degree angle is narrower than a right angle, so the third side has to come out shorter than the √74 a right angle would give, which checks the sign of that last term for you.","traps":["This adds the 35 rather than subtracting it, which is what a 120 degree angle would produce.","This drops the cosine term entirely and treats the triangle as a right triangle.","This multiplies by 1 instead of by cos 60, leaving 74 minus 70.","This adds the two given sides, a length the third side can never reach.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#oj4q4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A car travels at 60 miles per hour. What is its speed in feet per second? (1 mile = 5280 ft)","opts":["40.9 ft/s","0.0167 ft/s","88 ft/s","5280 ft/s","3.17 × 10⁵ ft/s"],"correct":2,"why":"60 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi ÷ 3600 s/h = 88 ft/s. Handy anchor: 60 mph is 88 ft/s.","traps":["Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so miles and hours cancel.","That is miles per second. Multiply by 5280 to get feet.",null,"You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour.","That is feet per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1478","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"How many seconds are in 0.6 minutes?","opts":["0.01","3.6","6","36","60.6"],"correct":3,"why":"1 min = 60 sec, so 0.6 min × 60 = 36 sec.","traps":["You divided the 0.6 by 60 instead of multiplying; seconds are smaller, so the count has to grow.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You read the 0.6 as six seconds; six tenths of a minute is 36 seconds.",null,"You added 60 to the 0.6 instead of multiplying the 0.6 by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1371","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A moving truck averages 40 miles per hour for 4.5 hours. How many miles does it cover?","opts":["8.89","140","160","180","220"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 40 × 4.5 = 180 mi.","traps":["Dividing 40 by 4.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 3.5 hours instead of 4.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 4 hours flat.",null,"Using 5.5 hours instead of the 4.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fa8","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A tank holds 3/4 of a quart of coolant, and a technician drains 1/3 of a quart then adds 1/6 of a quart. What fraction of a quart is in the tank?","opts":["11/12","15/12","3/12","5/12","7/12"],"correct":4,"why":"LCD = 12. 9/12 − 4/12 + 2/12 = 7/12.","traps":["You flipped both signs after the first term, adding the 1/3 and subtracting the 1/6.","You added all three terms instead of subtracting the middle one.","You subtracted the last term instead of adding it, 9/12 minus 4/12 minus 2/12.","You stopped after 3/4 minus 1/3 and never added the last sixth.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp008","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An item is marked up 20% from its cost, then discounted 25% from the marked-up price. The final price is $90. What was the ORIGINAL cost?","opts":["$72","$90","$100","$108","$120"],"correct":2,"why":"Cost C, so markup 1.20C, so discount 0.75 × 1.20C = 0.90C. 0.90C = 90 → C = 100.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:bnm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"Two numbers have a sum of 7 and a product of 10. What is the sum of their squares?","opts":["19","29","39","49","69"],"correct":1,"why":"(x+y)² = x² + 2xy + y² → 49 = x² + y² + 20, so x² + y² = 29.","traps":["You subtracted xy one time too many, taking 49 down by 30 rather than 20.",null,"You subtracted xy once instead of 2xy.","That's (x+y)².","You added 2xy instead of subtracting it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1205","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A reservoir holds 2,600 cubic meters of water, and rainfall adds 48 cubic meters. How many cubic meters does it hold now?","opts":["2552","2600","2648","2650","2700"],"correct":2,"why":"Work it out: 2600 + 48 = 2648. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 2600 minus 48, so the plus got read as a minus.","That is the starting number, with the 48 never added.",null,"That is 2600 plus 50, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 2,648.","That rounds 48 up to 100, which overshoots by about 50."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261003"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s13"}},{"id":"FULL_4","name":"Full-Length Test 4","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"written:bio-4-ih7vz8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Some bacteria living without oxygen still use an electron transport chain, passing electrons to sulfate or nitrate instead of to oxygen. How does that differ from fermentation?","opts":["Fermentation uses no electron transport chain and builds no proton gradient","It produces less ATP per glucose than fermentation does","Fermentation uses a chain too, but with oxygen as the final acceptor","It is the same process under a different name","Fermentation passes electrons to nitrate, while anaerobic respiration passes them to pyruvate"],"correct":0,"why":"Both happen without oxygen, which is why students blur them together, but only one of them uses a membrane. Anaerobic respiration still runs electrons through a chain and still builds a proton gradient, just with a different molecule waiting at the end, so it out-earns fermentation. Fermentation has no chain and no gradient: it hands NADH's electrons to an organic molecule so that glycolysis can carry on.","traps":[null,"Running a chain and a gradient yields more ATP per glucose than fermentation, not less.","If oxygen were the final acceptor it would be aerobic respiration, and fermentation has no chain at all.","The two are distinct: one uses a membrane chain and a gradient while the other does not.","This has the two acceptors swapped, since fermentation uses pyruvate or a molecule made from it, and nitrate or sulfate belongs to anaerobic respiration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g033","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Roan cattle have coats containing both fully red hairs and fully white hairs intermixed, and they result from crossing red cattle with white cattle. This coat pattern is an example of:","opts":["Random X-inactivation, as in calico cats","Epistasis","Polygenic","Incomplete dominance","Codominance"],"correct":4,"why":"The test for codominance is whether the heterozygote displays both parental products at full strength rather than an average of them. Look closely at a roan coat and it is not pink: it is red hairs and white hairs side by side, each individual hair committed to one allele's product. Compare that with a pink snapdragon, where every cell makes an in-between amount of a single pigment, and the difference between codominance and incomplete dominance stops being slippery.","traps":["That mechanism creates the patchwork coat of calico cats and applies to X-linked genes in females, while roan appears in bulls and cows alike.","Epistasis requires a second gene masking the first, and a single coat color gene accounts for this cross.","Polygenic inheritance produces a smooth range of shades across many offspring, rather than two discrete hair colors within one animal.","Incomplete dominance would give one uniform intermediate shade across the whole coat, not two distinct hair colors on the same animal.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-i1qexy","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A researcher raises the outside glucose concentration step by step and measures how fast glucose enters a muscle cell. Uptake rises at first, then levels off at a maximum rate. What does that plateau tell you?","opts":["The cell has begun pumping glucose back out at an equal rate","Glucose crossing has switched over to active transport","The carrier proteins are saturated, so transport has a ceiling","The membrane became impermeable to glucose at high concentration","Glucose is entering by simple diffusion straight through the lipid bilayer"],"correct":2,"why":"A plateau is the fingerprint of a finite number of protein binding sites. Simple diffusion climbs in a straight line with the gradient because there is nothing to fill up, while a carrier has a limited number of seats, and once every seat is occupied, adding more glucose cannot speed anything up. Curve flattens means protein involved, whether the transport is facilitated or active.","traps":["Nothing in the data shows efflux, and growing efflux would bend the curve downward rather than flatten it.","Glucose entry down its gradient needs no energy input, and changing modes would not by itself create a ceiling.",null,"Membranes do not switch off their permeability when solute concentration rises.","Simple diffusion gives a straight line with no ceiling, since there is no protein to saturate."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-158brxy","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Two turns of the Krebs cycle, the amount one glucose supports, produce 6 NADH and 2 FADH2. Using the modern estimates of about 2.5 ATP per NADH and about 1.5 ATP per FADH2, how much ATP does oxidative phosphorylation get from those carriers alone?","opts":["12","15","18","20","24"],"correct":2,"why":"Run the two carriers on separate lines, then add. Six NADH at 2.5 each is 15, two FADH2 at 1.5 each is 3, and 15 plus 3 is 18. Keeping NADH and FADH2 on separate lines is what protects you from the most common slip here, which is quietly giving them the same value.","traps":["This gives every carrier the FADH2 value of 1.5, so all eight are counted at the lower rate.","This counts the 6 NADH correctly but leaves the 2 FADH2 out of the total entirely.",null,"This uses the older textbook values of 3 ATP per NADH and 1 ATP per FADH2.","This applies 3 ATP per NADH and then credits FADH2 with that same higher value."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-xtwkrj","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Under which leaf conditions does RuBisCO most often grab oxygen instead of carbon dioxide?","opts":["Cool, damp weather with the stomata wide open and carbon dioxide flowing in freely","Any time the carbon dioxide level in the air climbs above normal","Darkness, once the light reactions have stopped for the night","Early spring, while the leaf is still building its chloroplasts","A hot, dry day with the stomata closed and oxygen building up inside the leaf"],"correct":4,"why":"Follow the gases rather than the weather. Heat and drought make a leaf close its stomata, which traps the oxygen the light reactions keep producing and starves the leaf of carbon dioxide, and RuBisCO binds whichever gas is around. Picture a sealed room slowly filling with the gas you did not want.","traps":["Open stomata keep carbon dioxide high inside the leaf, which is exactly when RuBisCO chooses carbon dioxide.","More carbon dioxide means less photorespiration, since the two gases compete for the same site.","Photorespiration needs the Calvin cycle running, and in the dark RuBP is no longer being regenerated.","Chloroplast development has nothing to do with which gas the enzyme binds.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1v1yzon","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Which molecule of ATP or GTP in cellular respiration is made with no help at all from the proton gradient?","opts":["The ATP made when NADH is oxidized at complex I","The GTP made as succinyl-CoA becomes succinate","The ATP made as protons spin the rotor of ATP synthase","The ATP made when cytochrome c passes electrons onward","The ATP made as oxygen is reduced to water at the end of the chain"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the split between substrate-level phosphorylation and oxidative phosphorylation. In substrate-level phosphorylation a phosphate moves straight from a high-energy substrate onto ADP or GDP, with no membrane required, and the Krebs cycle does it once per turn at the succinyl-CoA step. Glycolysis uses the same trick twice in its payoff phase, which is why glycolysis still works when the mitochondria are shut down.","traps":["Complex I oxidizing NADH pumps protons, so any ATP that follows still rides on the gradient.",null,"This is the definition of chemiosmosis, where the gradient is the entire energy source.","Cytochrome c moving electrons between complexes drives proton pumping rather than making ATP directly.","Reducing oxygen is the last electron handoff, and the energy released there goes into the gradient."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In the nitrogen cycle, the process of nitrogen fixation converts:","opts":["Organic to inorganic","NH₃ to NO₃⁻","NH₄⁺ to amino acids in plant tissue","N₂ to NH₃","NO₃⁻ to N₂"],"correct":3,"why":"Nitrogen gas makes up most of the air, but the triple bond in N₂ puts it out of reach for nearly every organism, so the cycle has to start by breaking that bond. Fixation is that step: bacteria convert N₂ into ammonia, a form plants can actually use. Keep the order straight by following the atom: fixation brings nitrogen in from the air, nitrification oxidizes ammonia to nitrite and then nitrate, assimilation builds it into tissue, ammonification releases it from dead material, and denitrification returns it to the atmosphere as N₂.","traps":["This describes decomposition and ammonification, which recycle nitrogen that is already inside living things.","This is nitrification, carried out by soil bacteria after fixation has already delivered the nitrogen.","This is assimilation, the step where a plant builds already fixed nitrogen into its own molecules.",null,"This is denitrification, which sends nitrogen back to the atmosphere instead of bringing it in."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-2vt0fz","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"A leaf's photosynthetic rate climbs as you raise the light intensity, then flattens out even as the light keeps getting brighter. What does that flat stretch tell you?","opts":["Oxygen has built up and is now blocking the photosystems directly","Something other than light, such as carbon dioxide, now sets the ceiling","Chlorophyll has been permanently bleached by the bright light","The light reactions have stopped and only the Calvin cycle is still running","The leaf has switched from linear electron flow to cyclic flow"],"correct":1,"why":"A plateau in any rate curve means you have stopped adjusting the scarce thing. Once every photosystem is already busy, adding photons changes nothing, and the ceiling is set by carbon dioxide supply, temperature or the amount of enzyme present. Ask what is in shortest supply and the graph reads itself.","traps":["Oxygen does compete with carbon dioxide at RuBisCO, but it does not block the photosystems and it is not what caps the rate here.",null,"Bleaching would send the rate downward rather than hold it steady on a plateau.","The Calvin cycle cannot run for long without the ATP and NADPH that the light reactions supply.","Cyclic flow changes what gets produced, but a plateau points to a supply ceiling somewhere else."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1ewpgtm","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"What do carotenoids do for a leaf?","opts":["They split water once chlorophyll a is saturated with light","They carry protons across the thylakoid membrane into the lumen","They reflect blue light, which is what gives autumn leaves their orange color","They absorb wavelengths chlorophyll misses and drain off excess energy","They sit in the reaction center and pass electrons to plastoquinone"],"correct":3,"why":"Accessory pigments hold down two jobs, and most students remember only the first. They widen the band of wavelengths the antenna can harvest, and they also soak up energy that would otherwise damage the photosystem on a blazing day. Think of them as a wider net and a safety valve at the same time.","traps":["Water splitting happens at the manganese containing complex of photosystem II, never at an accessory pigment.","Proton pumping is the job of the electron carriers in the membrane, not of a pigment.","Carotenoids absorb blue light and reflect yellow and orange, which is why those colors appear once chlorophyll breaks down in autumn.",null,"The reaction center is chlorophyll a, and carotenoids feed energy into the antenna system instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1oxgc4o","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"A cell is bathed in a solution containing all of the substances below at equal concentration. Which one crosses the plasma membrane fastest by simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer itself?","opts":["Carbon dioxide","Sodium ion","A small globular protein","Chloride ion","Glucose"],"correct":0,"why":"This asks what the oily core of the bilayer lets through on its own, with no protein involved. Small and nonpolar slips between the fatty acid tails, while charged or bulky polar things are turned away and need a channel or carrier. Rank any candidate by size and polarity first: gases and small nonpolar molecules sit at the top of that list every time.","traps":[null,"Ions carry charge and drag a shell of water with them, so they need a channel rather than the bare bilayer.","A protein is far too large to slip between phospholipids and enters by vesicle if at all.","Chloride is charged and is repelled by the hydrophobic core, so it crosses through channels only.","Glucose is polar and bulky, so it needs a carrier protein even though it moves down its gradient."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1ae3if3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"The chromatids have finished arriving at the two poles of the cell. Which set of events comes next, before the cytoplasm is divided?","opts":["A fresh round of DNA replication begins at each of the spindle poles","Chromosomes condense further while the nucleoli disassemble","Kinetochores capture microtubules coming from both of the spindle poles","Cohesin is cleaved at the centromere so the sister chromatids can separate","Nuclear envelopes reform, chromosomes decondense, nucleoli reappear"],"correct":4,"why":"Telophase is prophase run backward. The envelope rebuilds around each cluster of chromosomes, the chromosomes unwind so their genes can be read again, the nucleoli reappear as ribosome production restarts, and the spindle comes apart. Cytokinesis overlaps with it but is a separate event, dividing the cytoplasm rather than the nucleus. If you can describe prophase, you already know telophase in reverse.","traps":["Replication happens in S phase of the next interphase, only after the cell has divided and passed its G1 checks.","Condensing and losing the nucleoli is prophase, the opening of mitosis rather than its close.","Kinetochore capture belongs to prometaphase, back when the chromosomes still had to be gathered to the middle.","Cohesin cleavage is what started anaphase, so it has already happened by the time the chromatids reach the poles.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p092","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the basic repeating structural unit of compact bone?","opts":["Perforating (Volkmann) canals","Lacunae","Lamellae","Osteon (Haversian system)","Trabeculae"],"correct":3,"why":"Compact bone is organized around its blood supply. Each osteon is a cylinder of concentric lamellae built around a central Haversian canal carrying a vessel and a nerve, with osteocytes sitting in lacunae between the rings and reaching one another through canaliculi. Ask which structure contains all of the others and the choice naming the osteon is the repeating unit, while spongy bone uses trabeculae instead.","traps":["Perforating canals run crosswise to connect the central canals of neighboring osteons, carrying vessels in from the periosteum.","Lacunae are the small spaces in the matrix where individual osteocytes sit, linked to each other by canaliculi.","Lamellae are the concentric rings of matrix inside an osteon, which makes them a component of the unit rather than the unit itself.",null,"Trabeculae are the strut network of spongy bone, arranged along lines of stress rather than into cylinders."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g014","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A cell with a diploid number of 2n = 8 undergoes meiosis. How many chromosomes does each resulting gamete contain?","opts":["It cannot be determined without knowing how many crossovers occurred","8","16","2","4"],"correct":4,"why":"Meiosis runs one round of DNA replication followed by two divisions, which halves the chromosome number exactly once. If 2n equals 8, then n equals 4, so every normal gamete carries 4 chromosomes. When a stem hands you 2n, write n beside it before you look at the choices and the arithmetic stops being a trap.","traps":["Crossing over swaps segments between homologs and changes which alleles travel together, but it never changes how many chromosomes a gamete receives.","This is the parent cell's diploid count, which is what you get if the halving step is left out.","This doubles instead of halving; replication in S phase makes sister chromatids, not extra chromosomes.","This divides by four, probably because meiosis yields four cells, but four products does not mean four halvings of the chromosome number.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-1275ae4cf5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which change would increase the conduction speed of an axon the most?","opts":["More neurotransmitter stored in the terminal","A smaller axon diameter together with a myelin sheath","A larger axon diameter with the myelin removed","A larger axon diameter together with a myelin sheath","A more negative resting potential"],"correct":3,"why":"Two levers set speed. A wide axon is a wide pipe with low internal resistance. Myelin stops current from leaking out and lets the signal skip between nodes. Pull both levers and you get the fastest axon.","traps":["That affects the synapse, not the speed along the axon.","A narrow axon has more internal resistance, which slows current. Bigger is faster.","Losing myelin forces the signal to regenerate at every patch of membrane, which is slow.",null,"That moves the cell further from threshold. It does not speed the signal along the axon."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g036","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a dihybrid cross showing dominant epistasis, where a dominant allele at one gene masks the expression of a second gene, what F2 phenotypic ratio is expected?","opts":["9:3:3:1","13:3","12:3:1","9:3:4","15:1"],"correct":2,"why":"Work from 9:3:3:1 again and ask which classes become indistinguishable. Under dominant epistasis, every genotype carrying the masking dominant allele shows the same phenotype, so the 9 and one of the 3 classes fuse into 12 and the ratio reads 12:3:1. Use the leading number as your tell: a 12 means a dominant allele is doing the hiding, while a 4 means a homozygous recessive genotype is.","traps":["That is the baseline ratio for two genes acting independently, with neither one masking the other.","This is dominant suppression, in which three of the four classes collapse into one, a different interaction from straightforward dominant epistasis.",null,"The 4 in that ratio signals that a homozygous recessive genotype is doing the masking, which is recessive epistasis.","That is duplicate dominant gene action, where a dominant allele at either gene alone is enough to produce the same phenotype."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-3vie62","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Under the microscope, a dividing cell shows a single tight line of chromosomes across the middle of the cell, each one gripped from both sides. Which stage is this, and what holds the chromosomes in that line?","opts":["Interphase, held by the cell plate that is forming across the equator","Metaphase, held by balanced pull from microtubules reaching to each pole","Prophase, held together by a nuclear envelope that has not yet broken down","Telophase, held by the two nuclear envelopes that are reassembling nearby","Anaphase, held in place by the contractile ring forming around the equator"],"correct":1,"why":"Metaphase is defined by the balance, not only by the position. Each chromosome is attached on one face to microtubules from one pole and on the other face to microtubules from the other pole, and the plate is exactly where those two pulls come out even. That balance is also what the spindle checkpoint is measuring. When you see one tidy line with attachments on both sides, that is metaphase every time.","traps":["Interphase chromosomes are decondensed and invisible as separate bodies, and a cell plate belongs to plant cytokinesis after mitosis is over.",null,"In prophase the chromosomes are condensed but scattered inside an intact nucleus, with nothing yet gripping them.","In telophase the chromosomes are clustered at the two poles and unwinding, not lined up across the middle.","In anaphase the chromatids have already left the middle and are on their way to the poles, so you would see two groups rather than one line."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g077","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Early spontaneous abortion is most often associated with which kind of genetic abnormality?","opts":["Inversions","Point mutations","Trisomies","Triploidy, from two sperm fertilizing one egg","Translocations"],"correct":2,"why":"Most early pregnancy losses with an identified genetic cause are chromosomal, and among those the autosomal trisomies are the largest group. Think about why: an extra whole chromosome unbalances the dosage of hundreds of genes at once, which is far more disruptive than a single base change. When a stem asks what is most often behind early loss, reach for whole chromosome errors first.","traps":["An inversion keeps all of the genetic material present, so carriers are often completely unaffected.","A single base change usually affects one protein, which is rarely enough on its own to end a pregnancy.",null,"Triploidy does cause pregnancy loss, but it accounts for far fewer cases than the autosomal trisomies as a group.","Translocations do cause loss, particularly when a balanced carrier makes unbalanced gametes, but they account for far fewer cases than trisomies."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p018","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which ion binds troponin to start muscle contraction?","opts":["Ca²⁺","Cl-","Na+","K+","Mg²⁺"],"correct":0,"why":"This is the trigger question. Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin, troponin changes shape and drags tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites on actin, and the heads can then attach and pull. Keep the two ion jobs separate: sodium and potassium carry the action potential along the membrane, while calcium converts that electrical signal into an actual pull.","traps":[null,"Chloride helps stabilize the resting membrane and has no role in uncovering the myosin binding sites.","Sodium entry carries the action potential along the muscle fiber membrane, which is the arriving signal rather than the trigger inside the cell.","Potassium leaving the cell repolarizes the membrane after the impulse, ending the signal rather than starting the pull.","Magnesium partners with ATP in the myosin head's chemistry, but the binding site on troponin is built for calcium."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1cnibqa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell holds at the boundary between metaphase and anaphase and will not proceed. What is the checkpoint at that boundary actually monitoring?","opts":["That every kinetochore is attached to microtubules from opposite poles","That the DNA has finished replicating and carries no unrepaired damage","That the cleavage furrow has already begun pinching the cytoplasm inward","That the cell has grown large enough and has the nutrients to divide","That the nuclear envelope has completely reassembled around both sets of chromosomes"],"correct":0,"why":"Match each checkpoint to the disaster it prevents. The spindle checkpoint exists to keep a daughter cell from getting the wrong number of chromosomes, so the only thing it can be watching is attachment. Any kinetochore still unattached keeps sending a wait signal, and anaphase begins only when the last one is captured with pull coming from both poles. G1 asks about resources, G2 asks about the copy, and this one asks about the grip.","traps":[null,"That is what the G2 checkpoint asks, and by metaphase the replication question has long since been settled.","Cytokinesis follows chromosome separation rather than permitting it, and a furrow forming early would cut the cell before the chromosomes were sorted.","Size and nutrients are the G1 checkpoint's business, because that is where the cell decides whether to commit to a whole division.","The nuclear envelope reassembles during telophase, which comes after anaphase, so it cannot be a condition for starting anaphase."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d036","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is classified in which fungal phylum?","opts":["Glomeromycota","Basidiomycota","Zygomycota","Ascomycota","Chytridiomycota"],"correct":3,"why":"Yeasts are ascomycetes, the sac fungi, named for the ascus, the small sac in which their sexual spores form. The familiar members are the practical hooks: baker's and brewer's yeast, Penicillium, morels and truffles. Yeast is unusual for a fungus in being single celled and reproducing mostly by budding, so do not let the missing mushroom shape push you toward another group. Pair ascus with sac and basidium with club, and the two large phyla stay separate.","traps":["Glomeromycetes are the arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi that grow inside plant roots and do not live as free single cells.","Basidiomycetes are the club fungi, the mushrooms, puffballs, shelf fungi and rusts, which make spores on basidia.","Zygomycetes are the bread molds such as Rhizopus, which form a thick-walled zygospore when two mating types fuse.",null,"Chytrids are mostly aquatic fungi with flagellated spores, the only fungi that swim."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1d8y656","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"How many turns of the Calvin cycle does a chloroplast need to build one six carbon glucose?","opts":["Six","Two","Eighteen","Twelve","Three"],"correct":0,"why":"Each turn fixes exactly one carbon, and glucose holds six of them. The longer route to the same answer: three turns release one exportable G3P, and two G3P join to form glucose, so double the three. Counting carbons in against carbons out settles every Calvin cycle bookkeeping question.","traps":[null,"Two is the number of G3P molecules that combine into glucose, not the number of turns.","Eighteen is the ATP bill for one glucose, not a count of turns.","Twelve is how many G3P molecules are made across those turns, and most of them go straight back into RuBP.","Three turns yields a single G3P, which is only half a glucose."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In a vertebrate embryo, the notochord signals to the ectoderm lying directly above it. What does that signal induce?","opts":["Somites","Limb buds","The lens of the eye","Neural tube","Heart"],"correct":3,"why":"Induction means one tissue instructing a neighboring tissue about what to become, and the notochord is the textbook example. It runs down the midline beneath the dorsal ectoderm and signals that strip to thicken into the neural plate, which then folds up into the neural tube that becomes the brain and spinal cord. Remember the pairing as the notochord speaking upward to ectoderm, and note that the notochord itself mostly disappears, leaving the nucleus pulposus inside each intervertebral disc.","traps":["Somites are blocks of paraxial mesoderm that form beside the notochord on a segmentation clock, and while the notochord helps pattern them it does not call them into existence.","Limb buds arise from lateral plate mesoderm and are patterned by their own signaling centers rather than by midline induction.","The lens is a famous induction as well, but the inducer there is the optic vesicle reaching out from the forebrain and touching the overlying surface ectoderm.",null,"The heart forms from lateral plate mesoderm under its own signals, well away from the midline rod of the notochord."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Whales have small pelvic bones embedded in the body wall that are not attached to any hind limb. The presence of such vestigial structures is evidence for which of the following?","opts":["Convergent evolution","Structures shrink through disuse during an animal's lifetime and the reduction is passed to its offspring","Common ancestry","Ongoing adaptation","Random mutation"],"correct":2,"why":"A vestigial structure is a reduced version of something that was fully functional in an ancestor, and the only reason it is still present is that it was inherited. Whale pelvic bones make sense if whales descend from four limbed land mammals, a conclusion that fossils such as Ambulocetus and shared skeletal anatomy support independently. Whenever a structure exists in a form that does not match any current job, think inheritance from an ancestor in which it did.","traps":["Convergent evolution explains similar structures in unrelated lineages doing the same job, and a reduced bone with no locomotor job is a different situation entirely.","Changes an animal acquires during its own lifetime are not written back into its DNA, so disuse in one generation cannot be inherited by the next.",null,"Adaptation describes a trait being shaped for a role it plays now, while a vestigial structure is a reduced remnant of an ancestral one.","Mutation supplies the raw variation evolution works with, but on its own it does not explain why a whale carries a reduced version of a land mammal's hip."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p046","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The central chemoreceptors in the medulla oblongata adjust breathing rate primarily in response to changes in which of the following?","opts":["Arterial blood pressure","O₂","Temperature","CO₂ / pH","Glucose"],"correct":3,"why":"This asks what sets your breathing rate minute to minute. Carbon dioxide diffuses from blood into cerebrospinal fluid, forms carbonic acid, and releases hydrogen ions, and the central chemoreceptors read that pH drop and speed breathing up until the excess is blown off. Oxygen is the backup signal, watched by the carotid and aortic bodies, so the choice naming carbon dioxide and pH is the primary driver.","traps":["Pressure is read by baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch, which feed the cardiovascular centers rather than setting respiratory rate.","Oxygen is monitored by the peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies, and only once it falls quite low.","The hypothalamus handles core temperature; the medullary respiratory centers are tuned to the chemistry of the fluid around them.",null,"Glucose sensing belongs to the pancreatic islet cells and the hypothalamus, and it drives insulin and glucagon rather than breathing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-9webrs","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A human somatic cell has 46 chromosomes while it sits in G1. Consider that same cell at three moments: the end of G1, the end of G2, and the instant after anaphase has separated the sister chromatids but before the cytoplasm divides. How many chromosomes are present at each of those three moments?","opts":["46, then 92, then 46","46, then 46, then 92","46, then 92, then 92","23, then 46, then 92","92, then 92, then 46"],"correct":1,"why":"The trap is counting chromatid arms and calling them chromosomes. Count centromeres instead: after S phase the cell holds twice the DNA but still has 46 centromeres, so still 46 chromosomes, each now with two chromatids. The moment anaphase pulls those centromeres apart, every chromatid becomes a chromosome in its own right, so one undivided cell briefly holds 92. Cytokinesis then hands 46 to each daughter.","traps":["This makes the same G2 error and then halves too soon, since the count per cell only returns to 46 once cytokinesis splits the cytoplasm.",null,"This counts chromatids at the end of G2, but replication leaves the two copies joined at a single centromere, so they still count as one chromosome.","23 is the haploid number that belongs to a gamete after meiosis, not to a somatic cell at any point in the ordinary cell cycle.","This treats G1 as already replicated, and a G1 cell carries 46 chromosomes of one chromatid each."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-oggnpm","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Exactly one reaction in glycolysis reduces NAD+ to NADH. Which one?","opts":["1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate","Glucose to glucose-6-phosphate","Phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate split into two three-carbon sugars","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate"],"correct":4,"why":"You are being asked to find the oxidation step. Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate is oxidized as an inorganic phosphate is attached, and the electrons pulled off land on NAD+. Look for the aldehyde turning into an acyl phosphate: that is where the electrons leave, and because one glucose gives two of these molecules, one glucose makes two NADH here.","traps":["This step hands a phosphate to ADP, so what it makes is ATP rather than NADH.","This is the opening phosphorylation, which spends ATP and moves no electrons.","This is the final step of the pathway, and it also makes ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation.","This is the cut made by aldolase, a rearrangement with no oxidation involved.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-p9zot1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cytotoxic T cell presses Fas ligand against the Fas receptor on the surface of a target cell, and the target cell dies by apoptosis. Which pathway did that use, and which caspase is switched on first?","opts":["The extrinsic pathway, working through a death receptor, activating caspase-8","The intrinsic pathway, working through a death receptor, activating caspase-3 first","Neither pathway, since Fas binding produces necrosis rather than apoptosis","The intrinsic pathway, working through the mitochondrion, activating caspase-9","The extrinsic pathway, working through cytochrome c release, activating caspase-9"],"correct":0,"why":"Sort these by where the signal comes from. A ligand binding a death receptor on the outside of the cell is the extrinsic pathway, and it activates caspase-8 right at the membrane. Trouble arising inside the cell, such as DNA damage that cannot be repaired, is the intrinsic pathway, working through the mitochondrion and caspase-9. Both funnel into the same executioner caspases, so the only thing you have to hold separate is the trigger and the initiator.","traps":[null,"Death receptors do sit at the start, but that is what makes it extrinsic, and caspase-3 is an executioner activated further down either route.","Fas signaling is a controlled program with every hallmark of apoptosis, not the uncontrolled rupture of necrosis.","The intrinsic pathway begins with a problem inside the cell, and this signal arrived from outside through a surface receptor.","The pathway name is right and cytochrome c belongs to the other route, because the extrinsic pathway starts at the membrane rather than the mitochondrion."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-194ncsd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"An intestinal cell is treated with a drug that completely blocks the sodium potassium ATPase. Within minutes, glucose uptake across the apical membrane by the sodium glucose cotransporter falls off sharply. What is the best explanation?","opts":["The apical membrane depolarizes, which closes the carrier","The sodium gradient that powers the cotransporter runs down","The drug binds the glucose carrier directly and jams its binding site","Glucose is no longer phosphorylated once it arrives inside the cell","The cotransporter needs ATP, and the drug has cut off its supply"],"correct":1,"why":"The real question is what secondary active transport runs on. The cotransporter never touches ATP: it lets sodium fall downhill and drags glucose uphill with it, and that sodium gradient is built by the pump. Block the pump, sodium piles up inside, the gradient flattens, and the free ride ends. Any time a transporter is labeled secondary active, look upstream for the pump that stocked its gradient.","traps":["Voltage shifts here are small next to the loss of the sodium gradient itself.",null,"The stem says the drug acts on the pump, and the delay of a few minutes points to a gradient draining rather than direct inhibition.","Trapping glucose by phosphorylation keeps the glucose gradient favorable, but it is not the energy source for this carrier.","Secondary active transporters do not hydrolyze ATP themselves, that is the defining difference from primary ones."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-abf0966668","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"During the absolute refractory period no stimulus, however strong, can fire a second action potential. Why?","opts":["The K+ channels are closed","The voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated","The cell has run out of Na+","Neurotransmitter has been used up at the synapse","The Na+/K+ pump has stopped working"],"correct":1,"why":"Think of the inactivation gate as a door that locks itself after use. Until the membrane gets back down near rest, the lock stays on and no amount of stimulus can open those Na+ channels. That lock is also why the signal travels one way down the axon.","traps":["Closed K+ channels would not stop a spike, and for most of this window (the falling phase) the voltage-gated K+ channels are actually OPEN. The block is on the Na+ side.",null,"Only a tiny fraction of the ions move during one spike. The gradients are barely dented.","That is a synapse issue. The refractory period is a property of the axon membrane channels.","The pump keeps running the whole time. It is not what gates excitability."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"originals:ab-b115107d15","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Epinephrine is classified as which type of hormone, and how does it signal?","opts":["A steroid that binds a receptor inside the nucleus","An amino acid derivative that crosses the membrane like thyroid hormone","A peptide made of many amino acids that enters the cell through a channel","An amino acid derivative that binds surface receptors","A steroid released from the adrenal cortex"],"correct":3,"why":"Both epinephrine and thyroid hormone come from tyrosine, but they behave differently. Epinephrine is water soluble, so it binds a surface receptor and a second messenger such as cAMP carries the signal. That is why fight or flight switches on in seconds.","traps":["Epinephrine is not made from cholesterol. It is water soluble and stays outside the cell.","Thyroid hormone is the lipid soluble exception. Epinephrine behaves like a peptide and uses surface receptors.","It is derived from a SINGLE amino acid and it does not enter the cell.",null,"Epinephrine comes from the adrenal MEDULLA and is not a steroid."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e043","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In speciation, what does the process called reinforcement describe?","opts":["Random drift","Identical mating behavior","Hybrid sterility decreasing","Selection favoring the hybrids until the two populations merge back into one","Reduced fitness in hybrids, which strengthens prezygotic isolation"],"correct":4,"why":"When two diverging populations meet again and their hybrids do poorly, any individual that avoids mating across the line leaves more surviving offspring than one that does not. Selection therefore builds up traits that prevent the cross from happening in the first place, such as different songs, different flowering times or different courtship displays. Reinforcement is prezygotic isolation being strengthened precisely because postzygotic isolation is already costly.","traps":["Drift is random change in allele frequencies, while reinforcement is selection responding to a real cost paid by hybrids.","Identical courtship behavior would make interbreeding more likely, which is the opposite of what reinforcement builds up.","Reinforcement does not repair the hybrids, it reduces how often they are produced, so hybrid sterility is not what changes.","If hybrids did well, the two populations could blend back into one, which is the alternative outcome when reinforcement does not take hold.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-x1d1j6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Two cardiac muscle cells are joined so that ions and small molecules pass directly from the cytoplasm of one into the cytoplasm of the other. The structure responsible is:","opts":["A plasmodesma","A tight junction","A gap junction","A desmosome","A hemidesmosome"],"correct":2,"why":"The question turns on the word directly: no ligand, no receptor, just a pore joining two cytoplasms. Gap junctions are those pores in animal tissue, and they are why cardiac muscle contracts as one unit. Tight junctions seal the space between cells and desmosomes rivet cells together, so neither one passes a signal.","traps":["Plasmodesmata do exactly this job, but they are found in plant cells, and cardiac muscle is animal tissue.","Tight junctions seal the space between cells to stop leakage, they do not pass molecules between cytoplasms.",null,"Desmosomes are mechanical rivets that hold cells together against stress.","Hemidesmosomes anchor a cell to the extracellular matrix below it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e013","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Dogs that repeatedly heard a bell just before receiving food eventually began to salivate at the sound of the bell alone. This result illustrates:","opts":["Habituation","An unlearned reflex present from birth","Insight","Operant conditioning","Classical conditioning"],"correct":4,"why":"Classical conditioning takes a reflex the animal already has and attaches it to a new trigger. Food causes salivation with no learning at all, so food is the unconditioned stimulus and that salivation is the unconditioned response. Pairing the bell with food often enough turns the bell into a conditioned stimulus that draws out the same salivation, now a conditioned response. The test is that the animal is passive: nothing it chooses to do changes the outcome, and that is what separates this from operant conditioning.","traps":["Habituation is a response fading away to a repeated harmless stimulus, the opposite of a new response being built.","Salivating to food is the unlearned reflex, but salivating to a bell appears only after the bell has been paired with food, which makes it learned.","Insight is sudden problem solving without trial and error, and a reflex response to a tone is not problem solving.","Operant conditioning requires the animal's own action to produce a consequence, but the dogs were not doing anything to earn the food.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1u7pjce","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"The very first thing a cell does to glucose is attach a phosphate to it. What does that accomplish?","opts":["It supplies the phosphate that is eventually handed to oxygen","It gives the sugar a charge so it cannot leave the cell","It tags the sugar for transport into the mitochondrion","It releases the energy later captured as ATP","It converts glucose into fructose"],"correct":1,"why":"Phosphate groups carry negative charge, and charged molecules do not slide back through a lipid membrane or ride the glucose transporter. Phosphorylating glucose therefore locks it inside the cell and commits it to being used. The same trick shows up all over metabolism: add a phosphate, trap the molecule.","traps":["Oxygen never receives a phosphate. It receives electrons and protons and leaves as water.",null,"Glucose-6-phosphate is not what crosses into the mitochondrion; pyruvate is, and it uses its own transporter.","That step costs energy rather than releasing it, and the payoff does not arrive until later in the pathway.","An isomerase does that conversion, and it happens in a separate step after the phosphate is already on."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d002","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Fungi share a distinctive set of structural and nutritional features. Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of fungi?","opts":["Hyphae that grow into a mycelium","Heterotrophic absorbers","Cellulose walls","Chitin walls","Glycogen storage"],"correct":2,"why":"This question is really asking you to separate the fungal wall from the plant wall. Fungi work like animals on the inside, storing carbohydrate as glycogen and feeding by absorption, but they wall themselves in with chitin, the same material in an insect exoskeleton. Cellulose is the plant wall polymer, so the choice naming cellulose walls is the one that does not belong. Hold on to fungus equals chitin, plant equals cellulose, and this whole family of questions falls out.","traps":["A mycelium of branching hyphae is the standard fungal body plan, so this is a fungal feature, not the exception.","Fungi secrete enzymes onto their food and absorb the digested product, so absorptive heterotrophy is a fungal feature.",null,"Chitin is exactly what a fungal cell wall is built from, so this belongs on the fungal list rather than being the exception.","Fungi store carbohydrate as glycogen, the same polymer animals use, so this is a fungal feature."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev017","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Bone and cartilage develop primarily from which germ layer?","opts":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest","The notochord"],"correct":1,"why":"Bone, cartilage, blood, muscle and the kidneys are all middle layer structures, so mesoderm is the default answer for connective and supporting tissue. The vertebrae and ribs come from the sclerotome portion of the somites, and the limb skeleton comes from lateral plate mesoderm. The one nuance worth carrying is that neural crest, an ectodermal population, supplies a large part of the facial skeleton, which is why the face often gets carved out as an exception.","traps":["Ectoderm makes the outer covering and the nervous system, while connective and supporting tissues such as bone, cartilage and blood are middle layer products.",null,"Endoderm lines the gut tube and buds off its glands, and it contributes no structural connective tissue.","Neural crest does build much of the face and parts of the skull, which makes this a fair thought, but the skeleton as a whole, the limbs, ribs and vertebrae included, comes from mesoderm.","The notochord is a midline rod that helps pattern the vertebrae without becoming them, and its only adult remnant is the nucleus pulposus inside each intervertebral disc."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-fiwnsa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Epinephrine binds a receptor on a liver cell and the cell starts breaking down glycogen. After the receptor changes shape, put the middle of the pathway in order:","opts":["A G protein binds GTP, cAMP rises, adenylyl cyclase is activated, and protein kinase A is activated","Protein kinase A is activated, cAMP rises, a G protein binds GTP, adenylyl cyclase is activated","cAMP rises, a G protein binds GTP, protein kinase A is activated, adenylyl cyclase is activated","Adenylyl cyclase is activated, a G protein binds GTP, cAMP rises, protein kinase A is activated","A G protein binds GTP, adenylyl cyclase is activated, cAMP rises, protein kinase A is activated"],"correct":4,"why":"Order is the entire question, so walk the relay one handoff at a time. The activated receptor loads the G protein with GTP, the G protein switches on adenylyl cyclase, that enzyme makes cAMP from ATP, and cAMP frees protein kinase A to phosphorylate its targets. Receptor, G protein, enzyme, second messenger, kinase is the shape of every version of this pathway.","traps":["cAMP is made by adenylyl cyclase, so the enzyme has to be active before the messenger rises.","The kinase is the last step rather than the first, since cAMP is what releases it.","cAMP is a product of this pathway, so it cannot appear before the enzyme that makes it.","Adenylyl cyclase cannot switch on before the G protein that activates it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-4tojjq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A leaf running linear flow starts building up NADPH faster than the Calvin cycle can spend it. Why does routing some electrons into cyclic flow help?","opts":["The Calvin cycle needs more ATP than NADPH, so extra ATP with no extra NADPH balances the books","It raises oxygen output, which the leaf needs for its own respiration at night","It lets the leaf fix carbon in the dark using electrons stored during the day","It converts the surplus NADPH back to NADP+ by handing its electrons straight to carbon dioxide in the stroma","It rebuilds water molecules from the protons in the lumen, so the leaf loses less water"],"correct":0,"why":"This is a stoichiometry question wearing a physiology costume. Three turns of the Calvin cycle burn nine ATP but only six NADPH, so a plant running linear flow alone slowly falls short on ATP. Cyclic flow is the top up valve: pure ATP, no NADPH, called for whenever that ratio drifts.","traps":[null,"Cyclic flow bypasses photosystem II completely, so it produces no oxygen at all.","Cyclic flow still needs light striking photosystem I, so it is not a pathway for darkness.","Carbon dioxide is reduced by Calvin cycle enzymes in the stroma, not by a photosystem handing electrons straight to it.","Nothing in the light reactions puts water back together, and the protons in the lumen are spent driving ATP synthase."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p079","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Natural killer (NK) cells patrol the body and destroy virus infected and tumor cells. They are best classified as part of which of the following?","opts":["Innate immunity","Adaptive immunity","B cell line","T cell line","The myeloid cell line"],"correct":0,"why":"Sort immune cells with one question: does this cell have to learn its target first. Natural killer cells do not; they watch for cells that have lost their class I major histocompatibility complex display, which is exactly what many viruses and tumors do to hide from cytotoxic T cells, and they kill on contact with perforin and granzymes. That missing self trigger, plus the absence of memory, places them in innate immunity even though they develop along the lymphoid line.","traps":[null,"Adaptive cells need prior exposure and build memory, while natural killer cells attack on first contact using general danger signals.","B cells make antibodies and carry antigen specific receptors, while natural killer cells kill without any antigen specific receptor at all.","Cytotoxic T cells also kill infected cells, but they need antigen displayed on class I major histocompatibility complex, which is nearly the opposite of what triggers a natural killer cell.","Natural killer cells develop along the lymphoid line with B and T cells, even though they behave like an innate first responder."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p036","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Mature red blood cells (erythrocytes) circulating in a mammal are best described as which of the following?","opts":["Multinucleate","Able to divide to produce new red blood cells","Anucleate","Have many mitochondria","Square-shaped"],"correct":2,"why":"This is really asking what a mammalian red blood cell gives up to become a better oxygen carrier. During maturation it ejects its nucleus and loses its mitochondria, leaving a flexible biconcave bag of hemoglobin with the maximum room for cargo. When a question describes a cell built for one job at the cost of everything else, the choice saying it has no nucleus is the one that fits.","traps":["Multiple nuclei are a skeletal muscle fiber feature; a red blood cell pushes out the one nucleus it had rather than gaining extras.","With no nucleus there is no DNA to copy, so these cells cannot divide; replacements come from stem cells in the bone marrow.",null,"Red blood cells drop their mitochondria along with the nucleus and run on anaerobic glycolysis, which keeps them from burning the oxygen they are carrying.","The mature cell is a biconcave disc, a shape that adds surface area and lets it fold through narrow capillaries."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#yt5pvw","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 48 g of CH₄? (molar mass 16 g/mol)","opts":["2.89 × 10²⁵","4.52 × 10²³","1.81 × 10²⁴","7.22 × 10²⁴","12"],"correct":3,"why":"48 g / 16 g/mol = 3 mol CH₄. Times 4 hydrogen per molecule = 12 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 7.22 × 10²⁴.","traps":["You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each CH₄ holds 4 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 4.",null,"That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:hb-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"How many σ bonds in glycine (NH₂CH₂COOH)?","opts":["6","7","8","9","10"],"correct":3,"why":"Count σ: 2 N-H, 1 N-C, 2 C-H, 1 C-C, 1 C=O (the σ part), 1 C-O, 1 O-H = 9 σ. The C=O double bond has 1σ + 1π - only the σ counts here.","traps":["Six counts only the bonds that touch a carbon; the two N-H bonds and the O-H are sigma bonds too.","Seven misses two sigma bonds, so go atom by atom and include both N-H bonds and the O-H.","Eight misses one sigma bond, most often the O-H on the carboxyl group.",null,"Ten includes the pi bond of the C=O; a double bond is one sigma plus one pi, and only the sigma counts here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A 0.10 mol methane sample combusts in a bomb calorimeter (C_cal = 5.0 kJ/°C). T rises 18°C. ΔH per mol?","opts":["−900 kJ/mol","−90 kJ/mol","−450 kJ/mol","−1800 kJ/mol","−180 kJ/mol"],"correct":0,"why":"q = C_cal × ΔT = 5.0 × 18 = 90 kJ released. Per mol: 90/0.10 = 900 kJ. ΔH = −900 kJ/mol (matches the actual ~890 kJ/mol for CH₄ combustion).","traps":[null,"That is the total heat released in the bomb, stopping before it is spread over the 0.10 mol.","That uses half the heat, or 0.20 mol; the calorimeter took in 90 kJ and 0.10 mol reacted.","That divides by 0.050 mol, half the sample; 0.10 mol burned.","That divides by 0.50 mol instead of 0.10 mol; only a tenth of a mole burned."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Phosphorus trichloride (PCl₃) has a trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry similar to NH₃, with P-Cl bonds at angles near 100°. Phosphorus (group 15) contributes 5 valence electrons, and three Cl atoms each form one bond. After drawing the structure, how many lone pairs reside on the central P?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":1,"why":"P has 5 valence e⁻; using 3 of them in three P-Cl σ bonds leaves 2 electrons (one lone pair) on phosphorus. Steric number = 3 bonds + 1 lone pair = 4 (sp³, tetrahedral electron geometry, trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_m8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A 0.500 mol sample of liquid water at 25°C contains both H and O atoms. Each H₂O molecule has 3 atoms (2 H + 1 O). Using Nₐ = 6.02×10²³ molecules/mol, how many TOTAL atoms (H plus O) are in the sample?","opts":["9.03 × 10²³ atoms","3.01 × 10²³ atoms","1.5 × 10²³ atoms","6.02 × 10²³ atoms","1.8 × 10²⁴ atoms"],"correct":0,"why":"Molecules = 0.500 × 6.02×10²³ = 3.01×10²³. Each molecule has 3 atoms, so total atoms = 3 × 3.01×10²³ = 9.03×10²³. Forgetting the ×3 for atoms-per-molecule gives 3.01×10²³ (just molecules).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A real 0.10 m MgSO₄ solution shows ΔTf = 0.24 °C in water (Kf = 1.86). The \"ideal\" ΔTf for MgSO₄ assuming complete dissociation would be 0.372 °C. The effective van't Hoff factor i_eff is:","opts":["1.00","1.29","1.55","1.86","2.00"],"correct":1,"why":"i_eff = ΔTf_measured / (Kf · m) = 0.24 / (1.86 × 0.10) = 0.24 / 0.186 = 1.29. MgSO₄ is famously underdissociated - extensive ion pairing of Mg²⁺ with SO₄²⁻ in water means effective i is much less than the predicted 2.","traps":["i = 1 would mean MgSO₄ does not dissociate at all - that is wrong, it does dissociate, just incompletely.",null,"That is the ratio taken upside down, ideal over measured; i_eff is the measured depression divided by Kf times m.","1.86 is Kf, not i. You confused two different constants in the formula.","2.00 is the IDEAL i (full dissociation). The question asks for the actual measured i_eff, which is reduced by ion pairing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:lc6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"For 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH < 0. To MAXIMIZE SO₃ yield, you should:","opts":["↓T, ↓P","Add argon at constant volume","↑T, ↑P","↑T, ↓P","↓T, ↑P"],"correct":4,"why":"Exo (heat = product), so low T pushes forward. 3 mol gas → 2 mol gas, so high P pushes forward (toward fewer mol). Industrial constraint: too low T = too slow, so compromise temperature is used.","traps":["Low temperature is the right half, but dropping the pressure lets the system drift toward the three-mole side.","Argon at fixed volume raises the total pressure while leaving every partial pressure alone, so the yield does not move.","High pressure is the right half, but heating an exothermic reaction pushes it back toward SO₂ and O₂.","Both halves work against the yield: heating reverses an exothermic reaction and low pressure favors the three-mole side.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:gb-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"ΔH = −120 kJ, ΔS = −150 J/K. Spontaneous BELOW what T?","opts":["80 K","Always spontaneous","800 K","120 K","Never spontaneous at any T"],"correct":2,"why":"(−,−) quadrant: spont when T < ΔH/ΔS. T_cross = (−120,000)/(−150) = 800 K.","traps":["80 K comes from a units slip, and 120,000 J divided by 150 J/K gives 800 K.","The pair with negative ΔH and negative ΔS has a ceiling temperature, above which the entropy penalty turns ΔG positive.",null,"120 K is ΔH read off as a temperature, and the crossover needs ΔH divided by ΔS.","A negative ΔH can carry this on its own while T stays small, so there is a range below 800 K where it does run."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:x12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Combust 0.5 mol propane (C₃H₈), ΔH = −2220 kJ/mol. How much heat released?","opts":["25 kJ","2220 kJ","4440 kJ","555 kJ","1110 kJ"],"correct":4,"why":"q = 0.5 × 2220 = 1110 kJ. (Sign: |q| since asking heat released; ΔH is negative for exo combustion.)","traps":["Reading the 0.5 as grams rather than moles gives about 25 kJ; half a mole of propane is 22 g.","That is the heat from a full mole, and only half a mole burned here.","Doubling instead of halving gives 4440 kJ, which is the heat from 2 mol.","Halving twice gives 555 kJ; the 0.5 mol calls for one halving.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 1.00 L buffer of 0.10 M HA and 0.10 M A- (pKa 5.00) receives a 0.05 mol bolus of HCl, which converts 0.05 mol of A- into HA. New concentrations: [HA] = 0.15 M, [A-] = 0.05 M. Using Henderson-Hasselbalch, find the resulting pH.","opts":["4.00","4.52","4.74","5.00","5.48"],"correct":1,"why":"pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]) = 5.00 + log(0.05/0.15) = 5.00 + log(0.333) = 5.00 - 0.477 = 4.52. The buffer absorbed the proton and pH dropped only ~0.5 unit instead of crashing to ~1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#14xj1a3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH₂ and a molar mass of 84 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["C₆H₁₂","C₇H₁₄","C₁₂H₂₄","C₅H₁₀","CH₂"],"correct":0,"why":"Empirical mass = 14. 84 / 14 = 6, so multiply every subscript by 6: C₆H₁₂.","traps":[null,"Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number.","You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass.","Multiplier is one too low. Compute the empirical formula mass carefully, then divide.","That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:nu6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A radioactive sample of 80 g decays to 5 g in 24 days. What is its half-life?","opts":["6 days","4 days","12 days","3 days","8 days"],"correct":0,"why":"80 → 40 → 20 → 10 → 5 = 4 half-lives. 24 days / 4 = 6 days per half-life.","traps":[null,"A 4 day half-life gives six half-lives in 24 days, leaving about 1.25 g.","A 12 day half-life allows just two halvings, which would leave 20 g.","A 3 day half-life fits eight halvings into 24 days, which would take the sample far below 5 g.","An 8 day half-life gives only three half-lives, leaving 10 g rather than 5 g."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:bf-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"1.0 L of buffer: 0.10 M HA + 0.10 M A⁻, pKa 5.0. Add 0.05 mol HCl. New pH?","opts":["1.30","4.52","4.74","5.00","5.48"],"correct":1,"why":"HCl converts A⁻ → HA. New: HA = 0.15 mol, A⁻ = 0.05 mol. pH = 5.0 + log(0.05/0.15) = 5.0 + log(1/3) = 5.0 − 0.48 = 4.52.","traps":["That is the pH of 0.05 M strong acid on its own, ignoring the base form standing by to absorb it.",null,"That is the acetate pKa from other problems, and this buffer is built around a pKa of 5.0.","That is the starting pH, before the added acid tipped the ratio toward the acid form.","That adds the log instead of subtracting it, sending the pH up when added acid has to send it down."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-gibbs#12ayr68","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction, ΔH = -120 kJ and ΔS = 200 J/K. What is ΔG at 400 K?","opts":["-120 kJ","-80 kJ","-80100 kJ","-200 kJ","-40 kJ"],"correct":3,"why":"TΔS = 400 × 200 J/K = 80 kJ. ΔG = -120 − (80) = -200 kJ, so the reaction is spontaneous at this temperature.","traps":["You forgot to multiply ΔS by the temperature.","You dropped ΔH. Both terms matter.","Unit trap. ΔS is in J/K but ΔH is in kJ. Divide TΔS by 1000 before subtracting.",null,"Sign slip. ΔG = ΔH MINUS TΔS."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#151b5a5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"What is the pH of a 1.0 × 10⁻² M solution of NaOH at 25 °C?","opts":["2","7","11","12","16"],"correct":3,"why":"Strong base: [OH⁻] = 10⁻² M, pOH = 2, pH = 14 − 2 = 12.","traps":["That is the pOH. NaOH gives you [OH⁻], so finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","You assumed neutral. A strong base is well above 7.","Exponent slip. For 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ the pOH is exactly n.",null,"You added instead of subtracting from 14. A pH that high is a signal to recheck."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:nc4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Approximate molar solubility of MX with Ksp = 4 × 10⁻⁹ (no calculator).","opts":["~2 × 10⁻⁴ M","~2 × 10⁻⁵ M","~4 × 10⁻⁹ M","~10⁻³ M","~6 × 10⁻⁵ M"],"correct":4,"why":"For a 1:1 salt, Ksp = s², so s = √Ksp. √(4 × 10⁻⁹) = 2 × √(10⁻⁹). Note √(10⁻⁹) ≈ √(10⁻¹⁰) × √10 ≈ 10⁻⁵ × 3.16 ≈ 3 × 10⁻⁵. So s ≈ 2 × 3 × 10⁻⁵ = 6 × 10⁻⁵ M. Estimate within a factor of 2 of the exact answer.","traps":["2 × 10⁻⁴ M would give Ksp = 4 × 10⁻⁸ - one order of magnitude too high.","2 × 10⁻⁵ M comes from rounding 10⁻⁴·⁵ down to 10⁻⁵, and that half power is closer to 3 × 10⁻⁵.","Ksp is NOT solubility. Ksp = s², so you must take the square root to get s.","10⁻³ M would mean Ksp = 10⁻⁶ - three orders of magnitude too high.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"The catalytic Ostwald oxidation of ammonia is 4 NH3 + 5 O2 -> 4 NO + 6 H2O(g). Standard enthalpies of formation (kJ/mol): NH3 = -46, NO = +90, H2O(g) = -242, O2 = 0. Use ΔH_rxn = sum(n·ΔH_f,products) - sum(n·ΔH_f,reactants) to compute ΔH_rxn for one mole of equation.","opts":["-184 kJ","+908 kJ","-724 kJ","-908 kJ","-1092 kJ"],"correct":3,"why":"Products: 4(+90) + 6(-242) = 360 - 1452 = -1092. Reactants: 4(-46) + 5(0) = -184. ΔH = -1092 - (-184) = -908 kJ. The reaction is highly exothermic.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#pni2cb","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.139 h⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["4.99 h","0.0963 h","7.19 h","0.201 h","14.4 h"],"correct":0,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.139 = 4.99 h. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":[null,"You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath.","That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.","You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Neutral iron (Fe, Z = 26) has a ground-state electron configuration of [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. The six 3d electrons distribute across five degenerate 3d orbitals according to Hund's rule (maximize parallel spins before pairing). How many unpaired electrons does Fe have?","opts":["0","2","4","5","6"],"correct":2,"why":"Distribute 6 d-electrons in 5 orbitals: ↑↓ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑. After all five are singly occupied (5 unpaired), the 6th electron pairs with one of them, leaving 4 unpaired electrons. The 4s² electrons are all paired and contribute 0 unpaired.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A Zn/Cu galvanic cell (E°_cell = +1.10 V, n = 2) operates at 25°C with [Cu²⁺] = 0.010 M and [Zn²⁺] = 1.00 M. Q = [Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺] = 100. Use the Nernst equation E = E° − (0.0592/n) log Q. What is the cell potential E?","opts":["1.22 V","1.04 V","1.16 V","0.95 V","1.10 V"],"correct":1,"why":"E = 1.10 − (0.0592/2)·log(100) = 1.10 − (0.0296)(2) = 1.10 − 0.0592 ≈ 1.04 V. Lower [Cu²⁺] reduces the driving force, so E < E°. Using n = 1 instead of 2 gives 0.98; flipping Q's numerator/denominator gives 1.16.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Mix 25 mL 0.20 M HCl + 50 mL 0.10 M NaOH. Final pH?","opts":["0.7","1","7","11","13"],"correct":2,"why":"Count moles, not molarity: 0.025 L times 0.20 M gives 0.0050 mol of HCl, and 0.050 L times 0.10 M gives 0.0050 mol of NaOH. Equal moles of a strong acid and a strong base cancel completely, so nothing is left but NaCl, and neither of its ions tugs on water. That is why the answer is neutral, and it is also why a smaller volume of a stronger acid can still be an exact match for a larger volume of a weaker one.","traps":["0.7 is the pH of the 0.20 M HCl on its own, before the base arrived and before the volume tripled.","A pH near 1 assumes the acid survives the mixing, and every mole of HCl here meets a mole of NaOH.",null,"A pH of 11 is mildly basic, which would take a small excess of NaOH, and the two are matched mole for mole.","A pH of 13 assumes leftover hydroxide, and the base does not outnumber the acid in this flask."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"A concentration cell at 25°C uses Cu electrodes in two Cu^2+ solutions: [Cu^2+] = 0.10 M (left) and 1.0 M (right). Both half-cells share E° = 0 (same reaction) so E_cell is determined entirely by the concentration difference via E = -(0.0592/n) log([dilute]/[concentrated]) with n = 2. Find E_cell.","opts":["0.059 V","0.118 V","0 V","0.030 V","0.25 V"],"correct":3,"why":"E_cell = (0.0592/2) log(1.0/0.10) = 0.0296 x 1 = 0.0296 V ~ 0.030 V. Concentration cells extract free energy from a concentration gradient until the two cells equalize.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sw-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Four 0.10 M aqueous acid solutions at 25 °C are tested: HCl (strong), HF (Ka = 6.6x10^(-4)), HCN (Ka = 4.9x10^(-10)), and CH3COOH (Ka = 1.8x10^(-5)). Considering that pH depends on the extent of dissociation, which solution has the LOWEST pH?","opts":["CH3COOH","HF","All equal","HCN","HCl"],"correct":4,"why":"HCl is the only strong acid here; it fully dissociates to give [H+] = 0.10 M and pH = 1.0. The weak acids dissociate only partially, giving higher pH (HF ~ 2.1, CH3COOH ~ 3.4, HCN ~ 5.2).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A student dissolves 5.85 g NaCl (MW = 58.5) in 100. g water and measures ΔTf = 3.5 °C. The expected ΔTf assuming complete dissociation (i = 2) is 3.72 °C. The most likely explanation:","opts":["ion pairing in solution makes effective i slightly less than 2","NaCl decomposed during dissolution","water has Kf < 1.86 in concentrated solutions","the experiment was run above the boiling point","some of the NaCl never dissolved, so the true molality was lower"],"correct":0,"why":"Real ionic solutions show ion pairing - Na⁺ and Cl⁻ briefly associate, behaving as one particle. So effective i drops from 2 to ~1.88. This is exactly why measured ΔTf is slightly LESS than the i = 2 prediction.","traps":[null,"NaCl is stable in water - it does not decompose. The discrepancy is a solution-phase effect, not a chemical reaction.","Kf is a property of the SOLVENT (water), not the solute concentration. Kf is constant at 1.86.","Freezing-point measurement is done near 0 °C, well below boiling. Temperature is not the issue.","NaCl dissolves to about 36 g per 100 g of water, so 5.85 g goes fully into solution; the shortfall comes from ion pairing instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ar-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"An Arrhenius experiment varies the temperature of a first-order reaction from 300 K to 350 K while holding the activation energy constant at E_a = 75 kJ/mol. According to k = A·exp(−E_a/RT), how does the rate constant k change as T increases?","opts":["k oscillates with T","k inversely proportional to T","k increases with T","k decreases with T","k stays the same regardless of T"],"correct":2,"why":"As T increases, −E_a/(RT) becomes less negative, so exp(−E_a/RT) increases. Therefore k increases with temperature for any positive E_a.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_p-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-4","topic":"One cause, every trend","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Four main-group elements are compared for atomic radius using x-ray covalent radii: Si (118 pm), P (110 pm), Ge (122 pm), Sn (140 pm). Atomic radius increases down a group (more shells) and decreases across a period (higher Z_eff). Which element is the LARGEST?","opts":["P","Si","All approximately equal","Ge","Sn"],"correct":4,"why":"Sn lies lowest in group 14, so it has the most occupied principal shells (5p valence) and the largest radius. Going down trumps going across: Sn > Ge > Si and Si > P (same row).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"For the gas-phase equilibrium 2 NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g) at 298 K, the equilibrium constant in terms of concentrations is K_c = 4.7 M⁻¹. Using K_p = K_c·(RT)^Δn with R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K and Δn = (1 − 2) = -1, calculate K_p at 298 K.","opts":["0.0085","0.19","4.7","41","115"],"correct":1,"why":"Δn = mol gas products - mol gas reactants = 1 - 2 = -1. K_p = K_c·(RT)^Δn = 4.7 × (0.0821 × 298)^(-1) = 4.7 / 24.5 ≈ 0.19. Negative Δn means K_p < K_c whenever RT > 1, as it always is at room temperature.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#17dpkw9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 2 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 227 °C and 1 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["123 L","37.3 L","82.1 L","8310 L","41.1 L"],"correct":2,"why":"T = 227 + 273 = 500 K. V = nRT / P = (2)(0.0821)(500) / 1 = 82.1 L.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.",null,"You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A student dissolves 0.50 mol of solid Mg(NO3)2 in enough water to produce 250. mL of solution in a volumetric flask at 25 °C. Considering only the formal molarity of the salt (not the ion concentrations), what is the molarity of Mg(NO3)2 in the resulting solution?","opts":["5.0 M","0.50 M","2.0 M","1.0 M","4.0 M"],"correct":2,"why":"M = mol / V(L) = 0.50 mol / 0.250 L = 2.0 M. Doubling for 'two nitrates' (giving 4.0 M) is a common conceptual error since the question asks about the salt, not the ions.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#yj4iy5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 1 m aqueous solution of glucose? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-0.512 °C","-2.79 °C","1.86 °C","-3.72 °C","-1.86 °C"],"correct":4,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (1)(1.86)(1) = 1.86 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 1.86 = -1.86 °C.","traps":["You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C.","glucose is a molecular solute. It does not split into ions, so i = 1.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1jzd8nw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Menthyl chloride (shown) undergoes E2 with sodium ethoxide. The leaving Cl is equatorial in the stable chair, so the ring must flip to an all-axial chair before elimination. Which alkene is the MAJOR (essentially only) product?","opts":["CC1CC=C(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","C=C1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1C=CC(C(C)C)CC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Verified: classic menthyl chloride E2. With Cl axial only the C6 H is anti-periplanar (the isopropyl-bearing C2 H is equatorial), so the less-substituted 2-menthene (option A, double bond between unsubstituted ring carbons) is the sole product, not the Zaitsev menthene at the iPr carbon (option B).","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CC[C@@H](C(C)C)[C@@H](Cl)C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-16pm9l6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene (shown) is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Markovnikov addition gives a secondary carbocation that rearranges before bromide adds. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)C(Br)C","CC(C)CCBr","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation puts + on C2 giving secondary (CH3)2CH-CH(+)-CH3; a 1,2-hydride shift gives the tertiary (CH3)2C(+)-CH2CH3, trapped by bromide to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (CCC(C)(C)Br). The unrearranged Markovnikov product is 2-bromo-3-methylbutane; the 1-bromo isomers are anti-Markovnikov/wrong regiochemistry.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)C=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-349b912165","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Phenol is far more acidic than cyclohexanol. What is the main reason?","opts":["Cyclohexanol cannot form hydrogen bonds","The ring carbons of phenol are more electronegative than oxygen, so they pull the proton off the hydroxyl group","The O-H bond in phenol is longer","Phenol has a higher molar mass, and larger molecules ionize more completely in aqueous solution","The negative charge of the phenoxide ion is delocalized into the ring by resonance"],"correct":4,"why":"Always look at the conjugate base. In phenoxide the lone pair on oxygen sits next to a pi system, so the negative charge is shared with the ring carbons. In cyclohexanol the charge is stuck on one oxygen. A more stable conjugate base means a stronger acid.","traps":["Both compounds form hydrogen bonds. That does not explain the difference in acidity.","Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon. The key is that the charge can spread out over the ring.","The decisive factor is the stability of the conjugate base, not the bond length.","Molar mass has nothing to do with acidity.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-tgbsqx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"Which set of reagents/conditions would best convert the tertiary alkyl bromide shown into the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene as the major product?","opts":["Sodium iodide (NaI) in acetone","Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol, heat (a small, strong, unhindered base)","Potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) in tert-butanol, heat","Dilute aqueous NaOH at room temperature","Concentrated H2SO4, heat (E1 conditions that favor the Zaitsev alkene)"],"correct":2,"why":"Bulky KOtBu gives the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene; NaOEt and H2SO4/E1 favor Zaitsev, aqueous NaOH favors substitution, NaI/acetone is SN2 Finkelstein.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCC(C)(C)Br","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1vndb83","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Two carbonyls are available for reduction by NaBH4 in methanol: acetophenone (an aryl methyl ketone, drawn) and benzaldehyde. Considering electronic and steric effects on hydride addition, which statement about relative rate is correct?","opts":["Neither reacts because NaBH4 cannot reduce conjugated aryl carbonyls","Benzaldehyde is reduced faster than acetophenone because the aldehyde carbonyl is less hindered and more electrophilic","Both are reduced at identical rates because NaBH4 reacts only with esters","Acetophenone is reduced faster because the extra methyl group donates electron density that makes the carbonyl a better hydride acceptor","Acetophenone is reduced faster because ketones are inherently more electrophilic than aldehydes at the carbonyl carbon in every solvent"],"correct":1,"why":"Aldehydes are reduced faster than ketones by hydride: the aldehyde carbonyl carbon is less sterically hindered and more electrophilic (only one alkyl/aryl group donating electron density), so benzaldehyde reacts faster than the more hindered, more electron-rich acetophenone.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1bog4vm","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with sodium ethoxide (E2). Considering the anti-periplanar requirement, which is the major alkene?","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCC=CC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1"],"correct":4,"why":"Classic anti-periplanar override. In the trans isomer, when C-Br is axial the C2 methyl is also axial, so the only beta-H is equatorial (not anti-periplanar) and cannot eliminate; the tempting Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene cannot form. The only anti-periplanar (axial) beta-H is on C6, giving the less-substituted 3-methylcyclohexene (option A).","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1nni7r4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (shown) is converted to a product whose IR shows a strong carbonyl absorption near 1715 cm-1 and whose 1H NMR shows a 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm but NO aldehydic proton near 9-10 ppm. Which reagents produce this compound?","opts":["H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","1. O3, 2. (CH3)2S","H2, Lindlar catalyst","1. Disiamylborane, 2. H2O2/NaOH","1. BH3, 2. H2O2/NaOH"],"correct":0,"why":"A 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm plus a carbonyl band and no aldehyde proton identifies a methyl ketone (CH3-C=O), i.e. 1-cyclohexylethanone, which is the Markovnikov hydration product made with Hg2+/H3O+. Hydroboration (options 2,3) would give an aldehyde with a 9-10 ppm CHO; ozonolysis cleaves; Lindlar only gives an alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CC1CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-yuv7z3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Styrene is shown below. Without any spectra, how many degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) does its molecular formula C8H8 correspond to?","opts":["3","4","5","6","8"],"correct":2,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (16 + 2 - 8)/2 = 5. This accounts for the benzene ring (3 pi bonds + 1 ring = 4) plus the vinyl C=C (1), totaling 5.","traps":null,"qs":"C=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-2sknr7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"Cyclopentanone is treated with KCN and aqueous HCl. Predict the major product.","opts":["N#CC1(N)CCCC1","OC1CCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCC1","N#CC1CCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the ketone followed by protonation gives the cyclopentanone cyanohydrin (a quaternary carbon bearing both OH and CN). The nitrile without OH, the aminonitrile, the simple alcohol (reduction), and the six-membered ring (wrong skeleton) are incorrect.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1boltky","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"What is the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (1. BH3; 2. H2O2, NaOH)?","opts":["CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(C)(C)C(O)C","CC(C)C(O)CC","CC(C)(C)C=CO","CCC(C)(C)CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration is concerted/anti-Markovnikov with NO carbocation rearrangement, so the neopentyl skeleton is preserved and OH goes to the terminal carbon -> 3,3-dimethyl-1-butanol (primary). Keyed option A correct. option B = Markovnikov alcohol, option C = rearranged skeleton (the acid-hydration lure), option D = enol (retains unsaturation), option E wrong isomer.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)C=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17mk9ys","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then with dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","O=CCCCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C, replacing each carbon with a carbonyl and opening the ring to a chain; reductive Me2S workup gives carbonyls (not acids). The methyl-bearing (disubstituted) carbon becomes a ketone, the H-bearing carbon becomes an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (option A, C7H12O2 verified). option B (heptane-2,6-dione, same formula) wrongly makes both ends ketones; option C wrongly makes both aldehydes (ignores the methyl); option D is the over-oxidized acid (oxidative workup); option E is a non-cleavage alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-anc6po","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"(E)-4-Phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone) is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)(C)/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)/C=C(C)/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Option B adds methyl at the alpha carbon, option C is the 1,2 allylic alcohol (organolithium behavior, alkene retained), option D shows extra carbonyl reduction, and option E retains the alkene (incorrect). Index 0 is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-0a1ca31176","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Which of these acids has the lowest pKa?","opts":["Butanoic acid","They are all equally strong","4-chlorobutanoic acid","2-chlorobutanoic acid","3-chlorobutanoic acid"],"correct":3,"why":"The inductive effect works through sigma bonds and drops off with every bond it has to cross. Put the chlorine on the carbon next to the carboxyl group and it stabilizes the carboxylate the most.","traps":["With no electron-withdrawing group this is the weakest of the four acids.","The inductive effect depends on distance, so position matters.","The inductive effect fades quickly with distance. This chlorine is too far away to help much.",null,"The chlorine is one carbon further from the carboxyl group, so its pull is weaker."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fquwbw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane (shown) is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["C[C@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](O)CC","C[C@H](Br)CC","CCC(C)C#N"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary substrate + strong CN- in polar aprotic DMSO = SN2 with backside inversion. 3D inversion of (R)-2-bromobutane gives canonical CC[C@H](C)C#N = the (S)-nitrile (option A). The descriptor flips R to S because CN outranks ethyl; option B is retention, option C is achiral/flat, option D is unreacted, option E is wrong nucleophile.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](Br)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1eq5a2f","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl 2-methyladipate (shown) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acid. Of the two possible Dieckmann ring closures, which beta-ketoester is the major product?","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC(C)C1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CC(C)CC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1(C)CCCC1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Dieckmann is reversible and is driven by deprotonation of the product's carbon flanked by both carbonyls. If the methyl-bearing alpha carbon attacks the far carbonyl, that carbon becomes quaternary (option B) with no acidic proton, so that path reverses. The favored product comes from the unsubstituted CH2 enolate attacking, leaving an acidic alpha proton on the ester-bearing ring carbon: option A (C9H14O3), verified to equal the path-B closure.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)C(C)CCCC(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8neusk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which structure is the major product? (Consider carbocation stability carefully.)","opts":["BrCC(C)C(C)C","BrCCC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)CBr","CC(C)(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2 next to the quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a more stable tertiary cation that Br- traps, yielding 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (keyed option A, canonical CC(C)C(C)(C)Br). The classic trap (option B) is the unrearranged secondary bromide.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-h6tsou","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Four candidate structures share the molecular formula but differ in functional group. An IR spectrum shows a strong band at 1740 cm-1 and NO absorption in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region (no O-H, no N-H). Of the molecule drawn, which statement about why it matches is correct?","opts":["It is a carboxylic acid, so the broad O-H is simply too weak to see","It is an ester: strong C=O ~1740 cm-1 and no O-H/N-H bands in the 2500-3500 region","It is a ketone, identified only by the lack of any 3300 cm-1 band","It is an amide, and its two N-H bands are hidden underneath the strong C=O absorption","It is an alcohol, and the 1740 cm-1 band is the C-O single bond stretch of its hydroxyl group"],"correct":1,"why":"Ethyl acetate (ester): strong C=O ~1735-1745 (higher than a ketone due to inductive pull of the ester O) and no O-H/N-H, so nothing broad in 2500-3500. The clean elevated carbonyl plus no broad region distinguishes ester from acid and amide.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)OCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1juifa8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"The molecule below is 2-bromo-3-chlorobutane drawn with both stereocenters defined. Assign the (C2, C3) configuration. (Number from the bromine-bearing carbon as C2.)","opts":["(2R, 3R)","(2R, 3R) - and the molecule is meso","(2S, 3S) - and the molecule is meso","(2R, 3S)","(2S, 3R)"],"correct":0,"why":"(2R,3R). It is not meso because the two ends carry different halogens (Br vs Cl), so no internal mirror plane. Keyed correct=0 is right; the meso distractors are good traps.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Cl)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-10b6fdu","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"Phenylacetylene (drawn) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (Sia2BH) then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","opts":["OC=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal CH carbon, and oxidation/tautomerization gives the aldehyde phenylacetaldehyde. Acetophenone is the Markovnikov product; 2-phenylethanol is alkene-hydration thinking; the enol is the intermediate; 1-phenylethanol has the OH on the wrong carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"C#Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-y8rsnv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanamide is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. The stereocenter is at the carbon alpha to the carbonyl. What is the major organic product, including correct configuration?","opts":["NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1C","OC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","O=C[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide to a primary amine (C=O becomes CH2, nitrogen retained), giving 2-phenylpropan-1-amine. Distractor 1 inverts the center (R), the alcohol comes from wrongly treating the amide like an acid, the aldehyde is an incorrect reduction, and the last option adds a ring methyl.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(N)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-5ly0uz","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n2-methyl-2-butene + H2, Pd/C","opts":["CC(C)(C)C","CCCCC","CCC(C)CO","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"H2/Pd reduces the trisubstituted alkene to 2-methylbutane (o0, CCC(C)C). No oxygen introduced; skeleton preserved (not rearranged to neopentane or n-pentane). Distractors are wrong-skeleton or spurious-OH.","traps":null,"qs":"CC=C(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-duu60b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"5-Aminopentanal (drawn) is treated with NaBH3CN under mildly acidic conditions, where the molecule undergoes INTRAMOLECULAR reductive amination. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["C1CCNC1","NCCCCCO","C1CCNCC1","C1CCCNCC1","CCCCC=N"],"correct":2,"why":"The -NH2 and aldehyde condense to a cyclic iminium reduced to a saturated cyclic amine. Distractors: pyrrolidine (5-membered) and azepane (7-membered) wrong ring sizes, the acyclic amino alcohol, and an acyclic imine that also lost a carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"O=CCCCCN","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-dp6bvf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Hexane-2,5-dione is treated with aqueous NaOH (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","opts":["O=C1CCCC1=O","CC1=CCC(=O)C1","CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1CCC(=O)C1=O","CC1=CC(=O)CC1C"],"correct":2,"why":"An enolate at a terminal methyl attacks the other carbonyl intramolecularly to close a favorable five-membered ring; dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one (C6H8O). Keyed structure correct. Distractors: cyclopentane-1,2-dione (no aldol), a regioisomeric enone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and an extra-methyl ring. All valid, unique.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1sm5f0m","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"3-Phenyl-1,5-hexadiene (drawn) is heated, allowing a Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement to reach the more stable isomer. Which structure is that product?","opts":["C=Cc1ccccc1CCC=C","C1=CC=CC(c2ccccc2)C1","C=CCC(C=C)c1ccccc1","C=CC(c1ccccc1)C=CC","C=CCCC=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The Cope [3,3] shift of the 1,5-diene breaks the C3-C4 sigma bond and forms a new C1-C6 bond, relocating the double bonds. The phenyl ends up conjugated with a newly formed alkene (styrene-type), giving 1-phenylhexa-1,5-diene (opt 0), which is thermodynamically favored. Option 1 is the unchanged starting material; the others have incorrect connectivity or form a ring.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(c1ccccc1)CC=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fdnh1u","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Toluene is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Which is the MAJOR product?","opts":["Cc1ccc(Br)cc1Br","Cc1ccccc1Br","BrCc1ccccc1","Cc1cccc(Br)c1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":4,"why":"CH3 is an activating o/p director; para dominates over ortho for steric reasons. Keyed option A = para-bromotoluene, correct. Distractors are ortho, meta (wrong director), benzyl bromide (side-chain radical, wrong site), and dibromide (over-bromination).","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1yn307z","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"3-Methylbutan-2-one is treated with one equivalent of Br2 in acetic acid (acid-catalyzed, monobromination). Which is the major product?","opts":["O=C(CBr)C(C)(C)Br","O=C(CBr)C(C)C","O=C(C)C(C)(C)Br","O=C(C(Br)Br)C(C)C","O=C(C)C(C)CBr"],"correct":2,"why":"Under ACID catalysis, halogenation goes through the more-substituted (more stable) enol. Terminal (C1) bromination dominates only under base; acid stops cleanly at monobromination since the added Br deactivates further enolization, ruling out di-bromo options. Two layered concepts (regio + mono vs di), within the allowed 2-concept cap.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(C)C(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-xtm96d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"You need to convert the internal alkyne shown into the corresponding cis (Z) alkene WITHOUT over-reducing it to the alkane. Which reagent should you choose?","opts":["Excess H2 with Raney nickel (a highly active sponge-nickel catalyst)","H2 with Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline)","Na metal in liquid NH3 (dissolving-metal reduction through a vinyl radical anion)","H2 with Pd/C at one atmosphere in ethanol","H2 with PtO2 (Adams' catalyst, a finely divided platinum oxide surface)"],"correct":1,"why":"Lindlar's poisoned Pd catalyst stops at the alkene and delivers both H syn, giving the cis (Z) alkene. Pd/C, PtO2, and Raney Ni over-reduce to the alkane; Na/NH3 dissolving-metal gives the trans alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#CC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8gdlsf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Benzene is treated with 1-chloropropane (CH3CH2CH2Cl) and AlCl3. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation goes through a carbocation; the primary propyl cation undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the secondary isopropyl cation, so cumene (isopropylbenzene, choice A) is major. B n-propyl (no-rearrangement trap), C tert-butyl and D ethyl (wrong carbon count), E isobutyl (wrong skeleton).","traps":null,"qs":"ClCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-rsmsr8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Tetracyanoethylene (TCNE, shown) is one of the fastest known dienophiles in Diels-Alder reactions. Compared with simple ethylene, why does TCNE react so much faster with a given diene?","opts":["The four cyano groups are electron-donating, which raises the dienophile HOMO so that it overlaps much better with the LUMO of the diene","The four cyano groups are strongly electron-withdrawing, lowering the dienophile LUMO so it overlaps better with the diene HOMO","TCNE is sterically smaller than ethylene, so it slides between the two diene termini and reaches the cyclic transition state sooner","TCNE is aromatic, so it reacts by electrophilic substitution instead","The cyano groups make the alkene more nucleophilic toward the diene"],"correct":1,"why":"The four EWG nitriles make TCNE extremely electron-poor, accelerating cycloaddition; index 0 is the only chemically correct statement (nitriles withdraw, not donate; TCNE is not aromatic; it is not more nucleophilic).","traps":null,"qs":"N#CC(C#N)=C(C#N)C#N","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1jrwrt","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"In the addition-elimination mechanism of nucleophilic acyl substitution on acetyl chloride (shown), consider the very FIRST mechanistic step. Which curved arrow correctly describes it?","opts":["The carbonyl oxygen's lone pair attacks the nucleophile","A lone pair on the nucleophile attacks the chlorine atom directly and displaces the acyl fragment as a carbanion","The C-Cl bond ionizes first to give a resonance-stabilized acylium cation, which the nucleophile then captures at carbon","A lone pair on the nucleophile attacks the carbonyl carbon while the C=O pi electrons move onto oxygen","A C-H bond of the methyl group breaks to form an enolate"],"correct":3,"why":"Addition-elimination begins with the nucleophile's lone pair attacking the electrophilic carbonyl carbon as the C=O pi electrons shift onto oxygen, forming the tetrahedral intermediate (option A). Acid chlorides do not pre-ionize to an acylium under normal nucleophilic conditions (option B), no enolate forms, and the nucleophile does not attack O or Cl. Prompt explicitly allows 'identify one arrow.' Substrate drawn and valid.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)Cl","os":false,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:est1211","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A bottling line fills 2,984 vials an hour. How many vials does it fill in 80 hours?","opts":["23872","238720","239000","240000","241000"],"correct":1,"why":"Work it out: 2984 × 80 = 238720. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 2984 times 8, so the zero in the 80 went missing.",null,"That is the product rounded to the nearest thousand, when the exact 238,720 is offered.","That is 3000 times 80, the rounded estimate rather than the exact product.","That is more than 2,000 above the product, so the rounding ran past even 3000 times 80."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1737","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A tank is meant to hold 6 liters, and its actual level of x liters is off by exactly 5 liters. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["11","11 or 1","5 or -5","-1 or 1","1 or -11"],"correct":1,"why":"|x − 6| = 5 means x − 6 = ±5, so x = 11 or 1.","traps":["You solved x - 6 = 5 and stopped, and the branch x - 6 = -5 gives 1 as well.",null,"You set x equal to plus or minus 5 and never undid the minus 6 inside the bars.","That pairs 1 with its negative, and the other branch is 6 plus 5, which is 11.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pa3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Two fair coins are flipped. What is the probability that at least one of them lands heads?","opts":["1/2","1/4","1/3","3/4","1"],"correct":3,"why":"OR: P(H1) + P(H2) − P(both). 1/2 + 1/2 − 1/4 = 3/4. Or flip-to-none: 1 − 1/4 = 3/4.","traps":["That is the chance for one coin on its own; two coins give four equally likely outcomes, three of which hold a head.","That is the chance that both land heads, not the chance of at least one.","Counted two heads, two tails and one of each as equally likely; one of each happens twice as often as either other.",null,"Treated at least one head as a sure thing; both coins land tails once in every four tries."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ps5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Rent on a storage unit is $200 a month and rises 25 percent of its current amount in each of the next two years. What is the monthly rent after both increases?","opts":["$250","$256","$275.00","$300","$312.50"],"correct":4,"why":"×1.25 × 1.25 = ×1.5625. 200 × 1.5625 = $312.50.","traps":["That is one 25 percent step, and the second rise still has to be applied.","Nothing in the two steps lands at 256: 1.25 times 1.25 is 1.5625.","You added 25 dollars on the second step instead of 25 percent, which on 250 is 62.50.","You added the two rises into a single 50 percent, and sequential percents multiply instead.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#1xmzrot","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² − 6x − 16 = 0?","opts":["6","16","10","−16","−6"],"correct":0,"why":"It factors as (x − 8)(x + 2), so the roots are 8 and −2. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 6, product = c/a = −16.","traps":[null,"You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum.","That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.","Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:fam5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A delivery route runs 2/3 of a mile, then 5/6 of a mile further, then doubles back 1/2 of a mile. How many miles is the driver from the start?","opts":["5/6","2","1","3/4","7/6"],"correct":2,"why":"LCD = 6. 4/6 + 5/6 − 3/6 = 6/6 = 1.","traps":["You rescaled 1/2 as 4/6 instead of 3/6, taking away one sixth too many.","You added all three instead of subtracting the last one, 4/6 plus 5/6 plus 3/6.",null,"You drifted into fourths, but 3, 6 and 2 all fit inside sixths.","You rescaled 1/2 as 2/6 instead of 3/6, leaving one sixth too much."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_pfl1517","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A quality check flips a fair coin for each of eight cartons. What is the probability that all eight flips land heads?","opts":["0.004","0.01","0.03","0.125","1"],"correct":0,"why":"A half to the eighth power is one over two hundred and fifty six, which is about 0.004. Every flip is independent, so the chances multiply rather than add. Watch for the answer that rounds the run away to zero: rare is not the same as impossible, and on this exam the difference is usually the point.","traps":[null,"0.01 is (1/2) to the seventh rounded off, one flip short of eight.","A probability never falls below 0, so this one can go before any arithmetic starts.","0.125 reads eight flips as a 1 in 8 chance, and each flip halves the run, so eight of them give 1/256.","1 would make the run certain, and that sits much closer to the chance of at least one head across eight flips."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:fm5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is the product of 2/3, 3/4 and 5/6?","opts":["10/72","15/48","30/12","1/2","5/12"],"correct":4,"why":"Multiply across: 30/72 = 5/12 reduced.","traps":["You left the 3 out of the top row; 2 times 3 times 5 is 30.","You multiplied 3/4 by 5/6 to get 15/24 and then divided by 2 instead of multiplying by 2/3.","That multiplies all three tops but only the first two bottoms. Every denominator has to go in.","You stopped after the first two fractions; the 5/6 still has to come in.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#1n90no","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 500 and standard deviation 100. About what percent of scores fall above 600?","opts":["16%","32%","34%","68%","84%"],"correct":0,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 16%.","traps":[null,"That is BOTH tails together. Only the upper tail is asked for: halve it.","That is the slice between the mean and one SD. The question asks for the TAIL beyond it.","That is the middle band within one SD. The tail is what is left over, split in two.","That is everything BELOW the cutoff. The question asks for above."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1blwuhr","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$10000 is invested at 10% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 3 years?","opts":["$3310.00","$12100.00","$13000.00","$13310.00","$14641.00"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 10000 × (1.1)³ = $13310.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1056","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A machine fills 2/8 of a crate in 6/9 of a minute. What fraction of a crate does it fill in one minute?","opts":["8/72","12/17","8/3","12/72","3/8"],"correct":4,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 2/8 times 9/6 gives 18/48, which reduces by 6 to 3/8. Because 6/9 is less than 1, dividing by it has to push 2/8 upward, and 3/8 is larger, so the size is right. Flip, multiply, reduce, in that order.","traps":["You added the tops (2 + 6) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (8 + 9).","That flips the first fraction instead of the second, which inverts the whole answer.","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1185","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An 80 gram sample loses 25% of its mass in a first drying step and 10% of what is left in a second. What is its mass in grams at the end?","opts":["52","54","60","66","72"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply factors: 80 × 0.75 × 0.9 = 54. Naïvely adding gives 52, which is wrong.","traps":["You added the two cuts into a single 35 percent, and sequential percents multiply instead.",null,"That is the price after the 25 percent cut, one step short of the answer.","That raises the second step by 10 percent instead of cutting by it.","That applies only the 10 percent cut and leaves the 25 percent out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1028","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A pump moves 4/7 of a tank in the first hour and 4/5 of a tank in the second. How many tanks were moved over the two hours?","opts":["8/12","8/35","48/35","16/35","11/7"],"correct":2,"why":"LCD = 35. 4/7 = 20/35, 4/5 = 28/35. Combine: 48/35 = 48/35.","traps":["You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 8 over 12.","You put both 4s over 35 without rescaling; 4/7 is 20/35 and 4/5 is 28/35.",null,"You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You rounded 4/5 up to a whole; four fifths is 28/35, so the total is 48/35."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1740","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What values of x satisfy |x − 3| = 7?","opts":["4 or -4","10","4 or -10","-4 or -10","10 or -4"],"correct":4,"why":"|x − 3| = 7 means x − 3 = ±7, so x = 10 or -4.","traps":["That pairs 4 with its negative, and the branches give 3 plus 7 and 3 minus 7, which are 10 and -4.","You solved x - 3 = 7 and stopped, and the branch x - 3 = -7 gives -4 as well.","You subtracted the 3 instead of adding it back on both branches.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-mixture#1sypefl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"4 L of a 20% acid solution is mixed with 1 L of a 80% acid solution. What is the concentration of the mixture?","opts":["32%","40%","50%","68%","160%"],"correct":0,"why":"Acid: 20% of 4 plus 80% of 1 = 1.6 L. Total volume = 5 L. Concentration = 32%.","traps":[null,"You divided by the product of the volumes. Divide by the SUM: total volume.","You averaged the two percents. The mixture leans toward whichever solution you used MORE of, so weight by volume.","You swapped the volumes. Pair each concentration with its own volume.","You found the total acid but never divided by the total volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"written:qr-11-rn4znq","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"If cos θ = -0.28 and θ lies in the third quadrant, what is sin θ?","opts":["-0.96","-0.9216","-0.72","0.72","0.96"],"correct":0,"why":"Square and subtract: sin²θ is 1 minus 0.0784, which is 0.9216, and the square root of that is 0.96. The third quadrant has a negative y coordinate, so the sine is negative. The pair 0.28 and 0.96 is the 7-24-25 triangle scaled down by 25, which is a handy tell that the arithmetic came out clean.","traps":[null,"This is sin²θ, so the square root step has not happened yet.","This subtracts 0.28 from 1 instead of subtracting its square.","This subtracts the raw value and drops the sign as well.","This is the right size but the wrong sign, since sine is negative in the third quadrant."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1372","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A family drives at 50 miles per hour for 5.25 hours. How many miles do they cover?","opts":["9.52","212.5","250","262.5","312.5"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 50 × 5.25 = 262.5 mi.","traps":["Dividing 50 by 5.25 instead of multiplying.","Using 4.25 hours instead of 5.25.","Dropping the quarter hour and using 5 hours flat.",null,"Using 6.25 hours instead of the 5.25 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#2zw6b2","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 4 red and 2 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["4/9","2/3","2/5","1/3","3/5"],"correct":2,"why":"First red: 4/6. Second red: 3/5. Multiply: 2/5.","traps":["That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.","That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.",null,"You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble.","That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1494","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A number is multiplied by 3, then 5 is subtracted, leaving 13. What is the number?","opts":["5","6","7","9","18"],"correct":1,"why":"'Five less than 3 times a number' is 3x minus 5, not 5 minus 3x, and it equals 13. Add the 5 back to get 3x = 18, then divide by 3 for 6. The phrase 'less than' flips the order of what you write, so translate it slowly.","traps":["That is the 5 lifted straight out of the sentence rather than the number solved for.",null,"Three 7s minus 5 lands at 16, so this one runs a step high; 3x has to hit exactly 18.","Split the 18 by 2 instead of by 3, so the number came out half again too big.","Stopped one step short at 3x = 18 without splitting that 18 three ways."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1698","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A line passes through (12,-7) and (13,-2). What is its slope?","opts":["-9","-5","0.2","5","6"],"correct":3,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = 5/1 = 5.","traps":["-9 comes from writing -2 minus 7 for the rise instead of -2 minus -7, over a run of 1.","-5 has the right size and the wrong sign, since y rises from -7 to -2.","0.2 divides the run by the rise, 1 over 5, which is the slope upside down.",null,"6 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1658","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Can a triangle have sides of lengths 8, 7, and 1?","opts":["Yes","Only if it is isosceles","No","Only if right triangle","Cannot determine"],"correct":2,"why":"|8−7| = 1 and 8+7 = 15. 1 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["7 and 1 add to exactly 8, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it.","Equal sides are not the condition: 7 plus 1 ties the 8 exactly, and the sum has to be greater.",null,"There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 8, tying it rather than beating it.","The three lengths settle it: 7 plus 1 equals 8, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#1gsyt7e","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $200 item is marked UP by 10% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 25%. What is the final price?","opts":["$150.00","$165.00","$170.00","$220.00","$247.50"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 200 × 1.1 × 0.75 = $165.00. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1600","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"How many real roots does x² − 4x + 1 = 0 have?","opts":["two real roots","one real and one imaginary root","no real roots","two imaginary roots","one real root"],"correct":0,"why":"Δ = b² − 4ac = 16 − 4·1·1 = 12. > 0, so two.","traps":[null,"Complex roots come in conjugate pairs, so one real and one imaginary is not possible.","A discriminant of 12 is positive, so both roots are real, at 2 + √3 and 2 − √3.","Imaginary roots require a negative discriminant, and this one is positive.","One root needs a discriminant of zero, and 16 − 4 is 12."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-combined-work#fuu075","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One printer finishes a print run in 5 hours. A second printer does the same job in 20 hours. Working together, how long do they take?","opts":["0.25 hours","12.5 hours","4 hours","15 hours","25 hours"],"correct":2,"why":"Rates add: 1/5 + 1/20 = 1/4 of the job per hour. Time = 4 = 4 hours. Sanity check: it must be less than 5 hours.","traps":["That is the combined RATE (jobs per hour). Flip it to get the time.","You averaged the times. Two working together must be FASTER than the faster one alone.",null,"You subtracted the times. Rates add when both are working.","You added the times. Adding helpers cannot make the job take longer. Add the RATES: 1/a + 1/b."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#ejj15","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 108 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["0.03 m/s","1.08 × 10⁵ m/s","389 m/s","1800 m/s","30 m/s"],"correct":4,"why":"108 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 30 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":["That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#1a1rbm5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 60 miles at 50 mph and returns the same 60 miles at 75 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["62.5 mph","68.8 mph","30 mph","60 mph","61.2 mph"],"correct":3,"why":"Time out = 60/50 = 1.2 h, time back = 60/75 = 0.8 h. Average = 120 / 2 = 60 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.","Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d.",null,"That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:int1439","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A deposit of $1000 earns 7.5% interest compounded annually for 5 years. To the nearest cent, what is the total amount in dollars after 5 years?","opts":["375","1000.38","1075","1375","1435.63"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 1000·(1.075)^5 = 1435.63.","traps":["That is the simple interest for five years, not the ending amount.","Added rt as about thirty-eight cents rather than as 37.5 percent of the 1000.","Grew the balance for one year instead of five.","Used simple interest, so the later years never earned interest on the earlier interest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1333","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"For which values of x is -5x < 3 true?","opts":["x > -0.6","x > 0.6","x < 0.6","x = -0.6","x < -0.6"],"correct":0,"why":"Divide both sides by -5 (negative) and FLIP: x > -0.6.","traps":[null,"You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 3/-5.","You divided by 5 rather than -5, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.","You solved the boundary equation -5x = 3 instead of the range of values.","You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:vol1676","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A steel ball has a radius of 4 millimeters. To the nearest hundredth, what is its volume in cubic millimeters?","opts":["50.27","67.02","201.06","268.08","804.25"],"correct":3,"why":"V = (4/3)πr³ = 268.08.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1452","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A greenhouse layout places one bench at (3, 7) and another at (9, 12) on a unit grid. What is the approximate straight-line distance between the benches in units?","opts":["3.32","6","7.81","11","61"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (5)²) = √61 = 7.81.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1502","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"The sum of a number and 2 times the number is 6. Find the number.","opts":["1","2","3","4","6"],"correct":1,"why":"A number plus 2 times the number is 3 of them, because the number itself counts as one. That gives 3x = 6 and x = 2. Count the pieces first, then divide.","traps":["One plus twice 1 comes to only 3, half of the 6 the stem asks for.",null,"Divided by 2 instead of 3; the number itself is one of the three pieces.","That is 2 times the number, the second piece of the sum, rather than the number.","That is the total from the stem, copied down before the 6 was split three ways."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lg5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the value of log(40) − log(4)?","opts":["1","log(160)","10","log(36)","2.66"],"correct":0,"why":"Quotient law: log(a) − log(b) = log(a/b). So log(40) − log(4) = log(10) = 1.","traps":[null,"You used the product law and multiplied 40 by 4 instead of dividing.","You divided 40 by 4 and stopped there, and the log of that quotient still has to be taken.","You subtracted the inputs instead of using the log law. log(a)−log(b) ≠ log(a−b).","That divides one log by the other. Subtracting logs divides the numbers inside them, it does not divide the logs themselves."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-missing-value-mean#1y7ihef","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A student scored 82, 88, 86 on 3 quizzes. What score is needed on the next quiz to bring the average to exactly 88?","opts":["8","88","91","96","100"],"correct":3,"why":"Needed total = 88 × 4 = 352. Current total = 256. Next score = 352 − 256 = 96.","traps":["You used 3 quizzes. After the next one there are 4.","Scoring the target only works if you were already AT the target. You have to make up the gap on every earlier quiz too.","You averaged the old average with the new score. The old average counts for several quizzes, not one.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Required total = target × number of quizzes; subtract what you already have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:psm2","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A textbook's price is raised 30 percent and then cut 10 percent from the raised price. What is the net percent change?","opts":["+20%","−40%","+40%","+43%","+17%"],"correct":4,"why":"×1.30 × 0.90 = ×1.17. Net +17%.","traps":["You added the 30 up and the 10 down, and sequential percents multiply instead.","You added the sizes of the two changes and pointed the result downward, and the rise is the bigger move here.","You added the 10 percent drop on as another rise instead of taking it off.","That raises the second step by 10 percent instead of cutting by it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1477","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A race course is 7.8 kilometers long. What is that distance in meters?","opts":["0.01","78","780","1007.8","7800"],"correct":4,"why":"1 km = 1000 m, so 7.8 km × 1000 = 7800 m.","traps":["You divided by 1000 instead of multiplying; the smaller unit always gives the bigger count.","You moved the decimal one place instead of three, multiplying by 10 rather than 1000.","You multiplied by 100 instead of 1000; kilometers to meters moves three places.","You added 1000 to the 7.8 instead of multiplying the 7.8 by it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:st4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"At a college, 25 students take biology and 20 take chemistry, and 35 students take at least one of the two. How many take both?","opts":["5","10","15","20","45"],"correct":1,"why":"Rearrange inclusion-exclusion: |A ∩ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∪ B| = 25 + 20 − 35 = 10.","traps":["You took the difference between the two set sizes instead of working back from the union.",null,"You subtracted |B| from the union, which gives the part of A outside the overlap.","That is |B| on its own, which would only be the overlap if all of B sat inside A.","That's the sum of |A| + |B|."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pan1538","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"events: P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.3, P(A and B) = 0.13. P(A or B)?","opts":["0.18","0.23","0.3","0.77","0.9"],"correct":3,"why":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) = 0.6 + 0.3 − 0.13 = 0.77.","traps":["0.18 multiplies 0.6 by 0.3, and the problem gives the real overlap as 0.13.","0.23 is the chance neither event happens, 1 minus 0.77, the complement of what is asked.","0.3 is the chance of B on its own, so the rest of the or rule never runs.",null,"0.9 adds the two probabilities and leaves the 0.13 overlap counted twice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1102","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"To the nearest tenth, what is 0.7 divided by 7.4?","opts":["0.01","0.1","0.2","0.95","10.6"],"correct":1,"why":"Move both decimal points one place and divide: 7 into 74, which is about 0.095. The nearest value on the list is 0.1. A size check helps first: 7.4 goes into 0.7 far less than once, so the answer has to be a small piece of 1.","traps":["You pushed the point two places too far left, making the answer a hundred times too small.",null,"You rounded 7.4 down to about 4 before dividing, which nearly doubles the size of the answer.","You moved the point one place too far right; 0.7 divided by 7.4 is about 0.095, not ten times that.","You divided 7.4 by 0.7 instead of the other way round."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1353","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A team of 22 volunteers is assigned to two sites in a ratio of 4 to 7. How many volunteers are at the smaller site?","opts":["2","4","8","11","14"],"correct":2,"why":"Total parts = 11; one part = 22/11 = 2. Smaller = 4·2 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1255","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies -3x - 2 = x + 10?","opts":["-6","-4","-3","-2","3"],"correct":2,"why":"Move x: (-3-1)x = 10+2 → -4x = 12, so x = -3.","traps":["The x terms were combined as -3 - 1 = -2 instead of -4, leaving -2x = 12.","Substituting -4 gives 10 on the left and 6 on the right.",null,"The -2 was subtracted from the 10 instead of added, giving -4x = 8.","Dividing 12 by -4 gives -3, so this answer drops the minus sign."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261004"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s12"}}]};
